(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e1b.882fd1edda.js"]={"8":[{"id":"sdl8_q01","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of developmental dysplasia of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement most accurately describes the epidemiology and recommended screening strategy for developmental dysplasia of the hip (DDH)?","choices":{"A":"DDH occurs in 3-5 per 1000 live births; mild hip instability is found in 40%; bilateral in 37%; left > right; screening recommended at birth and every wellness visit until walking","B":"DDH occurs in 1 per 10,000 live births; mild instability in 10%; bilateral in 75%; right > left; screening recommended only once at birth by specialized pediatric orthopedics","C":"DDH occurs in 10-15 per 1000 live births; bilateral in 60%; right > left; screening with ultrasound is recommended for all infants between 2 and 4 months regardless of exam findings","D":"DDH occurs in 1 per 1000 live births; mild hip instability is found in 5%; unilateral in 90% of cases; screening is recommended only when clinical risk factors are identified at birth","E":"DDH occurs in 3-5 per 1000 live births; bilateral in 70% of cases; right > left side; screening is deferred until 6 months of age because physical exam before that age is unreliable"},"correct":"A","explanation":"DDH occurs in 3-5 per 1000 live births; mild hip instability is found in 40% of neonates (most resolve spontaneously); bilateral in 37%; left > right (due to left occiput anterior fetal positioning in most deliveries). Screening is recommended at birth and at every wellness visit until walking. Sources differ on incidence: the course slide gives 3-5 per 1000, while the CDT Orthopedics reading (SDL 9 p3) gives about 1 in 1000 live births in whites. The incidence is not 1 per 10,000 or 10-15 per 1000. Bilateral in only 37%, not 70%. Right is not more common than left.","boardPrep":"DDH epidemiology: 3-5/1000 live births. Mild instability: 40% (most resolve). Bilateral: 37%. Left > right (likely from left occiput anterior fetal position). Risk factors: female sex, breech position, family history, first-born, oligohydramnios. Screen at birth and every wellness visit until walking.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 1 — DDH: 3-5/1000; Ortolani (reduce), Barlow (dislocate); ultrasound <4mo, X-ray >4mo; Pavlik harness for <6mo; screen at birth and every wellness visit"},{"id":"sdl8_q02","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of developmental dysplasia of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement correctly differentiates the Ortolani maneuver from the Barlow maneuver in DDH examination?","choices":{"A":"Ortolani dislocates an unstable hip by applying posterior force with the hip adducted; Barlow reduces a dislocated hip by abducting while applying anterior force on the femoral head","B":"Ortolani reduces a dislocated hip (a clunk indicates the femoral head slipping back into the acetabulum); Barlow dislocates an unstable hip (posteriorly displaceable during the test)","C":"Ortolani tests hip abductor muscle strength in infants over 6 months; Barlow tests hip flexor tightness in infants under 3 months, both requiring radiographic confirmation of findings","D":"Ortolani is a dynamic ultrasound measurement of the acetabular angle; Barlow is a static clinical test that evaluates femoral head position relative to the acetabulum at rest","E":"Ortolani is performed with the hip in extension and external rotation; Barlow is performed with the hip in flexion and adduction to detect posterior hip joint laxity in infants"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ortolani is the REDUCTION maneuver: with the hip flexed and abducted while lifting the femoral head anteriorly, a palpable clunk indicates the dislocated femoral head slipping BACK INTO the acetabulum. Barlow is the DISLOCATION maneuver: adduction while applying posterior force causes the unstable femoral head to slip OUT. The descriptions are not reversed. Both are performed with the hip flexed at 90 degrees. Ortolani does not involve ultrasound measurement or hip extension.","boardPrep":"DDH physical exam: Ortolani = reduction maneuver (hip flexed 90°, abduct while lifting femoral head anteriorly; positive = clunk of femoral head reducing into acetabulum). Barlow = dislocation maneuver (hip flexed 90°, adduct while applying posterior force; positive = femoral head dislocating out posteriorly). Also: Galeazzi test (functional femoral shortening with hips flexed), Klisic test (>3 months), Trendelenburg gait (ambulatory).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 1 — DDH: 3-5/1000; Ortolani (reduce), Barlow (dislocate); ultrasound <4mo, X-ray >4mo; Pavlik harness for <6mo; screen at birth and every wellness visit"},{"id":"sdl8_q03","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of developmental dysplasia of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which imaging modality is recommended for the initial workup of developmental dysplasia of the hip, and what determines which modality is chosen?","choices":{"A":"MRI of the hip is the gold standard imaging modality for all ages regardless of the infant's age because it provides superior visualization of cartilaginous and bony hip anatomy","B":"Plain radiographs are preferred at all ages because they provide the most accurate measurement of the acetabular index and femoral head ossification center position in infants","C":"CT scan of the pelvis with radiation shielding is the preferred initial imaging modality to measure the acetabular index and confirm femoral head position in all ages","D":"Ultrasound is preferred before 4 months of age (cartilaginous femoral head not visible on X-ray); radiographs are preferred after 4 months when ossification centers appear on imaging","E":"Ultrasound should only be used after 6 months of age once the femoral head begins to ossify; radiographs with the hip in abduction are preferred for the first 6 months of life"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ultrasound is preferred before 4 months of age because the femoral head is cartilaginous and cannot be seen on plain X-ray. After 4 months, when the femoral head ossification center appears, plain radiographs become more informative and are preferred. MRI requires sedation and is reserved for complex cases, not first-line imaging. CT scan involves radiation and is not first-line. Ultrasound should NOT be deferred until 6 months — the 4-month threshold is the standard transition point.","boardPrep":"DDH imaging: ultrasound preferred before 4 months (femoral head is cartilaginous and not yet ossified — not visible on plain X-ray). Radiographs after 4 months when ossification centers appear. Hip ultrasound evaluates alpha angle (acetabular coverage) and femoral head position dynamically. Plain X-ray evaluates Shenton line, acetabular index, center-edge angle in older infants.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 1 — DDH: 3-5/1000; Ortolani (reduce), Barlow (dislocate); ultrasound <4mo, X-ray >4mo; Pavlik harness for <6mo; screen at birth and every wellness visit"},{"id":"sdl8_q04","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteogenesis imperfecta.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which molecular defect is responsible for the majority of osteogenesis imperfecta cases and what is the inheritance pattern of classical OI?","choices":{"A":"Defects in the production, processing, or use of Type 1 collagen account for approximately 85% of OI cases; classical OI (Sillence Types I-IV) is autosomal dominant","B":"Defects in Type 3 collagen synthesis account for 85% of OI cases; OI is exclusively autosomal recessive and the recessive form tends to be milder than the dominant form","C":"Defects in osteoclast function causing excessive bone resorption account for 85% of OI cases; OI is autosomal dominant with complete penetrance in all affected families","D":"Defects in bone matrix mineralization related to alkaline phosphatase deficiency account for 85% of OI cases; all forms of OI are autosomal recessive with variable expressivity","E":"Defects in fibrillin-1 causing abnormal connective tissue cross-linking account for 85% of OI cases; inheritance is X-linked recessive with severe phenotype in affected males"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Defects in the production, processing, or use of Type 1 collagen account for approximately 85% of OI cases (COL1A1 and COL1A2 mutations). Classical OI (Sillence Types I-IV) is autosomal dominant. Type V (classic) or type III (vascular subtype) collagen defects cause Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, not OI. Osteoclast dysfunction underlies osteopetrosis. Alkaline phosphatase deficiency causes hypophosphatasia. Fibrillin-1 mutations cause Marfan syndrome. OI is not X-linked.","boardPrep":"OI molecular basis: Type 1 collagen defects in 85% (COL1A1, COL1A2 mutations). Classical OI = autosomal dominant. Autosomal recessive forms (collagen prolyl 3-hydroxylation complex: LEPRE1, CRTAP, PPIB) = more severe. Osteoblast function and bone matrix mineralization defects account for remaining cases. Type 1 collagen is the primary structural protein of bone, skin, and tendon.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — OI: Type 1 collagen defect (85%); classic triad = fragile bones + blue sclerae + early deafness; Sillence I-IV; bisphosphonates benefit trabecular bone most"},{"id":"sdl8_q05","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteogenesis imperfecta.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which Sillence type of osteogenesis imperfecta is associated with perinatal lethality and the most severe skeletal deformity?","choices":{"A":"Sillence Type I is the mildest form with blue sclerae, presenile hearing loss, and recurrent childhood fractures, but results in perinatal death in 30% of affected newborns","B":"Sillence Type IV is moderately severe with white sclerae, variable dentinogenesis imperfecta, and short stature, and causes perinatal death in 50% of affected newborns","C":"Sillence Type II is the perinatal lethal form, characterized by severe bone fragility with multiple fractures and deformities at birth that are incompatible with survival","D":"Sillence Type III is the progressive deforming form, always lethal in the perinatal period due to thoracic kyphoscoliosis and respiratory insufficiency from rib cage deformity","E":"Sillence Type V has calcifying interosseous membranes and hyperplastic callus formation after fractures, and is lethal in the perinatal period from cardiac involvement"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sillence Type II is perinatal lethal, with multiple fractures in utero, accordion rib deformities, and marked bone fragility incompatible with survival beyond the neonatal period. Type I is the mildest form — patients survive with recurrent childhood fractures and blue sclerae. Type III is the most severe non-lethal type — patients survive but with progressive deformity and often wheelchair dependence. Type IV is moderately severe with white sclerae. None of the other types are perinatal lethal as a rule.","boardPrep":"Sillence OI Classification: Type I (mild) — blue sclerae, recurrent fractures, presenile hearing loss (30-60%), least severe, normal stature. Type II (perinatal lethal) — multiple fractures in utero, accordion ribs, incompatible with life. Type III (progressive deforming) — most severe non-lethal; short stature; scleral hue varies; dentinogenesis imperfecta and hearing loss common. Type IV (moderate) — white sclerae, variable dentinogenesis imperfecta. Classic triad: fragile bones + blue sclerae + early deafness.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — OI: Type 1 collagen defect (85%); classic triad = fragile bones + blue sclerae + early deafness; Sillence I-IV; bisphosphonates benefit trabecular bone most"},{"id":"sdl8_q06","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteogenesis imperfecta.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which pharmacologic therapy is used in osteogenesis imperfecta and where in the skeleton does it exert the greatest benefit?","choices":{"A":"Teriparatide (PTH analog) is the primary bone-targeted therapy for OI and exerts its greatest benefit in cortical bone at long bone diaphyses rather than in trabecular bone","B":"Bisphosphonates (IV pamidronate) are used in OI treatment and are more beneficial for vertebrae (trabecular bone) than for cortical long bones in children with OI","C":"Denosumab is the preferred first-line agent for OI and exerts equal beneficial effects on cortical and trabecular bone, reducing fracture rates in all skeletal compartments equally","D":"High-dose calcium and vitamin D supplementation are the established first-line pharmacologic treatments for OI, exerting their greatest benefit at the cortical long bone level","E":"Romosozumab is the recommended bone-targeted therapy for OI and exerts its greatest benefit at cortical long bone diaphyses rather than at the vertebral trabecular bone level"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bisphosphonates (IV pamidronate or zoledronate) reduce fracture rates and improve vertebral morphology in OI, with greater benefit demonstrated for trabecular (vertebral) bone than for cortical long bone diaphyses. Prolonged use may paradoxically weaken long bone material properties and increase nonunion risk. Bisphosphonates do NOT correct the underlying collagen defect. They do not provide equal benefit to all skeletal compartments. They are not the only required therapy — multidisciplinary care is essential.","boardPrep":"OI management: multidisciplinary approach — PT, bone-targeted therapies, surgical intervention, dental care, hearing/growth/cardiac monitoring. Bisphosphonates (IV pamidronate or zoledronate): reduce fracture rate, improve vertebral shape (trabecular bone benefit), may weaken long bone material properties with prolonged use. Bisphosphonates more beneficial for vertebrae (trabecular) than cortical long bones. Bisphosphonates reduce scoliosis incidence.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — OI: Type 1 collagen defect (85%); classic triad = fragile bones + blue sclerae + early deafness; Sillence I-IV; bisphosphonates benefit trabecular bone most"},{"id":"sdl8_q07","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of talipes equinovarus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The CAVE mnemonic describes the four components of talipes equinovarus. Which answer correctly identifies all four components?","choices":{"A":"Calcaneus (hindfoot in excessive dorsiflexion), Adduction (forefoot angled laterally), Valgus (foot everted outward), Extension (ankle held in maximal dorsiflexion at rest)","B":"Contracture (of the Achilles tendon), Articular (joint deformity), Varus (foot inverted), Extension (limited plantarflexion caused by shortened anterior compartment muscles)","C":"Cavus (high medial arch), Adduction (forefoot angled medially), Varus (foot inverted), Equinus (ankle held in plantarflexion with dorsiflexion limited by a tight Achilles tendon)","D":"Cavus (abnormally high lateral arch), Abduction (forefoot angled laterally), Valgus (foot everted), Equinus (ankle held in excessive plantarflexion from intrinsic muscle shortening)","E":"Curvature (of the spine), Adduction (hip adduction contracture), Varus (knee varus deformity), Extension (limited knee flexion from posterior capsular fibrosis and contracture)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The CAVE mnemonic for clubfoot describes: Cavus (high medial arch), Adduction (forefoot angled medially), Varus (hindfoot inverted), Equinus (limited ankle dorsiflexion). Calcaneus means excessive dorsiflexion — the opposite of equinus. Valgus means eversion — the opposite of varus. Abduction means lateral deviation — the opposite of adduction. The correction order in Ponseti casting follows the CAVE mnemonic from distal to proximal.","boardPrep":"Clubfoot (CAVE): Cavus = abnormally high medial arch (midfoot); Adduction = forefoot angled medially (forefoot/midfoot); Varus = foot inverted (hindfoot); Equinus = limited dorsiflexion (hindfoot/ankle). Mnemonic: CAVE = correction order from distal to proximal in Ponseti casting. The hindfoot is in varus and equinus; forefoot/midfoot in cavus and adductus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — Clubfoot (CAVE): Cavus, Adduction, Varus, Equinus; idiopathic 80% (rigid); Ponseti method = standard (5-10 serial casts, Achilles tenotomy 80%, FAB brace)"},{"id":"sdl8_q08","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of talipes equinovarus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement best describes the Ponseti method of clubfoot treatment and its expected outcomes?","choices":{"A":"The Ponseti method involves daily casting changes to achieve rapid correction of all four CAVE components simultaneously, followed by immediate full weight-bearing without bracing","B":"The Ponseti method involves a single-stage surgical correction of all four CAVE components in the operating room, followed by 6 weeks of casting and then functional rehabilitation","C":"The Ponseti method involves the French functional method using physiotherapy and taping; Achilles tenotomy is avoided; bracing is not required; outcomes equal surgical release","D":"The Ponseti method involves 2-3 serial casts for positional clubfoot only; idiopathic and syndromic clubfoot always require primary surgical release for acceptable long-term outcomes","E":"The Ponseti method involves weekly serial casting in CAVE order, usually 5-10 casts; Achilles tenotomy in approximately 90% of cases; excellent outcomes at 40 years of follow-up"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The Ponseti method uses weekly serial casting to correct deformities in CAVE order (cavus first, then adductus, then varus, then equinus), requiring 5-10 casts; Achilles tenotomy is performed in approximately 90% of cases to correct residual equinus; a foot abduction brace (FAB) follows: 22 hours a day for 3 months, then at night until age 3-5 years (Nelson, SDL 8 reading), or 1 to 4 years of bracing per CDT Orthopedics (SDL 9 p3). The method does NOT use daily cast changes. It does NOT begin with a foot abduction brace. It has replaced extensive soft tissue release surgery as the gold standard with excellent 40-year follow-up outcomes.","boardPrep":"Ponseti method: standard treatment for idiopathic clubfoot. Weekly serial casts correcting CAVE order (cavus first, then adductus, then varus, then equinus). 5-10 casts typically required. Achilles tenotomy performed in ~90% of cases (to correct residual equinus). Foot abduction brace (FAB, Dennis-Brown bar): 22 h/day for 3 months, then nights until age 3-5 years (Nelson); CDT Orthopedics: 1-4 years of bracing. Excellent results at 40-year follow-up. Gold standard — preferred over extensive soft tissue release surgery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — Clubfoot (CAVE): Cavus, Adduction, Varus, Equinus; idiopathic 80% (rigid); Ponseti method = standard (5-10 serial casts, Achilles tenotomy 80%, FAB brace)"},{"id":"sdl8_q09","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of talipes equinovarus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which characteristic best differentiates positional (postural) clubfoot from idiopathic congenital clubfoot?","choices":{"A":"Positional clubfoot always requires surgical correction because it is rigid and cannot be corrected with serial casting; idiopathic clubfoot is flexible and resolves with stretching only","B":"Positional clubfoot is associated with chromosomal anomalies and neuromuscular syndromes such as arthrogryposis; idiopathic clubfoot has no genetic component and is always unilateral","C":"Positional clubfoot is flexible (passively correctable), from intrauterine crowding; idiopathic clubfoot is rigid (not passively correctable) from intrinsic structural abnormality","D":"Positional clubfoot shows all four CAVE components on examination; idiopathic clubfoot shows only equinus and varus without cavus or adductus as the deformity is less complete","E":"Positional clubfoot is more common in males by a 4:1 ratio; idiopathic clubfoot is more common in females and requires immediate surgical repair in the first week of life"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Positional (postural) clubfoot is FLEXIBLE — passively correctable to neutral dorsiflexion and eversion — caused by intrauterine crowding, and treated with parental stretching with excellent prognosis. Idiopathic clubfoot is RIGID — not passively correctable — and requires Ponseti serial casting. The descriptions are not reversed. Positional clubfoot does not require surgery. Syndromic clubfoot (associated with chromosomal or neuromuscular disorders) is rigid and more treatment-resistant. Clubfoot overall has a 2:1 male predominance; the 4:1 figure in the distractor is incorrect.","boardPrep":"Clubfoot types: Idiopathic (80%) — isolated, rigid, intrinsic structural/vascular foot abnormality, requires Ponseti casting. Syndromic — rigid, associated with chromosomal/neuromuscular conditions (trisomy 18, myelodysplasia, arthrogryposis), more resistant to treatment. Positional — flexible (passively correctable), caused by inadequate intrauterine space, resolves with stretching. KEY: rigid vs flexible determines treatment approach.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — Clubfoot (CAVE): Cavus, Adduction, Varus, Equinus; idiopathic 80% (rigid); Ponseti method = standard (5-10 serial casts, Achilles tenotomy 80%, FAB brace)"},{"id":"sdl8_q10","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of metatarsus adductus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement most accurately describes metatarsus adductus and its relationship to other pediatric musculoskeletal conditions?","choices":{"A":"Metatarsus adductus is the second most common congenital foot deformity after clubfoot; it has no association with DDH; affects children from 2 to 5 years and is more common in males","B":"Metatarsus adductus is the most common congenital foot deformity; it is more common in singleton pregnancies; it has a strong association with spinal dysraphism in affected children","C":"Metatarsus adductus is more common in females than males by a 3:1 ratio; it occurs in 7 per 1000 live births in Caucasian infants and always requires surgical correction by age 2 years","D":"Metatarsus adductus is the most common congenital foot deformity; it is more common in twins; some studies show an association with DDH; affects infants typically under 1 year of age","E":"Metatarsus adductus is equally common in all populations; it has no known association with DDH; it is caused exclusively by tibial torsion rather than forefoot adduction deformity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Metatarsus adductus is the most common congenital foot deformity; it is more common in twins (intrauterine crowding); some studies show an association with DDH (shared etiology of intrauterine molding); and it typically presents in infants under 1 year. It is not second to clubfoot in incidence. The reported association is with DDH, not with spinal dysraphism. It is not exclusively caused by tibial torsion — forefoot adduction is the primary deformity with a neutral hindfoot.","boardPrep":"Metatarsus adductus: most common congenital foot deformity (incidence difficult to determine due to mild cases). More common in twins (intrauterine crowding). Associated with DDH (both caused by intrauterine molding). Usually in infants <1 year. Causes: intrauterine molding, anatomic variant. Foot: forefoot adduction, hindfoot neutral. Heel bisection line: normally through 2nd web space; in metatarsus adductus, line passes lateral to 2nd web space.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — Metatarsus adductus: most common congenital foot deformity; heel bisection line lateral to 2nd web space; flexible = stretching; recalcitrant = casting; association with DDH"},{"id":"sdl8_q11","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of metatarsus adductus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which physical examination finding on a bisection line drawn along the heel is most characteristic of metatarsus adductus?","choices":{"A":"The bisection of the heel falls medial to the 1st toe, indicating severe forefoot abduction and hindfoot valgus consistent with a rocker-bottom flatfoot deformity of the foot","B":"The bisection of the heel falls between the 3rd and 4th toes, indicating severe midfoot abduction with hindfoot varus consistent with a rigid clubfoot deformity in the infant","C":"The bisection of the heel falls through the center of the 2nd toe, which is the normal finding; deviation to the 1st web space indicates mild metatarsus adductus in newborns","D":"The bisection of the heel falls medial to the 2nd web space, indicating the normal alignment of the forefoot relative to the hindfoot in a newborn infant during examination","E":"The bisection of the heel falls lateral to the 2nd web space, indicating medial deviation of the forefoot relative to the hindfoot which is maintained in neutral alignment"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In metatarsus adductus, the bisection of the heel longitudinally falls LATERAL to the 2nd web space, because the forefoot is deviated medially (adducted) relative to the neutral hindfoot. The normal bisection passes through the 2nd web space. A line medial to the 1st toe would represent severe forefoot abduction (opposite deformity). A line between the 3rd and 4th toes would suggest a more severe varus pattern. Medial to the 2nd web space is the normal finding, not metatarsus adductus.","boardPrep":"Metatarsus adductus exam: bisect the heel longitudinally — normal line passes through 2nd web space. In metatarsus adductus: bisection line falls LATERAL to 2nd web space (forefoot is deviated medially). Hindfoot is neutral (distinguish from clubfoot where hindfoot is also in varus and equinus). Assess flexibility: gentle abduction pressure — flexible = correctable (good prognosis); rigid = requires more aggressive treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — Metatarsus adductus: most common congenital foot deformity; heel bisection line lateral to 2nd web space; flexible = stretching; recalcitrant = casting; association with DDH"},{"id":"sdl8_q12","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of metatarsus adductus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which treatment approach for metatarsus adductus is based on the rigidity of the deformity?","choices":{"A":"All cases of metatarsus adductus require immediate serial casting from birth regardless of flexibility; surgery is performed if casting fails after 6 months of conservative treatment","B":"Flexible metatarsus adductus requires immediate surgical metatarsal osteotomy at age 6 months; rigid metatarsus adductus resolves spontaneously with walking without any treatment","C":"Flexible deformity is treated with stretching and parental instruction; recalcitrant cases require serial casting; surgery is rarely indicated and reserved for severe persistent cases","D":"Rigid metatarsus adductus is treated with stretching and reverse-last shoes; flexible metatarsus adductus requires immediate surgical correction to prevent in-toe gait in adulthood","E":"Metatarsus adductus always resolves spontaneously without any intervention by age 3 years; no treatment is recommended for any degree of flexibility or rigidity of the deformity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Treatment of metatarsus adductus is based on deformity flexibility: flexible deformity is treated with parental stretching exercises at each diaper change; recalcitrant cases receive serial casting; surgery is rare and reserved for severe rigid persistent deformity. All cases do NOT require immediate casting regardless of flexibility. Rigid deformity does not resolve spontaneously with walking. Most mild-to-moderate flexible cases resolve with stretching alone, and the treatment escalation is stepwise.","boardPrep":"Metatarsus adductus treatment: (1) Stretching — gentle forefoot abduction stretching by parents at each diaper change for flexible deformity; (2) Casting — for recalcitrant flexible and mild-moderate rigid cases; (3) Surgery — rarely indicated, for severe rigid persistent deformity usually after age 4. Most mild-moderate cases resolve with stretching. The course slide states: \"Stretching → Recalcitrant: Casting → Rare: Surgery.\"","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — Metatarsus adductus: most common congenital foot deformity; heel bisection line lateral to 2nd web space; flexible = stretching; recalcitrant = casting; association with DDH"},{"id":"sdl8_q13","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of spinal dysraphism.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement correctly differentiates myelomeningocele (open spinal dysraphism) from spina bifida occulta (closed spinal dysraphism)?","choices":{"A":"Myelomeningocele opens both the vertebral column and the skin, exposing the meninges and spinal cord; spina bifida occulta has a vertebral defect with intact skin and no exposed neural tissue","B":"Myelomeningocele has an opening only in the skin without vertebral defect; spina bifida occulta has an opening in the vertebral column with meninges exposed but skin is intact over the defect","C":"Myelomeningocele is the milder form with only meningeal protrusion through the vertebral defect without cord involvement; spina bifida occulta involves complete cord exposure and paralysis","D":"Myelomeningocele involves only cerebrospinal fluid leakage through a skin defect without spinal cord exposure; spina bifida occulta involves full cord herniation with meningeal protrusion","E":"Myelomeningocele has no vertebral or skin defect but causes cerebellar herniation below the foramen magnum; spina bifida occulta is defined by herniation through both vertebral and skin defects"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Myelomeningocele (open spinal dysraphism) has an opening in both the vertebral column and the skin with meninges and spinal cord exposed — obvious at birth, requiring immediate surgical closure. Spina bifida occulta (closed) has a vertebral defect without skin opening; neural tissue is not exposed and there may only be skin stigmata. The two conditions are not reversed. Meningocele is intermediate — meninges herniate through the vertebral defect but the spinal cord itself is not exposed. Chiari II is a complication of myelomeningocele, not a subtype of spinal dysraphism.","boardPrep":"Spinal dysraphism types: Myelomeningocele (open/spina bifida aperta) = opening in vertebral column AND skin; meninges and spinal cord are exposed; obvious at birth; requires immediate surgical closure. Spina bifida occulta (closed) = vertebral column defect WITHOUT skin opening; neural tissue not exposed; may have skin stigmata (tuft of hair, dimple, hemangioma); often incidental finding. Meningocele = meninges herniate through vertebral defect but cord not involved (between the two).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 5 — Spinal dysraphism: myelomeningocele = open (cord exposed); spina bifida occulta = closed (skin intact); folic acid deficiency + valproate = causes; Chiari II + hydrocephalus = key complications"},{"id":"sdl8_q14","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of spinal dysraphism.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which etiologic factors are most strongly associated with the development of neural tube defects including spinal dysraphism?","choices":{"A":"Maternal diabetes mellitus and obesity are the only confirmed causes of neural tube defects; dietary folate intake has no proven relationship to neural tube defect prevalence or severity","B":"Retinoid (vitamin A derivative) exposure and alcohol consumption are the only confirmed environmental causes; folic acid supplementation has no effect on neural tube defect incidence","C":"Maternal hypothyroidism and gestational hypertension are the most common preventable causes; folate supplementation is recommended only if there is a previous affected pregnancy","D":"Folic acid deficiency during the periconceptional period and valproate (antiepileptic drug) exposure during early pregnancy are the most strongly implicated environmental causes","E":"Paternal smoking and advanced paternal age are the most strongly implicated etiologic factors; maternal nutritional deficiencies have not been shown to increase neural tube defect risk"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Folic acid deficiency during the periconceptional period and valproate (antiepileptic) exposure during early pregnancy are the most strongly implicated environmental causes of neural tube defects. Folic acid supplementation (400 mcg/day) prevents 50-70% of NTDs (Langman Ch 18). Maternal diabetes and obesity are risk factors but are not the only confirmed causes. Retinoids are teratogenic, but the claim that folate has no effect is false. Paternal factors are not the primary established NTD risk factors.","boardPrep":"Spinal dysraphism etiology: majority are isolated with multifactorial origin. Key causes: (1) Folic acid deficiency — folic acid 400 mcg/day periconceptionally prevents 50-70% of NTDs (Langman Ch 18). (2) Valproate (antiepileptic) — first-trimester exposure carries a 1-2% risk of NTDs (Katzung). Also: chromosomal disorders, other anticonvulsants (carbamazepine). Epidemiology: 1:2875 live births US; Hispanic highest rate (3.80:10,000); AA 2.73:10,000; Caucasian 3.09:10,000.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 5 — Spinal dysraphism: myelomeningocele = open (cord exposed); spina bifida occulta = closed (skin intact); folic acid deficiency + valproate = causes; Chiari II + hydrocephalus = key complications"},{"id":"sdl8_q15","sdlNumber":8,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of spinal dysraphism.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which complication of myelomeningocele is characterized by herniation of the cerebellar tonsils and brainstem through the foramen magnum?","choices":{"A":"Tethered cord syndrome involves downward herniation of the cerebellum through the foramen magnum and causes progressive lower extremity weakness and bladder dysfunction","B":"Hydromyelia involves herniation of the cerebellar tonsils through the foramen magnum and is the most common complication of all types of spinal dysraphism including spina bifida occulta","C":"Chiari II malformation (Arnold-Chiari type II) involves downward herniation of the cerebellum and brainstem through the foramen magnum and is characteristic of myelomeningocele","D":"Syringomyelia involves cerebrospinal fluid accumulation within the brainstem causing compression of cranial nerve nuclei and herniation of brainstem tissue into the foramen magnum","E":"Basilar invagination involves downward herniation of the cerebellar vermis through the foramen magnum and is the most frequent complication of myelomeningocele in neonates"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Chiari II malformation (Arnold-Chiari type II) involves downward herniation of the cerebellum and brainstem through the foramen magnum and is a near-universal complication of myelomeningocele. It causes CSF flow obstruction, contributing to hydrocephalus. Tethered cord causes progressive lower extremity weakness but does NOT involve foramen magnum herniation. Hydromyelia is an intramedullary CSF cyst. Syringomyelia is intramedullary fluid. Basilar invagination is upward (odontoid migration), not downward cerebellar herniation.","boardPrep":"Myelomeningocele complications: Chiari II malformation (almost universal in MMC — herniation of cerebellum/brainstem through foramen magnum; causes hydrocephalus, breathing problems, swallowing difficulties). Hydrocephalus (requires VP shunt in most). Tethered cord. Hydromyelia. Seizures. Learning disabilities. Urinary/bowel dysfunction. Skin ulcers. Orthopedic problems (paralysis level-dependent). Prenatal diagnosis: elevated maternal AFP + ultrasound (lemon sign/banana sign on fetal head).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 5 — Spinal dysraphism: myelomeningocele = open (cord exposed); spina bifida occulta = closed (skin intact); folic acid deficiency + valproate = causes; Chiari II + hydrocephalus = key complications"},{"id":"sdl8_q16","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of developmental dysplasia of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 2-week-old female infant, firstborn, delivered vaginally in breech presentation, is examined at her first wellness visit. On hip examination, when the hip is flexed 90° and abducted while the examiner lifts the femoral head anteriorly, a palpable clunk is felt. Which finding is this and what does it indicate?","choices":{"A":"Positive Ortolani sign indicating reduction of a previously dislocated femoral head back into the acetabulum; this infant requires ultrasound evaluation and pediatric orthopedic referral","B":"Positive Barlow sign indicating that the examiner has dislocated an unstable femoral head posteriorly out of the acetabulum; this is normal in newborns and requires no further workup","C":"Positive Galeazzi sign indicating functional shortening of the femur caused by a higher resting position of the femoral head on the affected side with the hips in a flexed position","D":"Positive Klisic test indicating that the line connecting the greater trochanter and the anterior superior iliac spine points above the umbilicus due to proximal femoral displacement","E":"Positive Thomas test indicating a hip flexion contracture from intrauterine positioning; this resolves spontaneously and does not require imaging or orthopedic referral in newborns"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lifting the femoral head anteriorly while abducting the hip with a palpable clunk is the Ortolani sign, indicating reduction of a previously dislocated femoral head back into the acetabulum. Barlow involves adduction with posterior force (dislocation maneuver). Galeazzi tests for functional femoral shortening (unequal knee heights). Klisic tests for proximal femoral displacement via a palpated line. Thomas test assesses hip flexion contracture. This infant with multiple DDH risk factors (female, firstborn, breech) requires ultrasound and pediatric orthopedic referral.","boardPrep":"DDH risk factors: female sex, firstborn, breech presentation, family history, oligohydramnios. Ortolani = REDUCTION of a dislocated hip (positive = clunk). Barlow = DISLOCATION of an unstable hip (positive = hip subluxes/dislocates). Both performed with hip flexed 90°. Positive exam → ultrasound if <4 months, X-ray if >4 months → pediatric orthopedics referral. Pavlik harness is first-line treatment for <6 months.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 5 — SCFE: both SCFE and DDH are high-yield pediatric hip conditions; DDH presents in infancy with instability; SCFE presents in adolescence with hip/knee pain and limited internal rotation; both require urgent orthopedic referral to prevent avascular necrosis"},{"id":"sdl8_q17","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of developmental dysplasia of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 2-month-old girl born breech is examined at a wellness visit. With the infant supine and both hips and knees flexed so that the feet rest side by side on the table, the left knee sits lower than the right. Which interpretation of this finding is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"A positive Ortolani sign, showing that a dislocated left femoral head has slipped back into the acetabulum with a palpable clunk","B":"A positive Barlow sign, showing that the left femoral head can be pushed posteriorly out of the acetabulum under gentle adduction","C":"A positive Klisic test, the line from the anterior iliac spine to the trochanter passing below the umbilicus in an older infant","D":"A normal finding in breech infants, since physiologic hip flexion contractures make knee heights unequal for the first 6 months","E":"A positive Galeazzi test, a functional shortening of the left femur listed among DDH findings in infants under 3 months"},"correct":"E","explanation":"With both hips and knees flexed and the feet together, a lower knee on one side is the Galeazzi sign, which the course lists for infants younger than 3 months with DDH as functional shortening of the femur, alongside hip instability on testing and asymmetric leg creases. A displaced femoral head sits more posteriorly, so the thigh looks shorter. Ortolani and Barlow are dynamic maneuvers that reduce or dislocate the hip with a palpable clunk; they are not judged by knee height. The Klisic test is listed for infants older than 3 months. Unequal knee heights are not a normal breech finding and, with a breech history, call for DDH work-up (ultrasound before 4 months) and pediatric orthopedic referral.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DDH exam by age: <3 months = Barlow/Ortolani instability, asymmetric creases, Galeazzi; >3 months = limited abduction, femoral shortening, Klisic; walking = Trendelenburg gait, limp, hyperlordosis. COMLEX: Risk factors include breech, female sex, firstborn; left > right.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — hip X-rays in the pediatric hip pain workflow"},{"id":"sdl8_q18","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of developmental dysplasia of the hip.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 16-month-old boy began walking at 14 months. His parents report he has an unusual gait. Exam shows a Trendelenburg gait, prominent right greater trochanter, shortened right leg, and hyperlordosis. Ortolani and Barlow tests are negative at this age. Which diagnosis is most likely and what is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Legg-Calve-Perthes disease presenting with avascular necrosis of the femoral head; initiate non-weight bearing immediately and order MRI to assess the degree of femoral head involvement","B":"Developmental dysplasia of the hip presenting in an ambulatory toddler; order an anteroposterior pelvis radiograph and refer urgently to pediatric orthopedics for evaluation","C":"Slipped capital femoral epiphysis causing a valgus deformity of the femoral head; obtain radiographs in the frog-leg lateral view and refer to orthopedics for immediate surgical pinning","D":"Cerebral palsy causing spastic hip adductor contracture and gait abnormality; refer to neurology for EMG testing and initiate physical therapy with botulinum toxin injection","E":"Normal developmental gait variation in a toddler; reassure the parents that hyperlordosis and mild leg length discrepancy are expected findings in newly ambulatory children at this age"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Trendelenburg gait, prominent greater trochanter, shortened leg, and hyperlordosis in a 16-month-old walking toddler are classic signs of undiagnosed ambulatory DDH. Ortolani and Barlow maneuvers become negative in ambulatory children because the hip capsule tightens. LCP (avascular necrosis) presents between ages 4-9 with hip pain (course slides SDL 5 p27, SDL 10 p24), not Trendelenburg gait from birth. SCFE affects adolescents with hip and knee pain and limited internal rotation. Normal developmental variation does not explain a shortened leg with prominent greater trochanter.","boardPrep":"DDH in ambulatory children: Ortolani/Barlow are negative once the hip capsule tightens. Presentation: Trendelenburg gait (positive Trendelenburg sign = hip abductor weakness on affected side), prominent greater trochanter, shortened leg, hyperlordosis, out-toeing, toe-heel walking. The course slides list these as ambulatory DDH findings. Imaging: plain X-ray. Undiagnosed DDH → early OA, chronic pain. Early referral to Pediatric Orthopedics is emphasized in SDL 8.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — Legg-Calve-Perthes disease: AVN of the femoral head in children 4-8yr with insidious hip pain and antalgic gait; distinguishes from DDH by age of presentation and radiographic findings of femoral head fragmentation rather than acetabular dysplasia"},{"id":"sdl8_q19","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteogenesis imperfecta.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 3-year-old girl presents with multiple long bone fractures following minimal trauma. Her sclerae appear distinctly blue. She has small, discolored teeth. Her mother reports she has had 6 fractures in her lifetime. Her father also had multiple childhood fractures. Skeletal survey shows osteopenia with cortical thinning. Which diagnosis and Sillence type best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Osteogenesis imperfecta Type II (perinatal lethal), characterized by multiple in-utero fractures and accordion rib deformity; survival beyond the neonatal period essentially never occurs","B":"Osteogenesis imperfecta Type III (progressive deforming), characterized by the most severe non-lethal course; sclerae vary from blue to white; most affected individuals are wheelchair-bound","C":"Osteogenesis imperfecta Type I (mild), characterized by blue sclerae, recurrent childhood fractures with minimal trauma, dentinogenesis imperfecta, and autosomal dominant inheritance","D":"Osteogenesis imperfecta Type IV (moderately severe), characterized by white sclerae, variable dentinogenesis imperfecta, short stature, and autosomal dominant inheritance pattern","E":"Child abuse (non-accidental trauma), which must be excluded before diagnosing any skeletal dysplasia; blue sclerae and dental abnormalities are not features of osteogenesis imperfecta"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Multiple long bone fractures from minimal trauma combined with blue sclerae, dentinogenesis imperfecta, and autosomal dominant family history in a 3-year-old = OI Type I (mild). Type II is perinatal lethal — this 3-year-old child is alive. Type III is the most severe non-lethal type with patients often wheelchair-bound; sclerae vary. Type IV has white sclerae and is moderately severe. Child abuse must always be excluded, but blue sclerae, dentinogenesis imperfecta, positive family history, and diffuse osteopenia are strongly suggestive of OI.","boardPrep":"OI Type I (mild): blue sclerae + recurrent fractures in childhood + presenile hearing loss (30-60%). Family history often positive (AD). Dentinogenesis imperfecta in some. Normal or near-normal stature. KEY diagnostic triad: fragile bones + blue sclerae + early deafness. OI vs NAT: OI can mimic child abuse; blue sclerae, dentinogenesis imperfecta, positive family history, and osteopenia help distinguish. Always consider OI when fractures are unexplained or disproportionate to trauma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — Pediatric vs adult bone: pediatric bone has greater porosity, periosteal vascularity, and growth plate; OI disrupts collagen-based bone matrix globally; Salter-Harris fractures in OI are complicated by the underlying bone fragility and abnormal collagen scaffold"},{"id":"sdl8_q20","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteogenesis imperfecta.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A genetics counselor explains the inheritance of osteogenesis imperfecta to a family. The affected child has a new (de novo) pathogenic variant in COL1A1. The parents are unaffected. Which statement is most accurate regarding OI inheritance?","choices":{"A":"Classical OI (Sillence Types I-IV) is autosomal dominant with common de novo mutations; recessive forms involve the collagen prolyl 3-hydroxylation pathway and tend to be more severe","B":"OI is exclusively autosomal recessive; both parents must carry one pathogenic variant to have an affected child; the recessive form is always milder than autosomal dominant variants","C":"OI is X-linked recessive; only males are severely affected; female carriers have mild bone fragility; the COL1A1 gene is located on the X chromosome and encodes alpha-1 collagen chains","D":"OI follows a codominant inheritance pattern; all forms have equal severity regardless of the number of pathogenic variants inherited; de novo mutations are extremely rare in OI","E":"OI is mitochondrial in inheritance; transmission is exclusively maternal; paternal contribution has no effect on OI severity or phenotype in any of the known molecular subtypes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Classical OI (Sillence Types I-IV) is autosomal dominant; de novo COL1A1/COL1A2 mutations are common, explaining why an affected child can have two unaffected parents. Autosomal recessive OI forms (LEPRE1, CRTAP, PPIB mutations affecting collagen prolyl 3-hydroxylation) tend to produce more severe phenotypes. OI is NOT exclusively autosomal recessive. It is NOT X-linked recessive — males and females are equally affected by classical forms. It is NOT mitochondrial in inheritance.","boardPrep":"OI genetics: Classical OI (Types I-IV) = autosomal dominant (COL1A1, COL1A2 mutations). De novo mutations common — unaffected parents can have affected child. Autosomal recessive forms = LEPRE1, CRTAP, PPIB (collagen prolyl 3-hydroxylation complex) → more severe phenotype. Type V = autosomal dominant (IFITM5). Parental mosaicism can cause recurrence risk even when parents appear unaffected. Genetic counseling is essential.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — Teratogens and abnormal MSK development: genetic mutations (AD, AR) cause intrinsic developmental abnormalities; OI results from dominant negative or haploinsufficiency mechanisms in Type 1 collagen synthesis analogous to how teratogens disrupt normal developmental signaling"},{"id":"sdl8_q21","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of osteogenesis imperfecta.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 12-year-old boy with osteogenesis imperfecta type III uses a wheelchair, has severe kyphoscoliosis and vertebral compression, and is followed by a multidisciplinary team. His parents ask which organ system accounts for most of the serious morbidity and mortality in OI. Which answer is best supported by the course reading?","choices":{"A":"Renal, because hypercalciuria from rapid bone turnover produces nephrocalcinosis and chronic kidney disease by adolescence","B":"Hepatic, because abnormal type I collagen in the liver capsule leads to progressive portal fibrosis and early cirrhosis","C":"Hematologic, because marrow space is crowded out by fracture callus, producing pancytopenia and recurrent sepsis","D":"Gastrointestinal, because weak collagen in the bowel wall causes recurrent spontaneous perforation in childhood","E":"Cardiopulmonary, with recurrent pneumonias and declining lung function in childhood and cor pulmonale in adults"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The OI chapter assigned for this SDL states that the morbidity and mortality of OI are cardiopulmonary: recurrent pneumonias and declining pulmonary function in childhood and cor pulmonale in adults. A small, deformed thorax and kyphoscoliosis restrict ventilation, and the course slides also list respiratory insufficiency, cardiac valve insufficiency, and aortic dilatation, which is why the cardiovascular system is monitored. Renal stone disease is not the listed cause. A hepatic, hematologic, or gastrointestinal process is not described as the main source of morbidity in OI. The chapter lists neurologic complications such as basilar invagination as additional, less common problems.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OI = defective type I collagen (COL1A1/COL1A2, mostly AD) → fragile bones, blue sclerae, hearing loss, dentinogenesis imperfecta; death mainly from cardiopulmonary failure. COMLEX: Multidisciplinary care with PT, bone-targeted therapy, dental, hearing, growth, and cardiovascular monitoring.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — type I collagen in bone, tendon, and ligament"},{"id":"sdl8_q22","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of talipes equinovarus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A newborn male is delivered at term and found to have bilateral rigid clubfoot on examination. The foot shows high medial arch, forefoot adduction, hindfoot inversion, and limited ankle dorsiflexion. The deformity is not passively correctable. Which is the most appropriate first step in management?","choices":{"A":"Schedule immediate extensive soft tissue release surgery (posteromedial release) in the first week of life to achieve complete correction before the tissues become too fibrotic","B":"Apply a rigid foot abduction brace (Dennis-Brown bar) immediately at birth without prior casting, worn 22 hours per day for 4 years to correct all deformity components","C":"Observe without treatment for 3-6 months to determine whether the deformity will resolve spontaneously, as most rigid clubfoot cases achieve spontaneous correction by 6 months","D":"Initiate the Ponseti method with serial casting beginning within the first week of life, correcting deformities in CAVE order with weekly cast changes for 5-10 weeks","E":"Perform genetic testing and karyotype analysis before initiating any orthopedic treatment, as all rigid clubfoot requires syndromic workup to guide the appropriate treatment plan"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Rigid bilateral idiopathic clubfoot requires Ponseti serial casting beginning in the first week of life, correcting deformities in CAVE order with weekly cast changes. Immediate extensive soft tissue release surgery (posteromedial release) is the historical approach that Ponseti has replaced as the standard of care. Foot abduction brace alone without prior casting is incorrect — the brace is applied AFTER casting. Rigid clubfoot will NOT resolve spontaneously. Genetic testing is not required before initiating treatment for isolated bilateral clubfoot.","boardPrep":"Clubfoot management: Ponseti method is the standard first-line treatment for idiopathic clubfoot — start EARLY (first week of life). Serial casting in CAVE correction order (cavus first → adductus → varus → equinus). 5-10 weekly casts. Achilles tenotomy in ~90% to correct residual equinus. Foot abduction brace (FAB/Dennis-Brown) 22 h/day for 3 months, then at night until age 3-5 years (Nelson, SDL 8 reading). NEVER start with FAB alone. Early surgery is NOT first-line; Ponseti method has replaced extensive soft tissue release.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — Fracture nomenclature and casting: serial casting and cast immobilization are orthopedic techniques applied across multiple pediatric MSK conditions; Ponseti casting for clubfoot uses the same principles of controlled mechanical force over sequential intervals to achieve progressive correction"},{"id":"sdl8_q23","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of talipes equinovarus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 10-week-old with idiopathic clubfoot has completed 8 serial Ponseti casts. The cavus, adductus, and varus components are fully corrected, but 20 degrees of equinus deformity persists. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Perform a percutaneous Achilles tenotomy to correct the residual equinus deformity; this is needed in approximately 90% of idiopathic clubfoot cases treated with Ponseti casting","B":"Apply a posterior night splint with the foot in maximum dorsiflexion for 3 months to stretch the Achilles tendon and correct the residual equinus without any surgical intervention","C":"Continue serial casting for an additional 10 weeks with the foot in progressive dorsiflexion to achieve correction of residual equinus without any tenotomy or surgical procedure","D":"Proceed with open surgical posteromedial release to address the residual equinus as this component always requires surgical correction when casting has not fully achieved correction","E":"Fit the foot abduction brace immediately without addressing the residual equinus, as the brace will passively stretch the Achilles tendon and correct the equinus over time alone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Residual equinus after correction of cavus, adductus, and varus with casting is treated with percutaneous Achilles tenotomy in approximately 90% of idiopathic clubfoot cases. A posterior splint alone cannot overcome a contracted Achilles tendon. Additional casting cannot achieve what the tenotomy provides for residual equinus. Open posteromedial release is not indicated when only equinus persists — targeted percutaneous tenotomy is the appropriate procedure. The foot abduction brace cannot passively correct an unresolved equinus deformity.","boardPrep":"Ponseti method — Achilles tenotomy: performed in ~90% of idiopathic clubfoot cases when residual equinus persists after casting is complete (cavus, adductus, and varus corrected). Percutaneous tenotomy under local anesthesia in clinic or OR. Final long leg cast worn for 3-4 weeks post-tenotomy (Nelson) to allow healing in corrected position. Then → foot abduction brace. Tenotomy avoids the need for extensive open soft tissue surgery. Long-term outcomes are excellent at 40-year follow-up.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the Achilles tendon is the most powerful plantarflexor; in clubfoot, a contracted Achilles is the primary cause of residual equinus; percutaneous tenotomy achieves correction that serial casting alone cannot overcome in most cases"},{"id":"sdl8_q24","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of talipes equinovarus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 4-month-old boy with idiopathic clubfoot finished Ponseti casting and had a percutaneous heel cord tenotomy, followed by a long leg cast for 3 weeks. The foot is now fully corrected. His parents ask what happens next and what most affects whether the deformity comes back. Which answer is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"No further treatment is needed once correction is complete, because the tenotomy permanently lengthens the heel cord and holds the foot in place","B":"An abduction brace worn 22 hours a day for 3 months, then at night until age 3 to 5; poor brace compliance is the main cause of recurrence","C":"Weekly casting continues until walking age, because the foot relapses unless it stays casted until weight bearing remodels the tarsal bones","D":"A posteromedial surgical release is scheduled at 12 months, because Ponseti correction is temporary and most feet need release to stay aligned","E":"Annual radiographs guide whether bracing is needed, because talocalcaneal parallelism on films predicts which corrected feet will relapse"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In the clubfoot chapter for this SDL, Ponseti casting and tenotomy are followed by a long leg cast for 3 to 4 weeks, then an abduction brace (bars and shoes) worn 22 hours a day for 3 months and at night until age 3 to 5. Compliance with bracing is essential because recurrence is common if the brace is not worn as recommended. Correction is not maintained without bracing. Casting is not continued until walking age. Surgical release is reserved for feet that fail minimally invasive treatment or for rigid syndromic and neuromuscular clubfeet, and the Ponseti results are excellent at up to 40 years. Radiographs are not recommended for idiopathic clubfoot and have not been shown to predict relapse.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Clubfoot (CAVE: cavus, adductus, varus, equinus): Ponseti weekly casts (5–10) → Achilles tenotomy (~90%) → abduction bracing; up to 20% need tibialis anterior transfer for dynamic supination. COMLEX: Examine the spine for occult dysraphism in every infant with clubfoot.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 5 — clubfoot is common in myelodysplasia, so inspect the spine"},{"id":"sdl8_q25","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of metatarsus adductus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 4-month-old infant is noted to have bilateral medial deviation of the forefoot during a well-child visit. The hindfoot is in neutral alignment. On examination, both feet are fully correctable to neutral with gentle lateral abduction pressure. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Apply bilateral serial casts immediately regardless of flexibility, as all metatarsus adductus deformities require casting to prevent in-toe gait and shoe wear problems in childhood","B":"Refer immediately to pediatric orthopedics for surgical metatarsal osteotomy to correct the flexible forefoot adduction before 6 months of age to prevent long-term gait abnormality","C":"Order bilateral lower extremity radiographs to quantify the metatarsus adductus angle before initiating any treatment or counseling the family about prognosis or management","D":"Apply bilateral reverse-last shoes immediately to reverse the forefoot adduction passively over 6 months, as parental stretching alone is insufficient for all metatarsus adductus cases","E":"Teach parents forefoot abduction stretching exercises at each diaper change; flexible metatarsus adductus has an excellent prognosis and typically resolves without further intervention"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Bilateral flexible metatarsus adductus (fully correctable with gentle lateral pressure) in a 4-month-old is treated with parental forefoot abduction stretching at each diaper change; most cases resolve without further intervention. Serial casting is not indicated for completely flexible deformity at this age. Surgery is not indicated for flexible deformity. Radiographs are not required before initiating treatment for a clinical diagnosis of flexible metatarsus adductus. Reverse-last shoes may be an option but parental stretching is the appropriate first step.","boardPrep":"Metatarsus adductus treatment algorithm: Flexible (passively correctable) → stretching by parents at every diaper change; most resolve spontaneously. Rigid (not correctable) → serial casting. Symptomatic residual deformity → surgery (rare), generally delayed until age 4-6 years (Nelson, SDL 8 reading). The course slide states: \"Stretching → Recalcitrant: Casting → Rare: Surgery.\" Reassurance is key for flexible cases; excellent prognosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — Talipes equinovarus: rigid clubfoot also begins management with serial casting (Ponseti method); the key principle is that deformity flexibility determines treatment; flexible metatarsus adductus (stretching) vs rigid clubfoot (Ponseti casting) exemplifies this principle"},{"id":"sdl8_q26","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of metatarsus adductus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 3-month-old has an inward-curving left foot. The examiner notes that the midfoot and hindfoot are normal, ankle dorsiflexion is full, and the subtalar joint moves normally. Which additional finding on the plantar view best fits metatarsus adductus rather than clubfoot?","choices":{"A":"A rigid equinus with the heel held high and a deep posterior crease, so the ankle cannot be brought to neutral dorsiflexion","B":"A heel in fixed varus with a cavus arch and a medial crease, so the sole faces inward and the foot cannot be passively everted","C":"A rocker-bottom sole with the talus palpable plantarly and the forefoot dorsiflexed, the shape of a congenital vertical talus","D":"A bean-shaped foot with a convex lateral border and a prominent fifth metatarsal base, while the hindfoot remains neutral","E":"A foot held in dorsiflexion and eversion with its dorsum touching the anterolateral leg, the appearance of calcaneovalgus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Nelson chapter for this SDL describes metatarsus adductus as adduction of the forefoot with a normal midfoot and hindfoot: the lateral border of the foot is convex, the base of the fifth metatarsal is prominent, the plantar view is bean-shaped, and ankle and subtalar motion are normal. Fixed equinus and hindfoot varus with cavus are components of clubfoot (CAVE), which this infant lacks. A rocker-bottom sole with dorsal dislocation of the midfoot and fixed equinus describes congenital vertical talus. A dorsiflexed, everted foot touching the anterolateral leg is calcaneovalgus, another molding deformity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Metatarsus adductus = most common congenital foot deformity; forefoot adducted, hindfoot neutral; heel bisector falls lateral to the second toe; bilateral in ~50%. COMLEX: Intrauterine molding → always examine the hips (some studies link MA with DDH).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — clubfoot CAVE deformity for comparison"},{"id":"sdl8_q27","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of metatarsus adductus.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 14-month-old walking child has metatarsus adductus that corrects only to neutral, not into abduction, with passive pressure. Parents have done office-taught stretching for several months, and they have tried putting the shoes on the opposite feet, with little change in the heel bisector. Which is the next nonoperative option described in the course reading before serial casting?","choices":{"A":"Observation without treatment, because a deformity that corrects to neutral will resolve on its own once walking begins","B":"Reverse-last shoes worn full time, about 22 hours a day, with the heel bisector line reassessed after 4 to 6 months","C":"Midfoot osteotomy now, because walking children have lost the remodeling potential that nonoperative care needs","D":"A night splint holding the foot in adduction, because positioning the forefoot medially relaxes the tight lateral structures","E":"Botulinum toxin to the abductor hallucis, because a dynamic great toe deformity is the main driver of the adducted forefoot"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Nelson chapter bases metatarsus adductus treatment on rigidity. Feet that overcorrect into abduction can be observed; feet that correct only to neutral get stretching, and in a walking child the shoes can be reversed. If that fails, reverse-last shoes that hold the foot abducted are worn full time (22 hours a day) and the foot is rechecked in 4 to 6 months; if there is no improvement, serial casts are considered. Observation is for feet that overcorrect into abduction. Surgery is reserved for symptomatic residual deformity and is generally delayed until 4 to 6 years of age. A splint holding the foot in adduction would reinforce the deformity. A dynamic hallux varus usually improves spontaneously and needs no active treatment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Metatarsus adductus ladder: observe (overcorrects) → stretching/reversed shoes (to neutral) → reverse-last shoes → serial casting (best before 8 months) → surgery rarely, at 4–6 years. COMLEX: Track the heel bisector line to monitor improvement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — footwear prescription principles"},{"id":"sdl8_q28","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of spinal dysraphism.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pregnant woman at 16 weeks of gestation has elevated maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) on routine screening. Subsequent targeted ultrasound is performed. Which condition is elevated AFP most commonly associated with in this context and what is the next step?","choices":{"A":"Elevated AFP is specifically associated with closed spinal dysraphism (spina bifida occulta) where neural tissue is not exposed; MRI of the maternal spine is the recommended next step","B":"Elevated AFP is exclusively associated with Down syndrome (trisomy 21); amniocentesis for karyotype is the recommended next step when AFP is elevated on routine prenatal screening","C":"Elevated AFP is associated with open neural tube defects including myelomeningocele; targeted ultrasound to look for a spinal defect and Chiari II malformation findings is next","D":"Elevated AFP is associated with placental insufficiency and intrauterine growth restriction; umbilical artery Doppler velocimetry is the recommended next step for elevated AFP levels","E":"Elevated AFP is a normal finding in pregnancy and does not require any additional workup unless it is elevated more than 10 multiples of the median for the gestational age"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Elevated maternal serum AFP is a biochemical marker for OPEN neural tube defects including myelomeningocele — the exposed neural tissue allows AFP to leak into the amniotic fluid and then into maternal serum. The next step is targeted ultrasound looking for lemon sign, banana sign, and absent cisterna magna. Closed spinal dysraphism (spina bifida occulta) does NOT cause elevated AFP because skin is intact. Down syndrome is associated with LOW AFP (not elevated). Placental insufficiency does not specifically elevate AFP.","boardPrep":"Spinal dysraphism prenatal diagnosis: Elevated maternal serum AFP (MSAFP) → open neural tube defects (open NTDs expose neural tissue and AFP leaks into amniotic fluid and maternal serum). Targeted ultrasound: \"lemon sign\" (scalloping of frontal bones), \"banana sign\" (cerebellar compression/Chiari II), absence of normal cisterna magna. Amniocentesis: amniotic fluid AFP and acetylcholinesterase confirm open NTD. Closed dysraphism (spina bifida occulta) = AFP is NORMAL (skin is intact, no leakage).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — Teratogens: valproate and folic acid deficiency cause neural tube defects; elevated AFP is the biochemical marker for open NTDs on prenatal screening; folic acid supplementation 400-800mcg periconceptionally reduces NTD risk by 50-70%"},{"id":"sdl8_q29","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of spinal dysraphism.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A newborn with myelomeningocele at the L3 level undergoes surgical closure within 24 hours of birth. At 2 weeks of age, she develops a rapidly enlarging head circumference, irritability, and a bulging fontanelle. Which complication is most likely responsible for this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Tethered cord syndrome causing progressive lower extremity weakness and urinary retention from re-tethering of the spinal cord at the surgical repair site in the neonatal period","B":"Wound dehiscence at the surgical closure site allowing CSF leakage and ascending bacterial meningitis causing fever, bulging fontanelle, and progressive neurological deterioration","C":"Basilar invagination compressing the brainstem from upward migration of the odontoid process through the foramen magnum causing acute obstructive hydrocephalus and apnea","D":"Syringomyelia with progressive intramedullary cyst expansion causing the head enlargement by compressing the aqueduct of Sylvius and disrupting normal cerebrospinal fluid dynamics","E":"Hydrocephalus from obstructed CSF flow due to the associated Chiari II malformation; most children with myelomeningocele develop it and need a ventriculoperitoneal shunt"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Progressive head enlargement with bulging fontanelle after myelomeningocele surgical closure = hydrocephalus from Chiari II-related obstruction of CSF flow at the foramen magnum, requiring ventriculoperitoneal shunt placement. Hydrocephalus is commonly associated with myelomeningocele (CDT Orthopedics; course slide p36 lists it as a complication). Tethered cord causes progressive lower extremity weakness but not enlarging head circumference. Wound dehiscence with meningitis would cause fever and sepsis. Basilar invagination is upward odontoid migration. Syringomyelia is an intramedullary cord cyst.","boardPrep":"Myelomeningocele complications: Hydrocephalus is common — caused by Chiari II obstruction of CSF flow at the foramen magnum. Presentation: enlarging head, bulging fontanelle, sunset sign (downward gaze), vomiting, irritability. Treatment: ventriculoperitoneal (VP) shunt. Chiari II malformation: downward herniation of cerebellum and brainstem through foramen magnum — hallmark of myelomeningocele (almost universal). Also: tethered cord, hydromyelia, seizures, urinary dysfunction, orthopedic problems.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 8 — Lumbosacral spinal stenosis: CSF flow obstruction is a shared mechanism in some spinal conditions; in myelomeningocele, Chiari II obstructs CSF at the foramen magnum causing hydrocephalus; in spinal stenosis, CSF is compressed at the lumbar level causing cauda equina syndrome"},{"id":"sdl8_q30","sdlNumber":8,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of spinal dysraphism.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 6-year-old girl with a repaired lumbar myelomeningocele has weakness of the calf muscles on both sides. Over the past year her right foot has come to rest dorsiflexed and everted, and she bears weight on her heel. Which mechanism best explains this foot deformity?","choices":{"A":"Intrauterine molding from oligohydramnios, the cause of the newborn calcaneovalgus foot that resolves within the first weeks","B":"Neuromuscular imbalance with weak or paralyzed gastrocsoleus, so unopposed dorsiflexors pull the foot into calcaneovalgus","C":"Folate deficiency in mid-childhood, which softens the calcaneus and lets body weight press the hindfoot into dorsiflexion","D":"Tethering of the cord that lengthens the Achilles tendon directly, so plantarflexion fails even though the calf is innervated","E":"Chiari II compression of the brainstem, which produces a spastic dorsiflexed foot through upper motor neuron involvement"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Orthopedic problems are a listed complication of spinal dysraphism. The Nelson foot chapter for this SDL notes that a calcaneovalgus deformity in an older child typically reflects a neuromuscular imbalance with weakness or paralysis of the gastrocsoleus, as in myelomeningocele; with the plantarflexors weak, the dorsiflexors pull the foot into dorsiflexion and eversion. Intrauterine molding causes the newborn calcaneovalgus foot, which resolves early and would not appear at age 6. Folate deficiency is a periconceptional risk factor for neural tube defects, not a cause of childhood foot deformity. Tethered cord causes progressive neurologic loss, not direct tendon lengthening. Chiari II is a hindbrain malformation linked to hydrocephalus and does not explain a lower motor neuron calf weakness pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Myelomeningocele (open NTD, ↑ AFP) complications: Chiari II, hydrocephalus, tethered cord, hydromyelia, urinary/GI problems, skin ulcers, orthopedic deformities (clubfoot, calcaneovalgus). COMLEX: Deficits depend on lesion level; care is multidisciplinary.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — neural tube defects and folate as the preventable risk factor"}],"9":[{"id":"sdl9_q01","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review primary regions of long bones as it pertains to anatomy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The region of a long bone located between the epiphysis and the diaphysis, containing predominantly cancellous bone, a rich vascular network, and the growth plate in skeletally immature individuals, is best described as which anatomical zone?","choices":{"A":"The metaphysis, the flared zone between the physis and diaphysis that is most metabolically active, most vascular, and the primary site of hematogenous osteomyelitis seeding in children","B":"The epiphysis, the bony terminus at each end of a long bone that is covered by hyaline articular cartilage and separated from the metaphysis by the cartilaginous growth plate","C":"The diaphysis, the elongated tubular shaft of a long bone composed predominantly of dense cortical bone surrounding the central medullary canal filled with yellow marrow in adults","D":"The periosteum, the fibrous vascular membrane covering the outer surface of cortical bone that contains osteoprogenitor cells responsible for appositional bone growth and fracture healing","E":"The endosteum, the thin cellular layer lining the inner medullary canal and trabecular surfaces that contains osteoclasts and osteoblasts involved in bone remodeling and marrow maintenance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The metaphysis is the flared zone adjacent to the physis, rich in cancellous bone and blood flow. Its sluggish capillary sinusoids and lack of phagocytic lining cells make it the primary site of hematogenous osteomyelitis in children. The epiphysis forms the articular end; the diaphysis is the compact cortical shaft; the periosteum and endosteum are membranous coverings rather than discrete anatomical zones.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Metaphysis = most vascular zone = osteomyelitis in kids. Diaphysis = yellow marrow in adults. COMLEX: Growth plate at metaphyseal-epiphyseal junction is the weakest point in pediatric bone under stress.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 1 — Bone histology: osteocyte anatomy and Haversian canal organization in cortical bone"},{"id":"sdl9_q02","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review primary regions of long bones as it pertains to anatomy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"In skeletally mature adults, the medullary canal of the femoral diaphysis is occupied predominantly by which type of tissue, and what does this represent compared to the composition found during fetal and early childhood development?","choices":{"A":"Red marrow containing hematopoietic precursor cells, representing the persistence of fetal marrow composition throughout adult life in order to meet the high metabolic demand of erythropoiesis in large bones","B":"Yellow marrow composed primarily of adipocytes, representing the normal age-related conversion from hematopoietically active red marrow that occupied the diaphysis during fetal development and early childhood","C":"Periosteal fibrous tissue that migrates inward from the cortical surface to fill the medullary space as endosteal remodeling reduces the diameter of the medullary canal with advancing age","D":"Cartilaginous matrix derived from retained physeal remnants that persists within the medullary canal after growth plate closure and gradually undergoes endochondral ossification in older adults","E":"Dense cortical bone that fills the medullary canal as endosteal apposition exceeds resorption over decades, progressively reducing marrow volume and increasing bone stiffness in older adults"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Red marrow progressively converts to yellow marrow during childhood, proceeding from distal to proximal and from peripheral to axial skeleton. By adulthood, only the axial skeleton and proximal femur and humerus retain significant red marrow. Reconversion of yellow to red marrow occurs in severe anemia, hematologic malignancy, and physiologic stress. Fetal and neonatal bones contain exclusively red marrow. The periosteum stays on the outer bone surface; it does not migrate into the medullary canal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Red to yellow marrow conversion: distal to proximal, peripheral to axial. Red marrow persists in: vertebrae, sternum, ribs, pelvis, proximal femur/humerus. COMLEX: Yellow marrow reconversion in hemolytic anemia — boards favorite.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 1 — Bone histology: osteocyte anatomy and Haversian canal organization in cortical bone"},{"id":"sdl9_q03","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review primary regions of long bones as it pertains to anatomy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The structural unit of compact cortical bone consists of concentric lamellae of mineralized matrix surrounding a central vascular channel, together forming a cylindrical unit aligned with the long axis of the bone. What is this unit called?","choices":{"A":"The trabecula, a thin rod or plate of cancellous bone forming an interconnected lattice oriented along lines of mechanical stress in the metaphysis and epiphysis of long bones","B":"The lacuna, the small ovoid cavity within mineralized bone matrix that houses an individual osteocyte and connects to adjacent lacunae via a network of microscopic canalicular channels","C":"The Volkmann canal, a channel that runs oblique or perpendicular to the bone axis connecting adjacent Haversian canals to each other and to the periosteal and endosteal surfaces","D":"The canaliculus, a submicroscopic channel extending between adjacent osteocyte lacunae that allows cytoplasmic process contact and nutrient diffusion throughout the mineralized matrix","E":"The osteon, also called the Haversian system, in which concentric bony lamellae surround a central Haversian canal containing arterioles, venules, and unmyelinated nerve fibers"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The osteon is the basic structural unit of compact bone. Each osteon consists of concentric lamellae surrounding a central Haversian canal (Pawlina Ch 8). Volkmann canals run perpendicular to Haversian canals and connect them to the periosteum. Lacunae house osteocytes; canaliculi connect adjacent lacunae. Trabeculae are the structural units of cancellous bone, not compact bone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteon = Haversian system. Concentric lamellae around central vascular canal. Osteocytes in lacunae communicate via canaliculi. COMLEX: Cortical bone remodeling disrupts osteons; cutting cones of osteoclasts tunnel through existing osteons.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 1 — Bone histology: osteocyte anatomy and Haversian canal organization in cortical bone"},{"id":"sdl9_q04","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss variables and nomenclature involved in appropriate fracture identification.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A long-bone fracture in which the fracture line wraps in a helical pattern around the shaft is characterized by which mechanism and what is the appropriate fracture nomenclature?","choices":{"A":"A spiral fracture caused by a torsional force applied along the long axis of the bone, in which the fracture propagates along a helical path of maximal shear stress","B":"A transverse fracture caused by a direct bending force perpendicular to the bone axis, in which the fracture line crosses the diaphysis at approximately 90 degrees to the long axis","C":"An oblique fracture caused by combined axial loading and angular force, in which the fracture line runs diagonally across the shaft at roughly 45 degrees to the long axis","D":"A comminuted fracture caused by high-energy direct impact, in which three or more fracture fragments are produced at a single level and the fracture cannot be reduced to two main fragments","E":"A segmental fracture caused by two separate high-energy impacts at different levels, in which an isolated middle bone segment is created between two distinct fracture lines"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Spiral fractures result from torsional or rotational forces. The helical fracture line follows the plane of maximal shear stress around the bone. Transverse fractures result from three-point bending. Oblique fractures result from combined loading. Comminuted fractures have three or more fragments. Segmental fractures have two separate fracture levels isolating a middle fragment. All of these terms are part of standard fracture nomenclature.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Spiral fracture = torsion. Classic boards scenario: spiral tibial fracture in a toddler from a twisting fall — \"toddler fracture.\" Spiral humerus fracture from arm-twisting is also a child abuse red flag. COMLEX: Document mechanism; mechanism predicts fracture pattern.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: Ottawa rules guide radiograph decision-making in acute ankle injury"},{"id":"sdl9_q05","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss variables and nomenclature involved in appropriate fracture identification.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"When describing a fracture radiographically, which term specifically refers to the directional relationship between the distal bone fragment and the proximal fragment, expressed as the direction the apex of the angle formed by the two fragments points?","choices":{"A":"Displacement, which refers to the lateral translation of one fragment relative to the other measured in millimeters or as a percentage of the bone shaft diameter at the fracture site","B":"Shortening, which refers to the longitudinal overlap of fracture fragments resulting in loss of overall bone length measured in centimeters on full-length standing or traction radiographs","C":"Rotation, which refers to axial twisting of one fracture fragment relative to the other around the long axis of the bone and may not be visible on standard two-view radiographs","D":"Angulation, which is reported as the direction the apex of the fracture deformity points — for example, apex-volar or apex-dorsal — and determines the direction of corrective manipulation required for reduction","E":"Comminution, which refers to the presence of three or more bone fragments at the fracture site typically resulting from high-energy trauma such as motor vehicle collision or fall from height"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Angulation describes the angle formed between fracture fragments and is reported by the direction the apex points. Apex-volar means the distal fragment is tilted dorsally, as in a Colles fracture. Displacement describes translational shift; shortening describes longitudinal overlap; rotation describes axial twist. All four descriptors are used together for complete fracture characterization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Complete fracture description requires: bone name, fracture type, location, displacement, angulation, rotation, shortening, open vs closed. COMLEX: Reduction technique is guided by reversal of the deforming force — knowing angulation direction determines how to apply corrective force.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: Ottawa rules guide radiograph decision-making in acute ankle injury"},{"id":"sdl9_q06","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss variables and nomenclature involved in appropriate fracture identification.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A fracture is classified as open when which defining criterion is met, and why does this classification significantly alter the clinical management approach?","choices":{"A":"A fracture is open when the fracture line extends into the adjacent joint space, creating an intra-articular injury that requires anatomic reduction to restore joint surface congruity and prevent arthritis","B":"A fracture is open when the periosteum is disrupted by the fracture, exposing the cortical bone surface and increasing the risk of delayed union due to loss of the periosteal osteoprogenitor cell layer","C":"A fracture is open when the fracture communicates with the external environment through a wound in the overlying skin, requiring emergent surgical debridement and antibiotics to prevent osteomyelitis","D":"A fracture is open when the bone fragments are widely displaced with greater than 50 percent translational shift relative to the bone shaft diameter, indicating high-energy mechanism and ligamentous disruption","E":"A fracture is open when marrow fat is released into the circulation, producing a fat embolism syndrome characterized by hypoxemia, petechiae, and confusion in the days following a long-bone fracture"},"correct":"C","explanation":"An open fracture communicates with the external environment, introducing contamination risk and dramatically increasing the likelihood of osteomyelitis. Management requires emergent operative debridement, irrigation, and IV antibiotics begun immediately (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 2). Gustilo-Anderson classification grades open fractures I through IIIC by wound size and soft tissue injury. Intra-articular extension is a separate descriptor; periosteal disruption occurs in most displaced fractures; fat embolism is a systemic complication, not the classification criterion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Open fracture = skin breach + fracture communication. Gustilo I: wound under 1 cm. Gustilo II: 1-10 cm. Gustilo III: over 10 cm or periosteal stripping. IV antibiotics begun immediately, debridement in OR. COMLEX: Osteomyelitis complication of open fracture — Staph aureus is most common organism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: Ottawa rules guide radiograph decision-making in acute ankle injury"},{"id":"sdl9_q07","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss differences between adult and pediatric bone stress.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which property of pediatric bone best explains why a bending force produces an incomplete greenstick fracture in a child but a complete transverse fracture in an adult subjected to the same force?","choices":{"A":"Pediatric bone has a thicker periosteum with a denser network of Sharpey fibers anchoring it to the cortex, generating mechanical resistance that halts fracture propagation across the bone before complete cortical failure","B":"Pediatric bone has a higher ratio of organic collagen matrix to inorganic mineral content, giving it greater plasticity and the ability to absorb energy by bending before the tension-side cortex ultimately fails","C":"Pediatric bone has a lower cortical bone mineral density due to ongoing endochondral ossification at the physis, reducing the stiffness of the diaphysis and allowing elastic bending without catastrophic failure","D":"Pediatric bone is supplied by a denser network of Haversian canals than adult bone, distributing stress over more vascular channels and preventing the stress concentration required to propagate a complete fracture line","E":"Pediatric bone undergoes faster remodeling than adult bone, allowing microcracks to be repaired before they can coalesce into a macroscopic fracture line, which slows the progression from incomplete to complete fracture"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pediatric bone has a higher collagen-to-mineral ratio than adult bone, giving it greater plasticity and energy absorption capacity. During a bending force, the tension cortex fails first while the compression cortex bends but remains intact, producing the classic greenstick pattern. Adult bone is stiffer and more brittle due to higher mineral content, causing both cortices to fail nearly simultaneously under the same force.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Greenstick = pediatric incomplete fracture from bending. Tension side breaks, compression side bends. Treat with reduction and casting. COMLEX: Pediatric bone material properties also explain torus/buckle fractures at the metaphysis from axial loading — different mechanism than greenstick.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — Osteogenesis imperfecta: defective Type I collagen causes brittle bone with fractures at minimal trauma"},{"id":"sdl9_q08","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss differences between adult and pediatric bone stress.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy falls and sustains a distal radius fracture. Radiograph shows cortical buckling on the dorsal surface of the distal radius metaphysis with no cortical disruption on the volar surface. This fracture pattern is best described as which type and from which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"A greenstick fracture caused by a bending force that fractures the tension cortex while the compression cortex remains intact, characterized by a visible cortical break on one side with intact cortex on the opposite side of the bone","B":"A Salter-Harris Type I fracture caused by shear forces through the distal radial physis, characterized by widening of the growth plate and physeal tenderness without a discrete cortical fracture line on standard radiographs","C":"A pathologic fracture caused by cortical thinning from a bone cyst or fibrous dysplasia lesion within the distal radial metaphysis, showing a cortical bulge due to the underlying lytic lesion rather than acute traumatic buckling","D":"A plastic deformation injury caused by the bone bending beyond its elastic limit without any cortical disruption, producing a visible bow to the entire metaphyseal segment rather than the localized cortical bulge seen in a buckle fracture","E":"A torus fracture, also called a buckle fracture, caused by axial compressive loading at the metaphysis that causes the cortex to buckle outward without fully fracturing, analogous to the buckling of a metal torus ring under compression"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A torus fracture results from axial compressive loading at the metaphysis. The cortex buckles outward on the compressive side without completing a fracture line. It is mechanically stable and typically treated with a short arm cast for 3 to 4 weeks. Greenstick fractures show cortical disruption on the tension side; Salter-Harris fractures involve the physis; plastic deformation shows global bowing without cortical buckling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Torus/buckle fracture = metaphyseal axial compression = cortical buckling. Stable, cast for 3-4 weeks, excellent prognosis. COMLEX: Distinguish from greenstick: torus = compression side buckle, no cortical break; greenstick = tension side cortical break with intact compression cortex.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — Osteogenesis imperfecta: defective Type I collagen causes brittle bone with fractures at minimal trauma"},{"id":"sdl9_q09","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss differences between adult and pediatric bone stress.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Pediatric long bone fractures are managed with less stringent alignment requirements than adult fractures in part because of the remodeling potential of growing bone. What is the primary mechanism driving this remodeling?","choices":{"A":"Intramembranous ossification at the fracture site activates dormant osteoprogenitor cells within the periosteum, producing a hypertrophic callus large enough to bridge angular deformities without requiring bony realignment","B":"Endochondral ossification at the fracture gap deposits a cartilaginous template that gradually mineralizes and reshapes under mechanical loading, correcting angular deformities through callus remodeling alone","C":"Secondary Haversian remodeling by cutting cone osteoclasts tunnels through the fracture callus and deposits new lamellar bone in the direction of loading, correcting angular deformities at the microscopic level","D":"Physeal-driven longitudinal bone growth combined with periosteal appositional remodeling guided by Wolff's law, which corrects angular deformities over months to years in proportion to the child's remaining growth potential","E":"Increased osteoclastic activity stimulated by parathyroid hormone surge after fracture resorbs the malunited callus, followed by osteoblastic refilling along functional loading lines to restore normal alignment"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Remodeling of fracture malunion in children is driven by two mechanisms: physeal growth, which progressively shifts bone away from the angular deformity as the bone elongates, and periosteal appositional remodeling under Wolff's law, which adds bone on the concave side and resorbs on the convex side. Greatest remodeling occurs in young children with maximal remaining growth and in deformities in the plane of joint motion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pediatric fracture remodeling potential is greatest when: young age with growth remaining, fracture near a rapidly growing physis, and deformity is in the plane of motion of the adjacent joint. COMLEX: Varus/valgus deformities remodel better than rotational deformities — rotation does not reliably correct with growth.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — Osteogenesis imperfecta: defective Type I collagen causes brittle bone with fractures at minimal trauma"},{"id":"sdl9_q10","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the Salter-Harris System for classification of pediatric fractures near a growth plate.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 9-year-old girl falls off her bicycle. Wrist X-ray shows a fracture line extending through the distal radial physis with a small triangular cortical fragment attached at the metaphyseal corner. The epiphysis is displaced but not split. Which Salter-Harris type is this, and what is its prognosis?","choices":{"A":"Salter-Harris Type II, the most common physeal fracture type, in which the fracture exits through the metaphysis creating the Thurston-Holland corner fragment, and prognosis for normal growth is generally favorable","B":"Salter-Harris Type I, in which the fracture passes entirely through the physis without involving the metaphysis or epiphysis, the radiograph appears nearly normal, and diagnosis is made clinically by tenderness directly over the physis","C":"Salter-Harris Type III, in which the fracture extends through the physis into the epiphysis and enters the joint, creating an intra-articular fracture that requires anatomic reduction to restore articular congruity","D":"Salter-Harris Type IV, in which a single oblique fracture line crosses the metaphysis, physis, and epiphysis, producing the highest risk of bony physeal bridge formation among the fracture types visible on initial radiograph","E":"Salter-Harris Type V, a crush injury of the germinal physis from axial compression that appears normal on initial radiograph and is diagnosed retrospectively when premature growth arrest becomes evident months later"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Thurston-Holland fragment — a metaphyseal corner fragment — is the hallmark of Salter-Harris Type II fracture. Type II is the most common physeal fracture and has a generally good prognosis because the germinal zone of the physis is spared. Type I is a pure physeal separation; Types III and IV involve the epiphysis and carry greater risk of growth disturbance; Type V is a crush injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Salter-Harris Type II = most common = metaphyseal corner fragment = Thurston-Holland sign. Mnemonic SALTR: Slipped, Above, Lower, Through, Rammed. Type V is the RAREST and WORST — often missed initially. COMLEX: CP8 pediatric named condition; follow-up to monitor for growth arrest.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 6 — Pediatric fractures: Salter-Harris and greenstick/torus classification in growing bone"},{"id":"sdl9_q11","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the Salter-Harris System for classification of pediatric fractures near a growth plate.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which Salter-Harris fracture type carries the worst long-term prognosis for normal bone growth, often presents with a normal or near-normal initial radiograph, and is definitively diagnosed only retrospectively after growth arrest becomes evident on follow-up imaging?","choices":{"A":"Salter-Harris Type IV, which extends through the metaphysis, physis, and epiphysis in a single oblique line, is visible on initial radiograph, has the highest surgical failure risk from bony bridge formation, but can be prevented with anatomic fixation","B":"Salter-Harris Type V, a crush or compression injury of the germinal zone of the physis that disrupts physeal chondrocytes without producing a visible fracture line, leading to premature physeal closure and growth arrest","C":"Salter-Harris Type III, which extends through the physis into the epiphysis creating an intra-articular fracture, is visible on initial radiograph, requires anatomic reduction, and carries moderate growth disturbance risk","D":"Salter-Harris Type I, a pure physeal separation with near-normal radiograph, occurs most commonly in neonates and young children, and generally has a favorable prognosis when recognized early and gently reduced","E":"Salter-Harris Type II, the most common physeal fracture with a visible metaphyseal corner fragment, has a generally favorable prognosis because the germinal zone is spared by the fracture exiting through the metaphysis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Salter-Harris Type V is a compression or crush injury of the physis that damages the germinal and proliferative chondrocyte layers without producing a fracture line visible on plain radiograph. It is diagnosed retrospectively when growth arrest or angular deformity is noted at follow-up. Type IV carries the highest surgical risk of bridge formation among radiographically visible types, but Type V has the worst overall prognosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Type V = axial crush of physis = normal X-ray = diagnosed retrospectively = worst prognosis. Type IV = crosses all three layers = needs fixation to prevent physeal bridge. COMLEX: Physeal fractures need follow-up growth monitoring with contralateral comparison X-rays.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 6 — Pediatric fractures: Salter-Harris and greenstick/torus classification in growing bone"},{"id":"sdl9_q12","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the Salter-Harris System for classification of pediatric fractures near a growth plate.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 14-year-old male soccer player inverts his ankle. Radiograph shows a fracture line extending from the distal tibial physis vertically downward through the lateral epiphysis into the ankle joint, with no metaphyseal fragment visible. Which Salter-Harris type is this and what management principle is critical?","choices":{"A":"Salter-Harris Type III, an epiphyseal fracture with intra-articular extension, requiring anatomic reduction of the articular surface to restore joint congruity and prevent post-traumatic arthritis at the ankle","B":"Salter-Harris Type II, in which the fracture exits through the metaphysis producing a corner fragment rather than splitting the epiphysis, generally managed with closed reduction and casting without strict articular alignment requirements","C":"Salter-Harris Type I, a pure physeal separation without epiphyseal or metaphyseal bony fragment, managed with immobilization alone since the articular surface is not involved and growth plate anatomy is preserved","D":"Salter-Harris Type IV, in which a single oblique fracture line crosses metaphysis, physis, and epiphysis simultaneously, requiring anatomic fixation to prevent a bony bridge across the physis that would cause angular growth arrest","E":"Salter-Harris Type V, a compression injury to the physis with no fracture line visible on plain radiograph, diagnosed only after growth arrest appears on follow-up and not presenting acutely with an epiphyseal fracture line"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Salter-Harris Type III fractures extend from the physis downward through the epiphysis into the joint. This pattern is common at the distal tibia in adolescents and at the distal femur. The key management principle is anatomic reduction of the articular surface, typically requiring CT to assess reduction adequacy. Failure to achieve anatomic reduction leads to post-traumatic arthritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Type III = physis + epiphysis = intra-articular. CT scan to assess reduction. Needs anatomic reduction to prevent arthritis. Classic boards: triplane fracture of the distal tibia in adolescents is a variant combining Types II and III. COMLEX: Growth arrest risk from asymmetric physeal closure causing angular deformity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 6 — Pediatric fractures: Salter-Harris and greenstick/torus classification in growing bone"},{"id":"sdl9_q13","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old cyclist is struck by a car bumper at knee height while riding. Radiograph of the knee shows depression of the lateral tibial plateau. Which imaging finding, if present on the lateral knee radiograph, would be pathognomonic for an intra-articular fracture communicating with the joint space?","choices":{"A":"A joint effusion seen as increased soft tissue density in the suprapatellar recess on the lateral radiograph, reflecting hemarthrosis that has accumulated within the knee joint after the plateau injury","B":"Cortical disruption of the tibial plateau surface seen as a step-off deformity on the AP radiograph, reflecting depression of the lateral articular surface by the femoral condyle at the time of impact","C":"Periosteal new bone formation along the proximal tibial metaphysis seen as a thin periosteal layer on the AP radiograph, reflecting periosteal elevation by hematoma extending from the fracture","D":"A lipohemarthrosis seen as a fat-fluid level in the suprapatellar pouch on a horizontal-beam lateral radiograph, as medullary fat enters the joint through the intra-articular fracture site","E":"Valgus alignment of the tibial plateau on a weight-bearing stress radiograph, reflecting collapse of the lateral compartment under axial loading through the depressed articular fragment"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lipohemarthrosis is the pathognomonic finding of intra-articular fracture. On a cross-table lateral radiograph, fat from the bone marrow layers above blood in the joint, creating a fat-fluid level in the suprapatellar pouch. A simple effusion has no fat-fluid level and occurs with ligament injuries as well as fractures. A step-off shows a plateau fracture but not joint communication, periosteal new bone is a later healing response rather than an acute sign, and valgus collapse on stress views shows compartment depression, not marrow fat in the joint. Lipohemarthrosis confirms that the fracture extends into the joint, even when the fracture itself is subtle on other views.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lipohemarthrosis on lateral knee X-ray = intra-articular fracture = pathognomonic. Requires horizontal-beam lateral. Also seen with occult tibial plateau fractures in elderly. COMLEX: Get MRI if X-ray shows effusion but no visible fracture — occult tibial plateau fractures are common in osteoporotic patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — Knee fractures: tibial plateau and patellar fractures in the diagnostic workflow for knee pain"},{"id":"sdl9_q14","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A trauma lecture compares distal femur fractures in adults and children. According to the course, which statement best describes the typical mechanism of injury in each group?","choices":{"A":"Both groups usually sustain distal femur fractures from low-energy falls, because the thin metaphyseal cortex fails at the same low load","B":"Adults usually sustain them from high-energy trauma such as car crashes, whereas children usually fracture from simple falls at play","C":"Both groups usually sustain them from high-energy trauma, because the distal femur is the strongest metaphysis in the skeleton","D":"Adults usually fracture from repetitive running loads, whereas children usually fracture from direct blows during contact sports","E":"Adults usually sustain them from low-energy mechanisms such as a fall, whereas children usually need high-energy trauma to break"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course lists distal femur fractures as 3 to 6% of adult fractures, more common in females, with a low-energy mechanism such as a fall in adults and a high-energy mechanism in children. In older adults, osteoporotic metaphyseal bone breaks with modest force, while the healthy, more elastic bone of a child needs a large force to break at this level. Saying both groups fracture with low energy ignores the high-energy mechanism in children. Reversing the pattern (high energy in adults, falls in children) contradicts the course. A high-energy mechanism is not typical in adults. Repetitive running loads describe stress fractures, not the distal femur fractures discussed here. Management in both groups is PRICE, immobilization, and surgical consultation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Knee fractures: tibial plateau (proximal tibia; females >51, osteoporosis; watch for compartment syndrome), patella (direct blow; surgery if >2 mm step-off, >3 mm displacement, or extensor disruption), distal femur (adults low energy, children high energy). COMLEX: A high-energy distal femur fracture in a child should prompt a search for other injuries.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — bone mineral loss and fracture risk in older adults"},{"id":"sdl9_q15","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 47-year-old falls directly onto his patella. Radiograph shows a transverse patellar fracture with 5 mm of articular step-off and the patient is unable to perform an active straight-leg raise. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Immobilization in a cylinder cast or knee immobilizer in full extension for 8 weeks followed by progressive quadriceps strengthening, allowing the fragments to unite without operative soft tissue disruption","B":"Open reduction and internal fixation using tension band wiring or cannulated screws to restore articular congruity and repair the extensor mechanism, followed by early range-of-motion rehabilitation","C":"Aspiration of the hemarthrosis and application of a functional knee brace locked in extension, followed by gradual unlocking of the brace over 6 weeks as quadriceps control and healing progress","D":"Patellectomy with repair of the quadriceps tendon directly to the patellar tendon stump, followed by extensor mechanism rehabilitation to restore active knee extension and straight-leg raise","E":"Urgent total knee arthroplasty with patellar resurfacing to address both the acute fracture and the patellar articular surface damage in a single definitive operative procedure"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Surgical indications for patellar fracture are articular step-off greater than 2 mm or fragment separation greater than 3 mm, and disruption of the extensor mechanism demonstrated by inability to perform a straight-leg raise. Tension band wiring converts tensile forces into compressive forces across the fracture during knee flexion. Non-displaced fractures with an intact extensor mechanism are managed non-operatively in extension, but casting or a locked brace cannot restore a 5 mm step-off or a disrupted extensor mechanism. Patellectomy is reserved for severely comminuted fractures that cannot be fixed and permanently reduces quadriceps mechanical advantage, and arthroplasty is not a treatment for an isolated traumatic patellar fracture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Patellar fracture surgical indications: articular step-off over 2 mm, separation over 3 mm, or failed straight-leg raise. Tension band wiring = compression across fracture with knee movement. COMLEX: Test extensor mechanism integrity at every knee fracture exam — straight-leg raise is the key clinical test.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — Knee fractures: tibial plateau and patellar fractures in the diagnostic workflow for knee pain"},{"id":"sdl9_q16","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of tibial shaft fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The tibia is more frequently involved in open fractures than any other long bone. Which anatomical feature is most responsible for this predisposition?","choices":{"A":"The tibial diaphysis has a relatively thin cortex compared to other long bones, causing the bone to fracture into sharp pointed fragments that more readily perforate surrounding soft tissues during high-energy injury","B":"The tibia bears the majority of axial load in the lower extremity, resulting in high-energy fracture patterns with greater displacement that tend to produce larger soft tissue injuries than weight-bearing bones elsewhere","C":"The tibial nutrient artery enters at the posterior cortex, creating a stress concentration point at the posterior diaphysis that predisposes to spiral fractures propagating toward the subcutaneous anterior surface","D":"The anteromedial tibial surface is subcutaneous throughout its entire length with minimal overlying soft tissue, making it highly susceptible to skin perforation when fracture fragments displace medially","E":"The interosseous membrane between tibia and fibula restricts outward periosteal expansion after fracture, directing displaced fragments toward the medial subcutaneous surface rather than allowing dissipation into deeper compartments"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The anteromedial border of the tibia is subcutaneous from the tibial tubercle to the medial malleolus, covered only by skin and subcutaneous fat. This minimal soft tissue coverage means that even relatively small amounts of fracture displacement can produce skin perforation. Combined with its exposed position, this explains why the tibia has the highest open fracture rate of any long bone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tibia = most common open fracture = subcutaneous anteromedial surface. Gustilo-Anderson classification I-IIIC. COMLEX: Open fracture management — IV antibiotics begun immediately; emergent irrigation and debridement within 6 hours is the accepted standard, though the evidence for that window is sparse (SDL 25 reading). Cephalosporin for all grades; add gram-negative coverage for Grade II/III.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — adult versus pediatric bone stress, the overuse end of the tibial injury spectrum (MTSS, stress fracture)"},{"id":"sdl9_q17","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of tibial shaft fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 31-year-old sustains a closed tibial shaft fracture while skiing. Five hours after a splint is applied, he reports pain rated 9 out of 10 that is not relieved by opioids, and has pain with passive dorsiflexion of his toes. The compartments feel tense to palpation. What is the most appropriate immediate management?","choices":{"A":"Emergent four-compartment fasciotomy of the leg, since disproportionate pain and pain with passive muscle stretch are the key clinical signs of compartment syndrome","B":"Measurement of compartment pressures using a pressure monitor before proceeding, with fasciotomy reserved for patients whose absolute compartment pressure exceeds 50 mmHg","C":"Bivalving and spreading the splint down to skin level to reduce external compression, followed by reassessment of pain, passive stretch, and compartment firmness in 2 hours","D":"Elevation of the extremity well above the level of the heart to reduce venous engorgement and lower compartment pressure, together with ice and continued opioid analgesia","E":"Emergent MRI of the leg to confirm muscle ischemia and edema within the anterior and deep posterior compartments before proceeding to operative fasciotomy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Compartment syndrome is a clinical diagnosis. Classic features are pain out of proportion to injury and pain with passive stretch of the muscles within the affected compartment. When clinical signs are unequivocal, emergent fasciotomy should not be delayed for pressure measurement. An absolute-pressure cutoff of 50 mmHg is too high and delays decompression. Bivalving the splint relieves external pressure but does not treat established compartment syndrome, elevating the limb above the heart lowers the arteriovenous gradient and worsens perfusion (keep it at heart level), and MRI only delays a clinical diagnosis. If pressure measurement is used, fasciotomy is indicated when the delta pressure (diastolic BP minus compartment pressure) is less than or equal to 30 mmHg.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Compartment syndrome = 5 Ps (pain, pallor, paresthesia, paralysis, pulselessness — late). Pain with passive stretch is earliest and most reliable sign. Delta-P threshold for fasciotomy: diastolic BP minus compartment pressure less than or equal to 30 mmHg. COMLEX: Tibial shaft fracture is the most common cause of lower leg compartment syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — adult versus pediatric bone stress, the overuse end of the tibial injury spectrum (MTSS, stress fracture)"},{"id":"sdl9_q18","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of tibial shaft fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the gold-standard definitive treatment for a displaced, closed tibial shaft fracture in a healthy adult, and why is this approach preferred over plating?","choices":{"A":"Open reduction and internal fixation using a medial plate applied to the anteromedial surface, which provides excellent stability but carries a high wound complication risk given the minimal soft tissue coverage over the tibial surface","B":"Long leg casting in a three-point mold with non-weight bearing for 12 weeks, which is appropriate for minimally displaced stable fractures but cannot control rotation or maintain length in significantly displaced tibial shaft fractures","C":"Intramedullary nailing, which restores length, alignment, and rotational stability while preserving the periosteal blood supply by avoiding open dissection of the fracture site, and allows early weight bearing","D":"External fixation with a unilateral frame spanning the fracture site, which is the preferred definitive treatment for open tibial fractures with contaminated wounds but results in higher malunion rates than nailing for closed injuries","E":"Functional bracing after initial casting for 4 weeks to allow early soft tissue healing, which has been used historically but produces unacceptable angular malunion rates compared to intramedullary nailing for fractures with displacement"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Intramedullary nailing is the gold standard for displaced tibial shaft fractures. The nail is inserted without exposing the fracture, preserving the periosteal blood supply and fracture hematoma. It controls length, alignment, and rotation through interlocking screws. Medial plating is avoided because the anteromedial tibial surface has minimal soft tissue coverage. External fixation is used for temporizing open fractures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tibial shaft fracture definitive treatment = intramedullary nailing. Preserves periosteal blood supply. Controls rotation via interlocking screws. COMLEX: Nail is inserted through patellar tendon approach. Compartment syndrome can still develop after nailing — monitor post-op.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — adult versus pediatric bone stress, the overuse end of the tibial injury spectrum (MTSS, stress fracture)"},{"id":"sdl9_q19","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of fibular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An isolated mid-shaft fibular fracture from a direct blow is noted on X-ray with an intact ankle mortise. How is this fracture managed, and what anatomical rationale justifies this approach?","choices":{"A":"Weight bearing as tolerated in an air-stirrup brace or walking boot, since the fibula is not a weight-bearing bone and this isolated shaft fracture is mechanically stable","B":"Surgical fixation with a lateral plate and screws to prevent late valgus angulation of the ankle and fibular shortening, followed by protected weight bearing in a boot for 6 weeks","C":"Non-weight-bearing immobilization in a long leg cast for 8 weeks to protect the fibula until radiographic union, followed by progressive weight bearing and ankle range of motion","D":"Intramedullary nailing of the fibula to provide axial and rotational stability and allow immediate full weight bearing, with hardware removal after radiographic union","E":"Emergent orthopedic referral for operative fixation within 24 hours, since displacement of the fibular shaft can progress and widen the ankle mortise if fixation is delayed"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The fibula does not bear or transfer body weight (Moore Ch 7). The interosseous membrane and intact tibia provide stability in isolated mid-shaft fibular fractures. As long as the ankle mortise is intact, conservative treatment with weight bearing as tolerated provides excellent outcomes. Plating, intramedullary nailing, long-leg casting, and urgent operative referral add risk or delay recovery without benefit for this stable injury; fixation is reserved for fractures with syndesmotic or mortise disruption. The critical clinical concern is always ensuring the ankle mortise is intact, as syndesmotic disruption or deltoid ligament injury changes management entirely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Isolated fibular shaft fracture with intact ankle mortise = conservative management. The fibula is not weight-bearing (Moore Ch 7). COMLEX: Always assess full ankle views — widened mortise changes fracture to unstable Weber B or C, requiring surgery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injuries: common peroneal nerve vulnerability at the fibular neck"},{"id":"sdl9_q20","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of fibular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old twists his ankle and is found to have no fracture on ankle radiographs, but he has proximal fibular tenderness. Full-length tibia-fibula films reveal a fracture at the fibular neck. What is the diagnosis and which associated ankle structure must be assessed for integrity?","choices":{"A":"An isolated stress fracture of the fibular neck from repetitive loading, which develops insidiously over weeks to months and is typically seen in runners and military recruits","B":"A peroneal nerve traction injury at the fibular head from the twisting mechanism, producing tenderness over the nerve with foot drop and dorsal foot numbness","C":"A Maisonneuve fracture: a pronation-external rotation injury that disrupts the syndesmosis and interosseous membrane, so the syndesmosis and deltoid ligament must be assessed","D":"A proximal tibiofibular joint dislocation from the twisting injury, which presents with proximal lateral knee pain, a prominent fibular head, and peroneal nerve symptoms","E":"A fracture of the fibular head from a direct varus knee mechanism, which is associated with lateral collateral ligament injury and peroneal nerve palsy at the knee"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Maisonneuve fracture results from a pronation-external rotation force at the ankle. The medial ankle structures fail first (deltoid ligament or medial malleolus), then the interosseous membrane tears proximally, finally producing a proximal fibular fracture. The ankle films appear nearly normal, but medial clear space widening indicates ankle instability. Full-length fibular films must be obtained in any ankle injury with unexplained mortise widening. A stress fracture lacks an acute ankle mechanism, a nerve traction injury does not explain a fibular neck fracture, and proximal tibiofibular dislocation or a varus fibular head fracture are knee injuries that do not involve the distal syndesmosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Maisonneuve = proximal fibular fracture from ankle mechanism. Mechanism: pronation-external rotation. Associated with deltoid ligament rupture or medial malleolus fracture. COMLEX: If ankle mortise is widened without a visible distal fibular fracture, get full-length films — classic boards pitfall.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injuries: common peroneal nerve vulnerability at the fibular neck"},{"id":"sdl9_q21","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of fibular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"According to the Danis-Weber classification, a fibular fracture occurring above the level of the ankle mortise and above the tibiofibular syndesmosis is classified as Weber C. What is the clinical implication of this fracture level?","choices":{"A":"Weber C implies the fracture is extra-articular and mechanically stable, since the fibular fracture is remote from the ankle joint and the syndesmosis remains intact because the fracture is above the level of the tibiofibular ligaments","B":"Weber C implies isolated lateral ankle instability from disruption of the anterior talofibular ligament without syndesmotic injury, requiring only cast immobilization and weight-bearing restriction until soft tissue healing is complete","C":"Weber C implies the fracture occurred from an inversion mechanism, consistent with the most common ankle injury pattern, and is associated with lateral ligament complex injury rather than syndesmotic disruption","D":"Weber C implies a stable fracture pattern amenable to closed treatment in a short leg walking cast, since the fibular fracture above the syndesmosis relieves rather than transmits destabilizing forces to the ankle mortise","E":"Weber C implies likely disruption of the tibiofibular syndesmosis and interosseous membrane, creating an unstable ankle mortise that needs fibular fixation plus syndesmotic positional screw or suture-button stabilization"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Weber C fractures occur above the tibiofibular syndesmosis and imply syndesmotic disruption because the fibula must fracture proximal to the intact syndesmosis for the fracture to occur at that level. The syndesmotic disruption destabilizes the ankle mortise, allowing lateral talar shift under loading. Surgical treatment includes fibular fixation and syndesmotic stabilization with a positional screw or suture-button device placed through the fibula and tibia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Weber A = below syndesmosis = stable. Weber B = at syndesmosis = variable. Weber C = above syndesmosis = unstable. Weber C always requires surgery. COMLEX: Test syndesmotic integrity with external rotation stress test and squeeze test. MRI or stress radiographs confirm syndesmotic disruption.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injuries: common peroneal nerve vulnerability at the fibular neck"},{"id":"sdl9_q22","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of ankle fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A quality-improvement project reviews adult ankle fractures seen in an emergency department. According to the epidemiology presented in the course, which pattern would the team expect to find?","choices":{"A":"Most fractures are trimalleolar, and men and women are affected mainly after age 70, from osteoporotic falls in the home","B":"Most fractures are bimalleolar, and men are affected far more often than women in every age group because of sports","C":"Most fractures are unimalleolar, with similar rates by sex: men mostly as young adults and women mostly at 50 to 70","D":"Most fractures involve the talar dome, and women are affected mainly as young adults while men are affected after 70","E":"Most fractures are isolated posterior malleolar, with women affected four times more often than men at all ages"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course gives an ankle fracture incidence of about 187 per 100,000 people per year. Most are malleolar: 60 to 70% unimalleolar, 15 to 20% bimalleolar, and 7 to 12% trimalleolar. Men and women are affected equally overall, with men affected mainly as young adults and women mainly between 50 and 70 years. Trimalleolar and bimalleolar fractures are the minority, so neither is most common. Men do not outnumber women in every age group. Talar dome injuries and isolated posterior malleolar fractures are not the common patterns described, and women are not affected four times more often.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ankle fractures: unimalleolar 60–70% > bimalleolar 15–20% > trimalleolar 7–12%; Ottawa ankle rules decide imaging; Danis-Weber level (A below, B at, C above the syndesmosis) guides stability. COMLEX: Bimodal by sex: young men (sport, trauma), women 50–70 (bone fragility).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ottawa ankle rules and ankle sprain grading"},{"id":"sdl9_q23","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of ankle fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A trimalleolar ankle fracture involves fractures of which three bony structures of the ankle?","choices":{"A":"The medial malleolus, lateral malleolus, and the anterior tibial plafond, where the anterior rim fracture is produced by forced dorsiflexion with axial loading of the talus","B":"The medial malleolus, distal fibular shaft, and the posterior tibial shaft, with the fibular component typically located above the syndesmosis in a Weber C pattern","C":"The lateral malleolus, navicular, and posterior malleolus, produced by a high-energy axial load that drives the talus into the distal tibia and midfoot","D":"The medial malleolus, lateral malleolus, and posterior malleolus, where the posterior malleolus is the posterior articular rim of the distal tibial plafond","E":"The medial malleolus, posterior malleolus, and the anterior process of the calcaneus, produced by forced inversion and plantarflexion of the hindfoot"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Trimalleolar fractures involve the medial malleolus, lateral malleolus, and posterior malleolus. The posterior malleolus is the posterior articular rim of the distal tibia; the anterior plafond, tibial shaft, navicular, and calcaneus are not malleoli. When it is fractured, it removes the posterior osseous constraint on the talus, significantly increasing posterior talar subluxation risk. The larger the posterior malleolus fragment, the more instability is created. Fragments involving more than 25% to 33% of the articular surface have traditionally been fixed (a threshold the SDL 25 reading calls controversial).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trimalleolar fracture = medial + lateral + posterior malleolus. Posterior malleolus fragment over 25-33% of the articular surface has traditionally been fixed (controversial per SDL 25 reading). COMLEX: Posterior malleolus fixation also reduces syndesmotic instability. CT scan is essential to measure posterior fragment size before planning surgery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: Ottawa rules and ligamentous patterns that parallel the Danis-Weber ankle fracture classification"},{"id":"sdl9_q24","sdlNumber":9,"batch":1,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of ankle fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A trimalleolar ankle fracture is considered more unstable than a bimalleolar fracture. Which anatomical principle best explains the additional instability conferred by the posterior malleolus fracture?","choices":{"A":"Fracture of the posterior malleolus disrupts the attachment of the posterior tibiofibular ligament at the tibia, eliminating the strongest component of the syndesmotic complex and causing complete lateral ankle instability","B":"Fracture of the posterior malleolus creates a posterior cortical stress riser in the distal tibia, increasing the risk of propagation to a pilon fracture pattern that would extend the articular injury to the central tibial plafond","C":"Fracture of the posterior malleolus exposes the Achilles tendon insertion to abnormal tensile loading, which pulls the calcaneus posteriorly and creates a secondary subtalar joint instability that compounds the ankle mortise disruption","D":"Fracture of the posterior malleolus interrupts the vascular supply to the distal tibia from the posterior tibial artery, increasing the risk of avascular necrosis of the tibial plafond and post-traumatic arthritis from ischemia","E":"Fracture of the posterior malleolus eliminates the posterior osseous buttress of the tibiotalar joint, removing the posterior constraint on the talus and allowing posterior subluxation of the talus under the tibia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The posterior malleolus provides the posterior osseous buttress for the tibiotalar joint. When fractured, the talus can sublux posteriorly, which dramatically reduces the contact area of the tibiotalar joint and accelerates post-traumatic arthritis. Posterior malleolus fragments involving more than 25% to 33% of the tibial articular surface have traditionally been fixed to restore the posterior buttress. The posterior tibiofibular ligament attaches to the posterior malleolus, so its fixation also indirectly reduces syndesmotic instability.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior malleolus = posterior tibial rim = posterior buttress for talus. Fragment over 25-33% of the articular surface = traditional fixation threshold (controversial per SDL 25 reading). COMLEX: Trimalleolar fractures require ORIF of all components when posterior malleolus is large enough to affect stability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: Ottawa rules and ligamentous patterns that parallel the Danis-Weber ankle fracture classification"},{"id":"sdl9_q25","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review primary regions of long bones as it pertains to anatomy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 16-year-old male is injured in a dirt bike crash and sustains a fracture described by the trauma surgeon as involving the \"zone of bone with the richest blood supply, adjacent to the growth plate, where the medullary canal transitions from a narrow isthmus to the flared bony ends of the femur.\" Which anatomical region is being described, and which clinical complication does this region's vascularity predispose to in children?","choices":{"A":"The metaphysis, which is the most vascular zone of long bone due to its rich capillary sinusoidal network, predisposing children to hematogenous osteomyelitis seeding because the sluggish sinusoidal blood flow facilitates bacterial lodgment","B":"The epiphysis, which receives blood from terminal epiphyseal vessels that cross the physis and is the primary site of septic arthritis in neonates when bacterial infection spreads from adjacent metaphyseal vessels into the joint space","C":"The diaphysis, which contains the nutrient foramen through which the nutrient artery enters and branches centrifugally to supply the cortical bone, and is the primary site of Ewing sarcoma arising from intramedullary mesenchymal cells","D":"The periosteum, which contains a dense capillary network in its inner cambium layer that supplies the outer cortex and provides the osteoprogenitor cells responsible for periosteal callus formation during fracture healing","E":"The physis, which is a highly vascularized cartilaginous disc containing the proliferative and hypertrophic zones of chondrocytes and is the weakest structural point in pediatric long bones under mechanical loading"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The metaphysis contains large, thin-walled sinusoids with sluggish blood flow and lacks phagocytic lining cells, making it the primary site for hematogenous osteomyelitis in children. Bacteria seed the metaphysis via the bloodstream. In neonates and infants, metaphyseal vessels cross the physis into the epiphysis, allowing spread to cause septic arthritis. Acute hematogenous osteomyelitis usually occurs in the metaphysis (CDT Orthopedics Ch 12).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osteomyelitis in children: metaphysis is the seeding site. Staph aureus is the most common organism overall; Salmonella in sickle cell disease. Neonates can have epiphyseal or joint extension. COMLEX: Always check for concurrent septic arthritis when diagnosing pediatric osteomyelitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — Bone histology and cellular composition: osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and Haversian system organization"},{"id":"sdl9_q26","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review primary regions of long bones as it pertains to anatomy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A first-year medical student asks about the blood supply to cortical bone during a histology lab session. She wants to understand which vessels supply the deeper (endosteal) part of the compact bone of a long bone diaphysis and which supply its outermost part. What is the correct answer?","choices":{"A":"The periosteal arteries supply the deeper compact bone by penetrating through Volkmann canals to the endosteal surface, while the nutrient artery supplies only the medullary hematopoietic tissue and does not contribute to cortical bone perfusion","B":"The nutrient artery supplies the marrow, spongy bone and deeper compact bone through its medullary branches, while periosteal artery branches supply the outermost compact bone; blood moves from the marrow outward to periosteal veins","C":"The epiphyseal arteries supply the deeper compact bone through their metaphyseal branches, while the periosteal arteries supply the outermost part, and the nutrient artery is a vestigial structure in adults with no functional blood flow","D":"The Haversian canal arterioles supply the deeper compact bone entirely independently of the medullary circulation, receiving their blood from the periosteum without contribution from the nutrient artery or endosteum","E":"Both the inner and outer cortex receive equal contributions from the nutrient artery and periosteal arteries, which anastomose freely within the Volkmann canals such that no regional distinction in cortical blood supply can be identified"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The nutrient artery enters through the nutrient foramen and divides in the medullary cavity, \"supplying the bone marrow, spongy bone, and deeper portions of the compact bone\" (Moore Ch 1), while small branches of the periosteal arteries supply the outer compact bone. Pawlina Ch 8 describes the flow as essentially centrifugal — from the marrow cavity through the bone tissue and out via periosteal veins — with a lesser supply to the outermost compact bone from periosteal arteries. The two textbooks differ on which supply dominates: Pawlina calls the periosteal contribution lesser, whereas Moore says periosteal branches nourish most of the compact bone and that a bone stripped of its periosteum dies. Both agree on the regional pattern tested here. The nutrient artery is neither vestigial nor limited to the marrow, Haversian vessels are reached through Volkmann canals rather than running independently of the medullary circulation, and the supply is regional rather than equal throughout the cortex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cortical blood supply — nutrient artery → marrow, spongy bone and deeper compact bone; periosteal arteries → outermost compact bone; flow is centrifugal (Pawlina Ch 8). Sources differ on dominance: Pawlina calls the periosteal supply lesser, Moore Ch 1 says periosteal branches nourish most of the compact bone. COMLEX: A bone stripped of its periosteum dies (Moore Ch 1), so periosteal stripping during fixation threatens the outer cortex.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — Bone histology and cellular composition: osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and Haversian system organization"},{"id":"sdl9_q27","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review primary regions of long bones as it pertains to anatomy.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 40-year-old cyclist sustains a segmental femoral shaft fracture with significant periosteal stripping noted intraoperatively. The orthopedic surgeon expresses concern about delayed union for the isolated middle fragment. Which characteristic of periosteum explains this clinical concern?","choices":{"A":"The periosteum contains dense nerve fibers that trigger the local inflammatory response critical for initiating osteoblast recruitment, and its stripping prevents the neurogenic pain signal that normally drives early bone repair","B":"The periosteum anchors ligaments and tendons to the bone through Sharpey fibers and provides the mechanical load transfer needed to stimulate piezoelectric osteoblast activation at the fracture site during early healing","C":"The periosteum acts as a mechanical barrier preventing hematoma dispersal from the fracture site, and its disruption allows the fracture hematoma to dissipate before organizing into a fibrocartilaginous bridging callus","D":"The periosteum's inner cambium layer contains osteoprogenitor cells and provides the majority of blood supply for periosteal callus formation, and its stripping deprives the middle segment of the cellular and vascular resources required for healing","E":"The periosteum provides the only route of venous drainage from the diaphyseal cortex through emissary veins, and its stripping causes venous congestion and cortical ischemia that independently delays osteoblast-mediated bone formation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The periosteum has two layers: an outer fibrous layer and an inner cambium layer. The cambium layer contains osteoprogenitor cells that differentiate into osteoblasts during fracture healing and produce periosteal callus. It also contributes to the vascular supply through the periosteal capillary network. Periosteal stripping eliminates this cellular and vascular contribution to healing, explaining the high non-union risk in segmental fractures and open fractures with extensive stripping.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Periosteum = outer fibrous + inner cambium layers. Cambium contains osteoprogenitor cells = periosteal callus. Periosteal stripping = delayed union, especially in segmental fractures. COMLEX: Open fracture debridement must preserve periosteum whenever possible to protect healing capacity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — Bone histology and cellular composition: osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and Haversian system organization"},{"id":"sdl9_q28","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss variables and nomenclature involved in appropriate fracture identification.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old man is hit by a car. AP and lateral radiographs of the thigh show the distal femoral shaft broken into two separate pieces that are widely separated from each other, with no additional fragments. A resident calls the consult a \"comminuted\" fracture. How should the fracture fragments be described?","choices":{"A":"Widely displaced but not comminuted, because comminution means three or more fragments and this fracture has two pieces","B":"Comminuted, because any fracture that separates the bone into unconnected pieces is comminuted regardless of how many fragments are present","C":"Highly comminuted, because wide separation of the fragments implies that small pieces too tiny to see on radiographs have broken free","D":"Compound, because wide displacement of a femoral fracture means the bone has penetrated the skin even if no wound is visible on exam","E":"Segmental, because a two-part shaft fracture is by definition segmental whenever the gap between the fragments exceeds the shaft width"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The fracture management chapter for this SDL defines comminution by the number of fragments: three or more fragments is comminuted, and more than four or five is highly comminuted. It uses a two-piece, widely displaced distal femur fracture as its example of a fracture that is widely displaced but not comminuted. Displacement describes how far apart the fragments are, so it is separate from comminution. Wide separation does not imply hidden fragments, and the description should match the radiograph. Orthopedists avoid the term compound because it is used inconsistently; open versus closed depends on a skin wound communicating with the fracture. A segmental fracture has two fracture levels that isolate a middle fragment, which this two-piece fracture does not have.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Describe fractures by open/closed, bone and location (diaphysis, metaphysis, epiphysis), pattern (transverse, oblique, spiral), comminution (≥3 fragments), displacement, angulation, shortening, rotation, intra- vs extra-articular. COMLEX: Always obtain two orthogonal views including the joints above and below a long-bone fracture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — describing and managing humerus fractures"},{"id":"sdl9_q29","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss variables and nomenclature involved in appropriate fracture identification.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 54-year-old is struck by a vehicle. Imaging shows a tibial fracture described as \"segmental with a 7 cm isolated middle fragment and 50% cortical displacement at both fracture levels.\" Which specific characteristic of this fracture type explains the increased risk of non-union compared to a simple two-fragment tibial fracture?","choices":{"A":"The fracture occurs at two separate levels, doubling the total fracture surface area requiring callus bridging and therefore requiring twice as long a healing time as a single-level fracture with the same amount of fracture displacement","B":"The fracture pattern indicates a torsional mechanism at the proximal level and a bending mechanism at the distal level, creating two biomechanically distinct fracture interfaces that respond differently to the same fixation device","C":"The middle segment retains the nutrient foramen in most tibial segmental fractures, which paradoxically impairs healing by competing with periosteal callus formation and reducing the centrifugal blood flow through the Haversian system","D":"The middle segment has lost both its endosteal and periosteal blood supply at both fracture ends, creating an avascular fragment that must be reperfused through new vessel ingrowth before bone healing can proceed at both fracture sites","E":"The fracture is classified as open by definition due to the extent of displacement, requiring more aggressive antibiotic prophylaxis and debridement than a single-level closed fracture of similar displacement and mechanism"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The isolated middle segment in a segmental fracture loses its blood supply at both its proximal and distal fracture interfaces. Without an intact periosteal or endosteal blood supply, the segment becomes relatively avascular and relies on neovascularization from surrounding soft tissue and from the remaining fibula. This dramatically increases non-union risk, particularly when periosteal stripping accompanies the injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Segmental fracture = isolated middle segment = high non-union risk due to avascularity. Often requires bone grafting. COMLEX: Segmental tibial fractures have highest complications of any tibial fracture pattern. Intramedullary nail spans both fracture levels and provides stability while vascularization occurs.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: Ottawa Ankle Rules and fracture versus ligamentous injury distinction"},{"id":"sdl9_q30","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss variables and nomenclature involved in appropriate fracture identification.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old female military recruit reports progressive anterior shin pain over 4 weeks of training. The pain worsens with activity and is relieved by rest. X-ray shows no fracture. MRI demonstrates periosteal edema and a faint cortical lucency. Which imaging modality best characterizes this injury when radiographs are negative?","choices":{"A":"Repeat plain radiographs at 2 to 3 weeks, which show periosteal reaction and a cortical fracture line once healing begins and are the standard confirmatory test for tibial stress injury","B":"MRI is the gold-standard imaging for stress fractures when plain radiographs are negative, detecting periosteal edema and endosteal signal change weeks before the cortical fracture line becomes visible on X-ray","C":"CT scan is the preferred imaging modality for stress fractures because it best identifies the cortical stress reaction and can quantify the degree of cortical disruption, providing better sensitivity than MRI for early periosteal changes","D":"Bone scintigraphy with technetium-99m is the only validated imaging modality for stress fractures, providing high sensitivity for any osteoblastic activity and distinguishing stress fractures from shin splints based on the focal versus diffuse uptake pattern","E":"Clinical diagnosis alone using the triad of activity-related pain, point tenderness, and response to rest is sufficient to diagnose stress fractures without any imaging, since false-positive imaging findings are common in active patients"},"correct":"B","explanation":"MRI is the most sensitive imaging modality for stress fractures, detecting periosteal edema, endosteal edema, and cortical signal change weeks before X-ray changes appear. Bone scintigraphy is highly sensitive but non-specific. CT can show the fracture line but misses early marrow edema. Repeat radiographs may eventually show periosteal reaction, but they lag weeks behind the injury and are insensitive early. Stress fractures in the anterior tibial cortex are high-risk and may require activity modification or even surgical fixation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Stress fracture = repetitive loading exceeds repair. MRI is gold standard when X-ray negative. High-risk locations requiring surgery: anterior tibial cortex, femoral neck tension side, navicular, 5th metatarsal diaphysis. COMLEX: Female athlete triad increases stress fracture risk: low energy availability, menstrual dysfunction, low bone mineral density.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: Ottawa Ankle Rules and fracture versus ligamentous injury distinction"},{"id":"sdl9_q31","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss differences between adult and pediatric bone stress.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl presents after a fall from a swing. Radiograph shows the radial diaphysis is angulated 25 degrees with the tension cortex clearly fractured and the compression cortex intact. Her parents ask why the bone did not break all the way through. What explanation is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"The periosteum in children is thicker and more firmly attached than in adults, generating enough mechanical resistance to halt fracture propagation before it can reach the compression cortex on the opposite side of the bone","B":"The child has open physes that absorb a portion of the mechanical energy from the fall before it can fully propagate through the radial diaphysis, diverting stress away from the cortex and limiting the fracture to one side","C":"Pediatric bone undergoes faster remodeling than adult bone, allowing the compression cortex to rapidly repair early microfractures as the force is applied, preventing the accumulation of damage required for complete cortical failure","D":"Pediatric bone has a higher collagen-to-mineral ratio and greater plastic deformability than adult bone, allowing the compression cortex to bend beyond its elastic limit without fracturing while the tension cortex fails first","E":"The nutrient foramen in pediatric bone is positioned on the tension cortex, creating a stress concentration point that causes the tension cortex to fail first while the compression cortex on the opposite side remains protected"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The greenstick fracture pattern is unique to pediatric bone because of its high plasticity. The collagen-rich matrix allows considerable bending before failure. During a bending force, the tension side experiences tensile stress and fails first. The compression side bends beyond the elastic limit but does not fracture because the ductile matrix can absorb the compressive energy. This is analogous to bending a green twig — it snaps on the tension side but the fibers on the other side hold together.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Greenstick fracture = tension side fractures, compression side bends. Unique to pediatric bone due to high collagen content. Treat with closed reduction and casting when angulation is unacceptable. COMLEX: Greenstick fractures may need to be completed to achieve adequate reduction in severely angulated cases.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — Osteogenesis imperfecta: defective Type I collagen causing brittle bone and pediatric fracture susceptibility"},{"id":"sdl9_q32","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss differences between adult and pediatric bone stress.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 32-year-old distance runner has a 6-week history of progressively worsening anterior tibial pain. MRI shows periosteal edema with a partial cortical fracture of the anterior tibial diaphysis. The sports medicine physician explains that this stress fracture type is classified as high-risk. What makes anterior tibial cortex stress fractures particularly dangerous compared to posteromedial stress reactions?","choices":{"A":"The anterior tibial cortex has a thinner periosteal covering than the posterior cortex, reducing the osteoprogenitor cell density available for healing and causing the fracture to progress to cortical nonunion more rapidly","B":"The anterior tibial cortex is more metabolically active than the posterior cortex due to its proximity to the tibial nutrient foramen, generating more osteoclastic activity that outpaces osteoblastic repair during periods of high training load","C":"The anterior tibial cortex is subjected to higher shear forces than the posteromedial cortex during running gait because the anterior tibialis muscle origin inserts directly into this cortex and generates repetitive tensile avulsion forces","D":"The anterior tibial cortex is the most vascular region of the tibia due to the periosteal network from the anterior tibial artery, and the resulting hyperemia paradoxically accelerates osteoclastic resorption faster than osteoblasts can repair","E":"The anterior tibial cortex is under tension during weight bearing, which inhibits fracture healing and predisposes to propagation to a complete transverse fracture known as the dreaded black line fracture"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The anterior tibial cortex is under tensile stress during the loading phase of gait. Tension-side stress fractures heal poorly because the fracture gap is under opening rather than compressive forces. Healing requires the fracture to be under compression. The classic \"dreaded black line\" on lateral X-ray represents a chronic tension-side stress fracture. These fractures often require surgical fixation with an intramedullary nail rather than conservative management.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anterior tibial stress fracture = high risk = tension side = dreaded black line on lateral X-ray. Often needs IMN. Contrast with posteromedial tibial stress reaction from MTSS: low risk, responds to rest. COMLEX: Female athlete triad predisposes to stress fractures — screen with DEXA scan.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — Osteogenesis imperfecta: defective Type I collagen causing brittle bone and pediatric fracture susceptibility"},{"id":"sdl9_q33","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss differences between adult and pediatric bone stress.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An orthopedic surgeon tells residents that a 6-year-old child's femur fracture with 20-degree angular malunion can be observed rather than operatively corrected, because significant spontaneous remodeling is expected over the next 2 to 3 years. Which combination of factors is most predictive of successful remodeling in this clinical scenario?","choices":{"A":"Young age with significant remaining growth, fracture near a physis contributing to longitudinal growth, and deformity in the plane of motion of the adjacent hip or knee joint — all three factors maximize remodeling potential","B":"Large periosteal callus formation at the fracture site combined with early mobilization, which generates mechanical stimulus for Wolff's law-driven remodeling, independently correcting angular deformities through adaptive bone resorption and deposition","C":"High serum levels of insulin-like growth factor 1 and growth hormone in young children, which drive systemic bone formation at all sites including the fracture and are sufficient alone to correct angular deformities through accelerated remodeling","D":"Adequate calcium and vitamin D intake during the healing period combined with early weight bearing, which stimulates piezoelectric osteoblast activation throughout the healing bone and provides the mineral substrate for remodeling the malunited callus","E":"The severity of the initial angular deformity exceeding 20 degrees, which paradoxically stimulates greater Wolff's law-mediated remodeling response than smaller deformities through the more pronounced asymmetric mechanical loading it creates"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Three factors predict successful remodeling: young age with substantial remaining growth, fracture proximity to the physis contributing to longitudinal growth, and deformity in the plane of motion of the adjacent joint. Physeal growth corrects deformity as the bone elongates. Rotational deformities do not remodel reliably. The further the fracture from the dominant physis, the less remodeling occurs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pediatric fracture remodeling predictors: young age, physeal proximity, plane of joint motion (CDT Orthopedics Ch 12). Rotation does NOT remodel. Valgus/varus in the coronal plane does. COMLEX: Acceptable angulation varies by age and location — more angulation accepted in younger children, less near wrist than hip.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — Osteogenesis imperfecta: defective Type I collagen causing brittle bone and pediatric fracture susceptibility"},{"id":"sdl9_q34","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the Salter-Harris System for classification of pediatric fractures near a growth plate.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Two 10-year-old boys have physeal fractures from separate falls: one through the distal femoral physis and one through the distal radial physis. Both are reduced and heal. When counseling families about the long-term risk of growth disturbance after a lower extremity physeal injury, which statement is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Upper and lower extremity physeal injuries carry the same risk, because growth arrest depends on Salter-Harris type and not on the physis involved","B":"The distal radial physis is the most common site of post-traumatic arrest, and it causes the largest functional limb length discrepancy","C":"Growth disturbance after physeal injury improves as abnormal growth continues, because the remaining physis remodels the bone toward normal","D":"Distal femoral physeal injuries have a high incidence of growth arrest and a greater effect on leg length, worsening as growth continues","E":"Lower limb length differences of any size require epiphysiodesis, because a shoe lift cannot compensate for discrepancy after physeal arrest"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The pediatric orthopedics chapter for this SDL notes that injuries to the physis worsen as abnormal growth continues, especially near rapidly growing areas such as the knee, and that distal femoral physeal injuries have a high incidence of growth arrest and a greater effect on leg length. Post-traumatic physeal arrest occurs most commonly after injury in the distal medial tibia. Salter-Harris type matters, but location also matters, so the two injuries do not carry equal risk. Upper extremity length differences are usually only cosmetic. Remodeling corrects many malunions, but permanent physeal damage worsens with growth rather than improving. Lower extremity discrepancies greater than 3 cm may limit function and need treatment, while lesser discrepancies can be managed with a shoe lift.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Physis = growth plate between metaphysis and epiphysis; physeal damage → bony bar → growth arrest (leg length discrepancy or angular deformity). The knee physes (distal femur, proximal tibia) grow fastest. COMLEX: Follow physeal fractures until growth is shown to continue.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 6 — Salter-Harris classification of pediatric physeal fractures"},{"id":"sdl9_q35","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the Salter-Harris System for classification of pediatric fractures near a growth plate.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 9-year-old male gymnast injures his wrist during vault training. Initial radiograph appears normal with only mild physeal widening. Six months later, the affected forearm is shorter than the contralateral side with progressive radial deviation deformity. What was the most likely initial injury, and why was it radiographically occult?","choices":{"A":"Salter-Harris Type III, which produces a fracture line visible on initial radiograph extending from the physis into the epiphysis, and the deformity developed because the intra-articular reduction was inadequate despite operative fixation","B":"Salter-Harris Type II, which produces a visible metaphyseal corner fragment at the time of injury, and the subsequent growth arrest indicates that the germinal layer was injured despite the favorable fracture pattern","C":"An occult torus fracture of the distal radial metaphysis, which buckles the dorsal cortex without a discrete fracture line, and the growth deformity resulted from inappropriate immobilization in a position that compressed the physis","D":"A ligamentous injury to the distal radioulnar joint causing chronic instability, which progressively transferred abnormal stress to the distal radial physis and caused asymmetric physeal closure from mechanical overload","E":"Salter-Harris Type V, an axial compression injury that crushes the germinal chondrocyte layer of the physis without producing a visible fracture line, causing physeal arrest and subsequent angular deformity as the ulna continues to grow"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Salter-Harris Type V is a crush injury to the physis. The compressive force damages the chondrocyte columns without producing a discrete fracture line visible on plain radiograph. It is often seen in sports requiring repetitive axial loading of the physis, such as gymnastics. The only finding may be subtle physeal widening acutely. Growth arrest is confirmed months later when leg or arm length discrepancy or angular deformity becomes apparent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Type V = axial crush = normal X-ray = delayed diagnosis = worst growth prognosis. Classic scenario: gymnast with wrist pain, normal X-ray, later develops premature physeal arrest. COMLEX: Treat with restriction from gymnastics and close follow-up with contralateral comparison films.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 6 — Salter-Harris classification and greenstick fractures in pediatric patients"},{"id":"sdl9_q36","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the Salter-Harris System for classification of pediatric fractures near a growth plate.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pediatric orthopedic surgeon is teaching residents using the mnemonic SALTR for the Salter-Harris classification. She assigns each letter to a fracture type in ascending order of severity. What structure does the letter \"L\" represent, and which Salter-Harris type does it correspond to?","choices":{"A":"\"L\" represents the Ligamentous attachments to the periosteum, corresponding to Salter-Harris Type II, in which the periosteal ligaments are avulsed along with the metaphyseal Thurston-Holland corner fragment","B":"\"L\" represents Longitudinal crack propagation, corresponding to Salter-Harris Type I, in which the fracture propagates along the full length of the physis from one side of the bone to the other without entering metaphysis or epiphysis","C":"\"L\" represents the Lower epiphysis, corresponding to Salter-Harris Type III, in which the fracture line passes through the physis and exits downward through the epiphysis into the joint space","D":"\"L\" represents the Loss of growth potential, corresponding to Salter-Harris Type V, in which the physis is directly crushed and all potential for further longitudinal growth is immediately eliminated at the time of injury","E":"\"L\" represents the Lateral displacement of the epiphysis, corresponding to Salter-Harris Type I, in which the epiphysis slides laterally off the physis without producing any bony fragment on either the metaphyseal or epiphyseal side"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The SALTR mnemonic corresponds to: S = Slipped/through physis alone (Type I), A = Above the physis through the metaphysis (Type II), L = Lower through the epiphysis (Type III), T = Through both metaphysis and epiphysis (Type IV), R = Rammed/crushed physis (Type V). Each letter reflects the key structural involvement and direction of fracture propagation in ascending severity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SALTR mnemonic: Slipped, Above, Lower, Through, Rammed. Types I-II = favorable. Types III-IV = moderate, require anatomic reduction. Type V = worst prognosis. COMLEX: Use this mnemonic reliably — Salter-Harris classification is a boards staple for both USMLE and COMLEX.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 6 — Salter-Harris classification and greenstick fractures in pediatric patients"},{"id":"sdl9_q37","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman is struck by a car bumper at knee level. CT scan reveals lateral tibial plateau fracture with 9 mm articular depression. The surgeon discusses the Schatzker classification. Which Schatzker type is a lateral split-depression fracture, and what is the surgical threshold for articular depression?","choices":{"A":"Schatzker Type I is a pure lateral tibial plateau split fracture without depression, and surgical fixation is indicated for this type when articular step-off exceeds 10 mm, with conservative management acceptable below that threshold","B":"Schatzker Type IV is a medial tibial plateau fracture from a varus mechanism, and surgical fixation is indicated for any displacement because medial plateau fractures are associated with higher-energy mechanisms and peroneal nerve injury","C":"Schatzker Type VI is a bicondylar tibial plateau fracture with metaphyseal-diaphyseal dissociation, and the articular depression threshold for surgery is 5 mm, with any amount of cortical comminution serving as an independent surgical indication","D":"Schatzker Type III is a pure lateral depression fracture without a split component, and conservative management is the standard approach regardless of the degree of articular depression given the intact cortical rim provides inherent stability","E":"Schatzker Type II is a combined split-depression fracture of the lateral tibial plateau, and surgical reduction is generally indicated when articular depression exceeds 3 mm to prevent progressive valgus deformity and lateral compartment arthritis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Schatzker Type II is a split-depression fracture of the lateral tibial plateau, the most common pattern in older patients with osteoporotic bone. The articular surface is both split and depressed. The threshold for surgical intervention is depression greater than 3 mm, as this degree of articular incongruity leads to premature lateral compartment arthritis. Schatzker I is a pure split; Type III is pure depression; Type VI involves both plateaus with metaphyseal separation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Schatzker II = split-depression = most common tibial plateau fracture overall. Surgical threshold: over 3 mm articular depression. COMLEX: Associated injuries: popliteal artery injury with high Schatzker types. Check ABI and pulses. Compartment syndrome risk is high with bicondylar fractures.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — Knee fractures: tibial plateau and patellar fractures within the knee pain diagnostic pathway"},{"id":"sdl9_q38","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with osteoporosis and diabetes falls from a step and sustains a proximal tibial plateau fracture. She is placed in a splint pending surgical consultation. Which complication listed in the course presentation of this fracture requires close monitoring in the hours after injury?","choices":{"A":"Fat embolism syndrome, because marrow fat from the plateau enters the venous circulation and lodges in the lung within the first hour","B":"Acute compartment syndrome of the leg, which can develop after a proximal tibia fracture along with ligament and meniscal injuries","C":"Avascular necrosis of the tibial plateau, because the fracture interrupts the single nutrient vessel supplying the proximal epiphysis","D":"Common peroneal nerve palsy from the splint, because pressure at the medial tibial condyle compresses the nerve as it crosses","E":"Septic arthritis, because blood within the knee joint after a closed plateau fracture becomes infected in the first few hours"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course lists pain, swelling, ligamentous injury, meniscal injury, and acute compartment syndrome in the presentation of proximal tibial (plateau) fractures, which are more common than midshaft or distal tibial fractures and are more frequent in women over 51 with comorbidities and osteoporosis. Swelling in the leg compartments after a proximal tibia fracture can raise pressure enough to cause compartment syndrome, so pain out of proportion and pain with passive stretch must be watched for. Fat embolism is not among the complications the course lists for this fracture. Avascular necrosis of the plateau is not described. The common peroneal nerve runs at the lateral fibular neck, not the medial tibial condyle. A closed fracture with hemarthrosis is not septic arthritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Compartment syndrome: pain out of proportion, pain with passive stretch, tense compartment; late signs paresthesia, pallor, pulselessness; delta pressure (diastolic − compartment) ≤30 mm Hg → fasciotomy. COMLEX: Initial plateau fracture care = PRICE, immobilization, surgical consult.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 6 — compartment syndrome after tibial shaft fracture"},{"id":"sdl9_q39","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of knee fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old osteoporotic woman falls from standing and sustains a lateral tibial plateau fracture with 10 mm of articular depression on CT scan. Orthopedic surgery plans for open reduction and internal fixation. What specific intraoperative challenge is unique to osteoporotic bone in this fracture type?","choices":{"A":"Osteoporotic bone cannot be imaged adequately with standard CT protocols at the time of surgery, requiring intraoperative fluoroscopy alone to guide reduction, which is less accurate than preoperative CT planning for achieving anatomic articular reduction","B":"Osteoporotic bone heals more slowly than normal bone due to reduced osteoblast density, requiring double the standard immobilization period after fixation and restricting weight bearing for 6 months rather than the usual 3 months","C":"Osteoporotic bone has insufficient structural strength to hold fixation screws and support a subchondral raft beneath the reduced articular surface, requiring bone graft or calcium phosphate substitute to fill the metaphyseal void after elevation","D":"Osteoporotic bone generates insufficient callus around implants due to low bone turnover, requiring prophylactic bisphosphonate therapy to be started immediately after surgery to prevent fixation failure and implant migration","E":"Osteoporotic bone cannot sustain the traction forces required for closed reduction of the articular surface, making open surgical elevation mandatory regardless of the degree of depression, even for fractures with less than 3 mm of articular depression"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When a depressed tibial plateau fragment is elevated in osteoporotic bone, the metaphyseal void left beneath the elevated articular surface has insufficient strength to resist subsidence. This void must be filled with autograft, allograft, or calcium phosphate bone substitute to maintain reduction. Failure to address this void leads to loss of reduction and articular re-depression under weight bearing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tibial plateau elevation creates metaphyseal void in osteoporotic bone — requires bone graft or substitute. Schatzker II in elderly = high risk for subsidence. COMLEX: Calcium phosphate cements are commonly used as synthetic bone substitutes in elderly osteoporotic plateau fractures.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — Knee fractures: tibial plateau and patellar fractures within the knee pain diagnostic pathway"},{"id":"sdl9_q40","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of tibial shaft fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 27-year-old sustains a closed tibial shaft fracture. Four hours after splinting, the nurse reports that the patient is screaming in pain despite IV morphine. On examination, the anterior compartment is tense and the patient has paresthesias in the first dorsal web space. Compartment pressure is measured at 45 mmHg, and blood pressure is 130/72 mmHg. What is the delta-pressure and what is the appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"The delta-pressure is 85 mmHg (systolic minus compartment pressure), well above the 30 mmHg threshold, so the compartment is adequately perfused and repeat pressure monitoring every 30 minutes is appropriate","B":"The delta-pressure is about 45 mmHg (mean arterial minus compartment pressure), above the 30 mmHg threshold, confirming that fasciotomy is not yet indicated and that observation with pain control is appropriate","C":"The absolute compartment pressure of 45 mmHg is below the 50 mmHg cutoff, so fasciotomy is not indicated and the splint should be bivalved with reassessment of the leg in 2 hours","D":"The delta-pressure of 27 mmHg indicates adequate perfusion, so the severe pain is attributed to opioid tolerance requiring patient-controlled analgesia and adjunct non-opioid medications before surgery","E":"The delta-pressure is 72 − 45 = 27 mmHg, at or below the 30 mmHg threshold, which together with the clinical findings confirms compartment syndrome; emergent four-compartment fasciotomy is required"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Delta pressure = diastolic blood pressure minus compartment pressure: 72 − 45 = 27 mmHg. A delta-P at or below 30 mmHg supports fasciotomy, and this patient also has the clinical picture of compartment syndrome: pain out of proportion despite morphine, a tense anterior compartment, and first dorsal web space paresthesias (deep peroneal nerve). Emergent four-compartment fasciotomy is required, and when the clinical signs are unequivocal, pressure measurement should never delay surgery. Using systolic or mean arterial pressure overestimates perfusion, a delta-P of 27 mmHg is not adequate perfusion, an absolute-pressure cutoff of 50 mmHg is too high, and attributing the pain to opioid tolerance risks missing an evolving compartment syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Delta-P = diastolic BP minus compartment pressure. Fasciotomy threshold: delta-P at or below 30 mmHg. Clinical signs precede pressure changes — never delay fasciotomy for pressure measurement when the exam is diagnostic. COMLEX: Four leg compartments: anterior, lateral, deep posterior, superficial posterior.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — adult versus pediatric bone stress, the overuse end of the tibial injury spectrum (MTSS, stress fracture)"},{"id":"sdl9_q41","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of tibial shaft fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An 18-month-old is brought to the emergency department by her parents after they noticed she stopped walking. X-ray shows a non-displaced spiral lucency of the mid-tibial diaphysis. Parents report no witnessed trauma. What is the diagnosis, and what is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"A non-accidental injury fracture pattern requiring mandatory child protective services report, because spiral tibial fractures in toddlers are pathognomonic for child abuse and cannot result from accidental mechanisms","B":"A pathologic fracture through a unicameral bone cyst requiring urgent orthopaedic referral and biopsy to rule out malignancy, since non-displaced spiral fractures in toddlers without witnessed trauma are suspicious for underlying bone disease","C":"A toddler's fracture, a non-displaced spiral tibial shaft fracture from a minor twisting mechanism in a young child just learning to walk, treated with simple immobilization with expected full recovery","D":"A Salter-Harris Type I physeal fracture of the distal tibia rather than a diaphyseal fracture, and it requires reduction followed by casting to prevent physeal arrest given the growth plate involvement in this age group","E":"A stress fracture from excessive parental pressure for early ambulation requiring behavioral counseling and reduced weight-bearing activity, since 18-month-old children should not yet be expected to walk and premature ambulation overloads the tibial cortex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Toddler's fracture is a classic diagnosis in young children just learning to walk (CDT Orthopedics Ch 12; Nelson notes it especially in children younger than 6 years) who sustain a minor twisting injury. The fracture is characteristically non-displaced and spiral, occurring in the tibial diaphysis. It presents with refusal to bear weight or limp. The mechanism is often minor and unwitnessed. Treatment is simple immobilization. It is NOT pathognomonic for abuse, though non-accidental injury must always be in the differential.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Toddler's fracture = non-displaced spiral tibial fracture in a child learning to walk. Often unwitnessed, benign minor mechanism. Simple immobilization. Not pathognomonic for abuse — evaluate clinical context. COMLEX: Classic test question: distinguish toddler's fracture from abuse. Key factors: age, fracture pattern, multiple fractures, delayed presentation, inconsistent history.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — adult versus pediatric bone stress, the overuse end of the tibial injury spectrum (MTSS, stress fracture)"},{"id":"sdl9_q42","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of tibial shaft fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 36-year-old sustains a Gustilo Type IIIB open tibial shaft fracture in a motorcycle crash with a 5 cm wound and periosteal stripping. What is the most critical time-sensitive intervention, and what is the recommended sequence of management?","choices":{"A":"Emergency wound closure by the plastic surgery team is the most time-critical intervention, as the 6-hour window for primary closure determines whether the wound can be closed or requires a rotational flap with significantly higher complication risk","B":"Intravenous antibiotics begun immediately after injury are the most time-critical intervention, followed by emergent irrigation and debridement in the operating room, temporary external fixation, and delayed wound closure after serial debridement","C":"Definitive intramedullary nailing at the index procedure is the priority to achieve mechanical stability before wound colonization occurs, since the first 6 hours represent the golden window for definitive fixation in contaminated open fractures","D":"CT angiography of the affected extremity is the first priority to exclude popliteal artery injury before any other intervention, as vascular repair must precede both wound management and fracture stabilization in all high-energy tibial fractures","E":"Wound culture and sensitivity testing before antibiotic administration ensures that prophylaxis is targeted to the specific organisms present in the contaminating environment, avoiding unnecessary broad-spectrum antibiotic use and resistance selection"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The most time-critical intervention in open fracture management is IV antibiotics begun immediately to prevent osteomyelitis (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 2). Cefazolin covers gram-positive organisms for Gustilo Types I and II. Gram-negative coverage is added for Type III. Management sequence: antibiotics in ED, then emergent OR debridement, then temporary external fixation, then serial debridement every 48-72 hours until clean, then soft tissue reconstruction, then definitive fixation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Open fracture antibiotics begun immediately — the single most important intervention. Gustilo IIIB requires plastic surgery for soft tissue coverage. Do NOT close primarily. Serial debridements every 48-72 hours. COMLEX: Antibiotics for open fractures: cefazolin for Type I-II; add aminoglycoside for Type III; add penicillin G for farm contamination.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — adult versus pediatric bone stress, the overuse end of the tibial injury spectrum (MTSS, stress fracture)"},{"id":"sdl9_q43","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of fibular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old soccer player pronates his ankle forcefully. In the emergency department he has medial ankle tenderness and ecchymosis, but ankle X-rays show only mild mortise widening without a fibular fracture. On palpation, he has proximal fibular tenderness. What is the full injury diagnosis, and what additional imaging is mandatory?","choices":{"A":"A Maisonneuve fracture with syndesmotic disruption and deltoid ligament injury; full-length tibia-fibula radiographs are mandatory to identify the proximal fibular fracture that will be missed on ankle films alone","B":"An isolated deltoid ligament sprain with ankle mortise stress injury; no additional imaging beyond standard ankle views is needed since the mortise widening is mild and the patient should be treated with immobilization and gradual weight bearing","C":"A proximal tibiofibular joint dislocation from a torsional mechanism; knee radiographs are mandatory to evaluate the proximal tibiofibular articulation and rule out concurrent lateral compartment injury at the knee","D":"A stress fracture of the fibular shaft from chronic repetitive soccer training that was acutely displaced by the pronation mechanism; MRI of the entire fibula is mandatory to determine the extent of stress reaction before planning treatment","E":"A Lisfranc midfoot injury with tarsometatarsal disruption that referred pain proximally; weight-bearing foot radiographs are mandatory to evaluate the Lisfranc joint, and the proximal fibular tenderness represents a referred pain pattern"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Maisonneuve fracture results from a pronation-external rotation mechanism. The medial ankle structures fail first, then the force transmits proximally along the interosseous membrane, producing a proximal fibular fracture. The ankle appears relatively normal on standard views, but mortise widening indicates instability. Full-length tibia-fibula films are mandatory. This injury requires surgical fixation including syndesmotic stabilization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Maisonneuve = proximal fibula fracture from ankle mechanism. Mortise widening without distal fibular fracture = get full leg films. Associated with deltoid ligament rupture. Unstable ankle requiring syndesmotic screw fixation. COMLEX: Classic boards pitfall: treat the ankle, miss the proximal fibula.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injuries: common peroneal nerve vulnerability at the fibular neck"},{"id":"sdl9_q44","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of fibular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old female sustains a distal fibular fracture at the level of the ankle mortise. On clinical examination, the medial ankle is tender. Stress examination under fluoroscopy shows widening of the medial clear space with external rotation force. What is the Danis-Weber classification and what is the management implication of the stress finding?","choices":{"A":"Weber A fracture below the syndesmosis; the medial clear space widening indicates associated deltoid ligament sprain rather than rupture, and can be managed non-operatively since below-syndesmosis fractures are inherently stable regardless of ligamentous tenderness","B":"Weber C fracture above the syndesmosis; syndesmotic disruption is confirmed by the proximal fibular fracture level, and the stress examination finding of medial widening is expected for this fracture type and does not add additional management information","C":"Weber B fracture at the syndesmotic level; the medial clear space widening on stress examination indicates deltoid ligament rupture, converting this from a potentially stable to a definitively unstable ankle fracture requiring surgical fixation","D":"A bimalleolar equivalent fracture by definition; any fibular fracture with medial ankle tenderness must be treated as a bimalleolar fracture with immediate surgical fixation of the fibula and medial ankle reconstruction without further stress testing","E":"An unstable fracture-dislocation of the ankle; the mortise widening indicates complete dislocation of the talus, and emergent closed reduction with sedation must be performed immediately before any further diagnostic workup or imaging"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A distal fibular fracture at the level of the joint line is Danis-Weber type B. The course reading (Current Diagnosis & Treatment in Orthopedics Ch 2, SDL 25) notes that with type B, if the medial malleolus is intact there can be a tear of the deltoid ligament, and that a lateral malleolus fracture with complete deltoid rupture is considered bimalleolar on a functional basis. Medial clear space widening under stress shows the talus shifting laterally because the deltoid no longer restrains it — a second break in the ankle ring (SDL 9 ring-scheme slide) — so the mortise is unstable; the same reading notes that a lateral talar shift of as little as 1 mm decreases tibiotalar contact by 40%, which is why the fracture is fixed surgically. Type A is below the joint line and type C above the syndesmosis; medial tenderness alone does not establish a functional bimalleolar injury without the stress finding; and stress widening is not a talar dislocation needing emergent reduction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Weber B = at the syndesmosis = variable stability; medial clear space widening on stress means deltoid rupture. COMLEX: Lateral malleolus fracture plus complete deltoid rupture is a functional bimalleolar fracture (course SDL 25 reading) and is unstable; 1 mm of lateral talar shift reduces tibiotalar contact by 40%.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injuries: common peroneal nerve vulnerability at the fibular neck"},{"id":"sdl9_q45","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of fibular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old male football player is struck on the lateral proximal leg just below the knee. He immediately develops foot drop with weakness of ankle dorsiflexion and toe extension, and reports numbness over the dorsum of the foot and the first web space. Which nerve is injured, at what specific anatomical location, and what is the expected prognosis?","choices":{"A":"The deep peroneal nerve at the anterior ankle retinaculum, where it passes under the extensor retinaculum and can be compressed by a tight boot or direct blow, producing dorsal foot numbness and toe extension weakness without complete foot drop","B":"The common peroneal nerve at the fibular neck, where it wraps around the posterolateral fibular head in a superficial location that makes it vulnerable to direct compression and traction injury, with variable recovery depending on severity of axonal injury","C":"The superficial peroneal nerve at the crural fascia in the distal lateral leg, where it perforates the deep fascia and is vulnerable to injury, producing only sensory loss over the dorsolateral foot without any motor weakness or foot drop","D":"The tibial nerve at the popliteal fossa, where it travels with the popliteal artery and can be injured by posterior knee dislocation, producing plantarflexion weakness and plantar foot numbness rather than the dorsiflexion weakness seen here","E":"The saphenous nerve at the medial knee, where it exits the adductor canal and descends along the medial leg, producing purely sensory deficits along the medial leg and foot without any motor involvement or foot drop"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The common peroneal nerve wraps around the fibular neck just below the lateral knee and is covered only by skin and subcutaneous fat at that level. It is the most commonly injured nerve in the lower extremity, highly vulnerable to direct trauma, compression, or fracture at the fibular head. Injury produces foot drop from tibialis anterior paralysis and sensory loss over the dorsum of the foot including the first web space. Recovery depends on nerve continuity — neuropraxia recovers in weeks to months; axonotmesis may take months to a year.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Common peroneal nerve at fibular neck = foot drop. Most commonly injured lower extremity nerve. Causes: direct blow, fibular head fracture, tight cast, prolonged leg crossing. First web space numbness is pathognomonic. COMLEX: Also injured during fibular head fracture reduction — protect the nerve during all proximal fibular procedures.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 8 — Lower extremity nerve injuries: common peroneal nerve vulnerability at the fibular neck"},{"id":"sdl9_q46","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of ankle fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 40-year-old man has a displaced intra-articular fracture of the distal tibia at the ankle, and a 40-year-old woman has a displaced intra-articular fracture of the acetabulum. Both heal with a small residual step in the joint surface. According to the course reading, why is the ankle more likely than the hip to develop post-traumatic arthritis from the same degree of incongruity?","choices":{"A":"The ankle is non-weight-bearing in normal gait, so its thin cartilage never adapts to load and wears out quickly once the surface is uneven","B":"The ankle cartilage has its own blood supply that is torn by the fracture, whereas hip cartilage is fed by synovial fluid and keeps living","C":"The ankle is weight-bearing with motion limited to one plane, whereas the hip is a universal joint that shifts to spread abnormal forces","D":"The ankle has no capsule or ligaments, so even a small step lets the talus subluxate freely while the hip is held by a thick capsule","E":"The ankle fractures always involve the physis, which stops growth and creates a progressive deformity that the adult hip does not develop"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The fracture management reading explains that intra-articular fractures that change the shape of the joint surface accelerate cartilage wear, and that the ankle and knee are especially susceptible because they are weight-bearing joints with motion limited to a single flexion-extension plane. The hip is also weight-bearing, but it is a universal joint that can move in any plane, so it can shift its position and spread abnormal forces over its surface. The reading adds that fractures are the leading cause of arthritis in the ankle. The ankle is weight-bearing, not non-weight-bearing. Articular cartilage has no blood supply in either joint. The ankle has a capsule and strong ligaments. These are adult injuries, so physeal growth is not a factor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fractures cause arthritis by chondrocyte death, angular malunion loading adjacent joints, intra-articular incongruity, and denser, less elastic healed bone. COMLEX: Displaced intra-articular ankle fractures need anatomic reduction; even 1 mm of talar shift sharply cuts contact area.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 8 — talar shift and loss of tibiotalar contact area"},{"id":"sdl9_q47","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of ankle fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman slips on ice and sustains a bimalleolar ankle fracture. Radiograph shows 3 mm lateral talar shift within the ankle mortise. An orthopedic resident asks the attending why this small amount of displacement is clinically significant. What is the physiological explanation?","choices":{"A":"Lateral talar shift stretches the deltoid ligament beyond its recoverable elastic range, causing permanent elongation that will prevent the ankle from returning to its stable weight-bearing position after fracture healing is complete","B":"Even 1 mm of lateral talar shift reduces tibiotalar contact area by about 40 percent, and more shift reduces it further, sharply raising peak contact stress on the remaining cartilage and predicting early post-traumatic arthritis","C":"Lateral talar shift by 3 mm indicates that the fibula is shortened by the same amount, and fibular shortening of 3 mm is the threshold above which the ankle develops a fixed valgus deformity that cannot be corrected by cast immobilization alone","D":"Lateral talar shift represents subtalar joint subluxation rather than tibiotalar shift, and the subtalar joint is more sensitive to small displacements than the tibiotalar joint because of its complex three-dimensional motion requirements","E":"Lateral talar shift indicates complete rupture of all four ankle ligament complexes simultaneously, which is the threshold injury required to produce even 1 mm of talar displacement under physiologic loading conditions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The tibiotalar joint has a small articular surface area compared to the hip or knee. Lateral talar shift dramatically reduces this already-limited contact area. One millimeter of lateral shift reduces tibiotalar contact by about 40% (course reading), and larger shifts reduce it further. This creates focal stress concentration on the remaining articular cartilage, accelerating chondral wear and post-traumatic arthritis. This biomechanical reality is the primary justification for anatomic surgical reduction of ankle fractures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lateral talar shift 1 mm = ~40% contact area loss; more shift = more loss. Even subtle shift = dramatically increased arthritis risk. COMLEX: This is why ankle fractures with mortise disruption require anatomic ORIF — the tibiotalar joint cannot tolerate any incongruity without long-term consequences.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: ligamentous injury spectrum and Ottawa Ankle Rules guiding fracture workup"},{"id":"sdl9_q48","sdlNumber":9,"batch":2,"objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of ankle fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old sustains a trimalleolar ankle fracture. CT scan shows the posterior malleolus fragment involves 30 percent of the articular surface. The orthopedic surgeon decides to fix all three malleoli. What rationale justifies fixing the posterior malleolus specifically, beyond achieving articular reduction?","choices":{"A":"Fixation of the posterior malleolus indirectly stabilizes the distal tibiofibular syndesmosis because the posterior inferior tibiofibular ligament attaches to the posterior malleolus, and reducing the fragment anatomically restores syndesmotic integrity without a separate syndesmotic screw","B":"Fixation of the posterior malleolus prevents Achilles tendon avulsion from its calcaneal insertion by reducing the posterior tibial cortex, which transmits tensile forces from the triceps surae through the posterior ankle capsule to the posterior malleolus","C":"Fixation of the posterior malleolus is required in all trimalleolar fractures regardless of fragment size because any posterior bony fragment creates a mechanical block to ankle dorsiflexion that prevents return to normal gait without operative reduction","D":"Fixation of the posterior malleolus prevents propagation of the fracture anteriorly into the tibial plafond under weight-bearing stress because the unfixed posterior fragment acts as a stress riser for pilon-type fracture extension in the early post-operative period","E":"Fixation of the posterior malleolus is necessary to reattach the posterior tibiotalar ligament at its tibial origin, since ligamentous avulsions at the posterior malleolus heal inadequately without bony fixation due to the poor vascularity of the posterior ankle capsule"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The posterior inferior tibiofibular ligament, a component of the syndesmotic complex, attaches to the posterior malleolus. When the posterior malleolus fragment is anatomically reduced and fixed, this ligament is restored to its normal length and tension, which substantially stabilizes the distal tibiofibular syndesmosis. This is one of the main reasons to fix posterior malleolus fragments over 25% to 33% (a traditional threshold the SDL 25 reading calls controversial) — it reduces or eliminates the need for a separate syndesmotic screw.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Posterior inferior tibiofibular ligament attaches to posterior malleolus. Fixing the posterior malleolus indirectly stabilizes the syndesmosis. Fragment over 25-33% of the articular surface = traditional fixation threshold (controversial per SDL 25 reading). COMLEX: This is a frequently tested syndesmotic anatomy point — the syndesmotic complex includes the AITFL, PITFL, and interosseous membrane.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — Ankle sprain: ligamentous injury spectrum and Ottawa Ankle Rules guiding fracture workup"}],"10":[{"id":"sdl10_q01","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of radial head subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 2-year-old girl is brought to urgent care after her father pulled her arm to keep her from running into traffic. She now holds her left arm adducted and refuses to move it. Which of the following best describes the mechanism that produces radial head subluxation?","choices":{"A":"Forced supination of the forearm causes the radial head to displace anteriorly against the capitellum, rupturing the annular ligament and producing a stable partial dislocation that requires reduction under procedural sedation","B":"A fall onto an outstretched hand transfers axial load up the radius, compressing the radial head against the capitellum and producing a buckle-type physeal injury at the proximal radial growth plate in young children","C":"Axial traction on a pronated, extended forearm causes the annular ligament to slip distally over the radial head and become interposed between the radial head and capitellum in the radiohumeral joint, producing the subluxation","D":"Valgus stress applied to the elbow during a throwing motion causes the radial head to rotate excessively within the annular ligament, producing microavulsions at the ligament proximal attachment to the lateral ulna","E":"Hyperextension of the elbow from a swinging lift causes the olecranon to lever against the posterior humerus, driving the radial head anteriorly through the annular ligament and producing a complete anterior radial head dislocation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Radial head subluxation occurs when axial traction is applied to a pronated, extended forearm. The annular ligament, which holds the radial head in place, slips distally over the radial head and becomes interposed between the radial head and capitellum. This is the classic mechanism of nursemaid's elbow, typically occurring when a caregiver pulls or swings a young child by the arm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic mechanism = axial traction on pronated extended forearm causes annular ligament to slip over radial head. Age 1-4, F > M, left > right. Reduction: supination with flexion produces a palpable click. COMLEX: No imaging needed unless age >5 or atypical mechanism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — Elbow joint dislocations: reduction and management of pediatric elbow joint injuries"},{"id":"sdl10_q02","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of radial head subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pediatric emergency physician notes that nursemaid's elbow accounts for a large volume of pediatric elbow injuries. Which of the following epidemiological statements about radial head subluxation is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Radial head subluxation accounts for approximately 20,000 cases per year in the United States, occurs mostly between ages 1 and 4 with 80 percent of cases at ages 1 to 3, is more common in females, and more often affects the left arm","B":"Radial head subluxation occurs most commonly between ages 5 and 10, is more common in males than females, and is equally distributed between left and right arms because the mechanism of injury is symmetric and not influenced by caregiver handedness","C":"Radial head subluxation occurs almost exclusively in infants under age 12 months due to extreme ligamentous laxity present in early infancy, and it rarely recurs after spontaneous resolution because the annular ligament matures and thickens rapidly","D":"Radial head subluxation affects children up to age 12, occurs with equal frequency in males and females, and is most commonly bilateral because caregivers typically use both hands to lift children by their arms simultaneously","E":"Radial head subluxation primarily affects children between ages 1 and 4, is more common in males than females due to higher activity levels, and most commonly affects the right arm because most caregivers are right-handed and hold the child by the right arm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Radial head subluxation affects approximately 20,000 children per year in the US. It is most common between ages 1 and 4, with 80% of cases in the 1-to-3 age range, reflecting the period when the annular ligament is lax relative to the radial head. It is more common in females and more often affects the left arm, likely because right-handed caregivers pull the child's left arm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Age 1-4 peak; F > M; left > right. 20,000 cases/year US. COMLEX: Clinical diagnosis — no imaging unless atypical. Mechanism and demographics are high-yield for question stems.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — Elbow joint dislocations: reduction and management of pediatric elbow joint injuries"},{"id":"sdl10_q03","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of radial head subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy is brought to clinic after a yanking injury to his arm. He holds the arm adducted to the body with the elbow extended and the forearm slightly pronated. Which of the following additional findings is most consistent with radial head subluxation?","choices":{"A":"The child has visible swelling and ecchymosis over the lateral elbow, passive pronation reproduces pain more than supination, and he allows full elbow flexion but refuses any forearm rotation in either direction with marked resistance","B":"The child has a prominent posterior bulge at the olecranon from posterior displacement of the ulna, and the elbow is locked in slight flexion with complete refusal to allow any passive range of motion including supination, pronation, and flexion","C":"The child screams with lateral elbow palpation and cannot passively flex the elbow beyond 90 degrees due to guarding from protective muscle spasm of the biceps brachii, which prevents forearm rotation in any direction","D":"The child has no visible deformity, demonstrates pain specifically with attempted supination of the forearm, may have mild tenderness to palpation at the radial head, and otherwise has normal passive range of elbow motion","E":"The child holds the arm in a sling position with the elbow flexed to 90 degrees, has significant swelling over the antecubital fossa, and demonstrates specific point tenderness over the medial epicondyle with valgus stress testing of the elbow"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Radial head subluxation presents with the arm adducted, the elbow extended or slightly flexed, and the forearm pronated. Pain occurs specifically with supination because this motion attempts to slide the annular ligament back over the radial head. There is no significant swelling, no visible deformity, and the child typically has normal passive range of motion in all planes except supination.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Key exam findings = arm adducted, elbow extended, forearm pronated, pain with supination, no swelling/deformity. Normal X-ray. COMLEX: Reduction by supination with flexion at elbow — palpable click = success, child uses arm within minutes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — Elbow joint dislocations: reduction and management of pediatric elbow joint injuries"},{"id":"sdl10_q04","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of little league shoulder and little league elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old male competitive pitcher presents with progressive right shoulder pain during and after pitching. X-ray shows subtle widening of the proximal humeral growth plate on the right compared to the left. Which of the following most accurately describes the pathology?","choices":{"A":"Rotator cuff tendinopathy from repetitive overhead motion causes progressive inflammation of the supraspinatus tendon at its insertion on the greater tuberosity, resulting in impingement under the coracoacromial arch during the late acceleration phase of throwing","B":"Repetitive throwing stress produces a physeal stress reaction at the proximal humeral growth plate, with physeal widening and pain during or after throwing; this condition, most common in adolescent male pitchers, is little league shoulder","C":"Calcific tendinosis of the biceps long head tendon at its attachment to the superior labrum produces SLAP lesions that extend into the posterior labrum and cause clicking during the late cocking phase in overhead throwing athletes","D":"Acromioclavicular joint sprain from repetitive overhead stress on the superior shoulder complex in a throwing athlete causes capsular laxity and progressive superior migration of the distal clavicle relative to the acromion during pitching","E":"Repetitive microtrauma to the posterior glenoid labrum during the deceleration phase of pitching produces labral tears that destabilize the glenohumeral joint and cause superior humeral head migration during the follow-through phase of the throw"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Little league shoulder is a stress reaction or Salter-Harris Type I physeal injury of the proximal humeral growth plate caused by repetitive rotational forces during throwing. It most commonly affects adolescent male pitchers aged 11-16, with a peak at age 13. X-ray may show physeal widening compared to the contralateral shoulder. Treatment is rest from throwing for up to 3 months, analgesics, and PT, with pitch count programs for prevention.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Little league shoulder = proximal humeral physeal stress reaction; age 11-16, peak 13, males. X-ray widening of growth plate. Treatment: rest up to 3 months. COMLEX: Prevention by pitch count limits is testable.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — Medial epicondylitis: valgus overuse stress at the medial elbow in adult and pediatric throwing athletes"},{"id":"sdl10_q05","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of little league shoulder and little league elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 12-year-old pitcher is diagnosed with little league elbow after presenting with medial elbow pain worse with pitching. Which of the following most accurately describes the pathomechanics of this injury?","choices":{"A":"Repetitive extension forces during ball release place tensile stress on the lateral collateral ligament complex, producing chronic lateral elbow instability and progressive osteochondral damage to the capitellum in young throwing athletes","B":"Combined valgus and distracting forces during the cocking and acceleration phases of pitching produce repetitive traction stress on the medial epicondylar growth plate, causing apophysitis or avulsion fracture of the medial epicondyle","C":"Overuse of the flexor-pronator mass from repetitive wrist flexion during follow-through causes tendinous degeneration at the medial epicondyle, producing a condition identical to adult medial epicondylitis but with greater recurrence due to open growth plates","D":"Repetitive compressive forces from the medial coronoid against the trochlea during the acceleration phase produce subchondral bone edema at the trochlea, progressing to articular cartilage fragmentation in severe or untreated cases","E":"Repetitive supination of the forearm during the pronation-to-supination transition in the late acceleration phase causes the annular ligament to become inflamed, entrapping the posterior interosseous nerve and producing lateral elbow pain"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Little league elbow is apophysitis or avulsion fracture of the medial epicondylar growth plate caused by combined valgus and distracting forces during the cocking and acceleration phases of pitching. The medial epicondylar growth plate normally closes in the mid-teens, making it vulnerable during adolescence. It most commonly affects pitchers aged 9-14, with avulsion fractures more common in ages 12-14.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Little league elbow = medial epicondylar apophysitis or avulsion from valgus and traction forces during pitching. Age 9-14. Growth plate closes mid-teens. X-ray shows medial epicondyle widening or displacement. COMLEX: Treatment = rest 4-6 weeks, analgesics, PT; surgery rare but indicated for significant displacement with instability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — Medial epicondylitis: valgus overuse stress at the medial elbow in adult and pediatric throwing athletes"},{"id":"sdl10_q06","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of little league shoulder and little league elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 10-year-old pitcher is diagnosed with little league elbow. X-ray shows medial epicondyle apophyseal widening without fragment displacement. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":{"A":"Rest from throwing for 4 to 6 weeks, analgesic therapy for pain control, physical therapy for gradual rehabilitation, and return to sport guided by an age-appropriate pitch count program to prevent recurrence","B":"Immediate operative fixation with percutaneous screw placement across the medial epicondyle is indicated for all cases of little league elbow, because any delay in surgical stabilization results in permanent medial instability from growth plate damage","C":"A long arm cast applied with the elbow at 90 degrees of flexion and the forearm in full supination is standard conservative treatment, maintained for 8 to 12 weeks regardless of fragment size or degree of physeal widening on radiograph","D":"Corticosteroid injection at the medial epicondyle is first-line treatment for little league elbow, providing rapid anti-inflammatory relief that allows safe return to throwing within 1 to 2 weeks when combined with a counterforce brace","E":"Transition to batting-only practice with continued half-effort throwing is an acceptable alternative to full rest in young athletes, as partial activity maintains muscle conditioning while allowing gradual healing of the growth plate apophysis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Non-displaced little league elbow is managed conservatively with 4-6 weeks of rest from throwing, analgesics, and physical therapy. Return to sport should be gradual and guided by pitch count programs, which are the primary preventive tool. Surgery is reserved for significantly displaced fragments or cases with valgus instability, which are uncommon at this age without a clear avulsion fracture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Non-displaced little league elbow = rest 4-6 weeks, analgesics, PT. Surgery rare. Prevention = pitch counts. COMLEX: Distinguish from adult medial epicondylitis — in children, the growth plate is the weak link, not the UCL.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — Medial epicondylitis: valgus overuse stress at the medial elbow in adult and pediatric throwing athletes"},{"id":"sdl10_q07","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Osgood-Schlatter disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old male athlete presents with anterior knee pain and a prominent, tender bump just below the kneecap that worsens with running, jumping, and kneeling. He is in a period of rapid growth. Which of the following best describes the underlying pathological process?","choices":{"A":"Patellofemoral syndrome caused by maltracking of the patella in the trochlear groove, producing retropatellar cartilage friction and diffuse peripatellar pain with prolonged sitting, stair climbing, and squatting in rapidly growing adolescent athletes","B":"Patellar tendinopathy from repetitive microtrauma at the inferior pole of the patella, producing degenerative changes within the tendon substance and localized swelling at the superior insertion of the patellar tendon on the patella itself","C":"Prepatellar bursitis from repetitive mechanical irritation of the superficial bursa anterior to the patella, producing soft tissue swelling that is fluctuant on palpation and localizes to the anterior patella rather than the tibial tuberosity","D":"Stress fracture of the proximal tibial metaphysis from repetitive compressive loading, producing point tenderness over the medial tibial flare and periosteal new bone formation visible on plain radiographs of the proximal tibia below the joint line","E":"Traction apophysitis at the insertion of the patellar tendon on the tibial tuberosity, caused by repetitive quadriceps contraction during rapid skeletal growth, producing pain and bony prominence at the tibial tuberosity that worsens with knee flexion activities"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Osgood-Schlatter disease is traction apophysitis at the tibial tuberosity, where the patellar tendon inserts. Repetitive quadriceps contraction during rapid growth applies repetitive traction stress to the tibial tuberosity apophysis, causing inflammation, pain, and eventual bony prominence. It is most common in active adolescents aged 9-14, predominantly males, and bilateral in 25-50% of cases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osgood-Schlatter = traction apophysitis at tibial tuberosity, patellar tendon insertion. Age 9-14, M > F, athletes 2x risk, bilateral 25-50%. Pain with knee flexion activities. COMLEX: No imaging required. Treatment = activity modification, PT, analgesics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — Patellofemoral syndrome: anterior knee pain in adolescents and diagnostic differentiation from apophysitis"},{"id":"sdl10_q08","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Osgood-Schlatter disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following most accurately describes the epidemiology of Osgood-Schlatter disease?","choices":{"A":"Osgood-Schlatter disease affects children between ages 5 and 9, is equally common in males and females, and almost always presents bilaterally because patellar tendon stress is symmetric across both knees during growth spurts in all active children","B":"Osgood-Schlatter disease most commonly affects children between ages 9 and 14, is more common in males than females, carries approximately double the risk in athletes compared to non-athletes, and presents bilaterally in 25 to 50 percent of cases","C":"Osgood-Schlatter disease primarily affects obese adolescents between ages 12 and 17 and is more common in females, since a high-riding patella in females increases patellar tendon length and amplifies tensile stress at the tibial tuberosity during running","D":"Osgood-Schlatter disease is equally common across all age groups from 8 to 18, shows no sex predilection, and is more prevalent in sedentary adolescents because inactivity leads to relative tightening of the quadriceps mechanism during growth","E":"Osgood-Schlatter disease affects children between ages 6 and 12, is more common in females due to earlier onset of growth spurts, and accounts for the majority of all anterior knee pain presentations seen in pediatric primary care visits"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Osgood-Schlatter disease peaks at ages 9-14, coinciding with the adolescent growth spurt. Males are more commonly affected. Athletes have approximately double the risk compared to non-athletes. Bilateral presentation occurs in 25-50% of cases. The incidence in adolescents aged 12-15 has been estimated at approximately 9.83%.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Age 9-14, M > F, athletes 2x risk, bilateral 25-50%. Rapid growth is the key predisposing factor. COMLEX: The fact that imaging is not necessary is commonly tested — Osgood-Schlatter is a clinical diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — Patellofemoral syndrome: anterior knee pain in adolescents and diagnostic differentiation from apophysitis"},{"id":"sdl10_q09","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Osgood-Schlatter disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 12-year-old soccer player is diagnosed with Osgood-Schlatter disease. He trains 5 days per week. Which of the following best describes the correct treatment approach?","choices":{"A":"Treatment centers on rebalancing the ratio of physical stress to recovery time, supported by physical therapy for quadriceps flexibility and strengthening, analgesics for pain control, and activity modification rather than complete cessation of sport","B":"Complete immobilization in a long leg cast for 6 to 8 weeks is indicated to allow the apophysis to heal before resuming activity, as continued sport participation without immobilization consistently leads to avulsion fracture of the tibial tuberosity in growing athletes","C":"Surgical excision of the tibial tuberosity apophysis is the definitive treatment for Osgood-Schlatter disease in athletes who fail 4 weeks of conservative management, as prolonged apophyseal inflammation leads to a painful ossicle requiring operative removal","D":"Intraarticular corticosteroid injection into the infrapatellar region is first-line treatment for Osgood-Schlatter disease, providing immediate anti-inflammatory relief and allowing rapid return to sport when combined with a patellar tendon strap brace","E":"Complete cessation of all athletic activity for a minimum of 3 months is required, as continued sport participation accelerates apophyseal fragmentation and significantly increases the long-term risk of tibial tuberosity avulsion fracture in skeletally immature athletes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Treatment of Osgood-Schlatter disease focuses on rebalancing the ratio of stress to recovery rather than mandating complete rest. Physical therapy addresses quadriceps tightness and strengthening, analgesics manage pain, and activity modification reduces apophyseal load. Regenerative injection therapy such as prolotherapy is an option for refractory cases. Complete cessation of sport is generally not required.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osgood-Schlatter treatment = rebalance stress vs recovery, PT, analgesics, activity modification. Not immobilization or surgery in most cases. COMLEX: Prolotherapy is mentioned as a regenerative option for refractory cases.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — Patellofemoral syndrome: anterior knee pain in adolescents and diagnostic differentiation from apophysitis"},{"id":"sdl10_q10","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Legg-Calve-Perthes disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy presents with insidious onset left hip pain and limp. Hip X-ray shows femoral head flattening with increased density. Which of the following best describes the pathophysiological sequence of Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease?","choices":{"A":"Chronic repetitive loading of the femoral head causes cartilage degeneration at the articular surface, exposing subchondral bone to synovial fluid, which triggers inflammatory degradation of trabecular bone progressing inward from the articular margin","B":"Inadequate blood flow to the femoral head causes ischemic necrosis of the epiphysis; new bone deposits on avascular trabeculae while calcification of necrotic marrow crushes them, distorting the femoral head and risking subluxation from the acetabulum","C":"Autoimmune synovitis causes inflammatory mediators to diffuse into the femoral head, producing reactive bone resorption at the subchondral plate and secondary avascular necrosis from cytokine-mediated vascular thrombosis in the epiphyseal vessels","D":"Repetitive microtrauma to the proximal femoral physis from running and jumping activities produces stress fractures through the growth plate, causing progressive physeal widening and subsequent collapse of the femoral head under axial weight-bearing load","E":"Slippage of the proximal femoral epiphysis through a weakened physis produces mechanical impingement of the femoral neck against the acetabulum, causing secondary avascular necrosis of the femoral head from repetitive vascular compression during weight-bearing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease is avascular necrosis of the femoral head in children. The sequence is: inadequate blood flow causes ischemic epiphyseal death, new bone attempts to deposit on avascular trabeculae, calcification of necrotic marrow crushes the avascular scaffold, and femoral head distortion progresses to subluxation or dislocation. Initial X-rays may be negative; bone scan and MRI detect it earlier.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LCP = AVN of femoral head in children age 4-9, M:F 4:1, bilateral 10%. Pathology: ischemia leads to femoral head collapse and distortion. Initial X-rays may be negative — MRI or bone scan detect earlier. COMLEX: Decreased abduction and IR on exam; can refer to knee.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — Avascular necrosis of the hip: pathophysiology of femoral head ischemia and management across age groups"},{"id":"sdl10_q11","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Legg-Calve-Perthes disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A parent brings their 5-year-old son to clinic with a 2-week history of limp. He occasionally complains of knee pain. Hip X-ray is initially unremarkable. Which of the following best characterizes the typical presentation and epidemiology of Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease?","choices":{"A":"Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease presents acutely with severe hip pain, fever, and refusal to bear weight in children aged 10 to 15, with a male-to-female ratio of 1:4 and bilateral involvement in approximately 50 percent of cases at the time of initial diagnosis","B":"Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease most commonly presents between ages 8 and 12 with bilateral hip pain and a waddling gait, affects females more commonly than males at a ratio of 4:1, and is associated with rapid growth spurts causing relative vascular insufficiency","C":"Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease presents in overweight adolescents aged 12 to 15 with acute hip and groin pain after minor trauma, with equal sex distribution, bilateral involvement in 30 percent, and external rotation deformity on passive hip examination","D":"Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease most commonly affects children aged 4 to 9, with a male-to-female ratio of 4:1, and typically presents with insidious onset hip pain, antalgic limp, pain that may refer to the knee, and decreased hip abduction and internal rotation on examination","E":"Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease presents subacutely in children aged 2 to 6 with bilateral hip stiffness and a Trendelenburg gait, affects females more than males, and is strongly associated with a prior episode of septic hip arthritis in the majority of cases"},"correct":"D","explanation":"LCP occurs in children aged 4-9 with a 4:1 male predominance. Presentation is insidious onset hip pain, limp, and referred knee pain. Exam shows decreased hip abduction and internal rotation. Bilateral involvement occurs in only 10% of cases. Initial radiographs may be normal, requiring bone scan or MRI for early diagnosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LCP demographics: age 4-9, M:F 4:1, bilateral 10%, insidious onset. Classic exam = decreased abduction and IR. Referred knee pain is a trap — always examine the hip in a limping child. COMLEX: MRI or bone scan when X-ray is negative.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — Avascular necrosis of the hip: pathophysiology of femoral head ischemia and management across age groups"},{"id":"sdl10_q12","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Legg-Calve-Perthes disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 7-year-old male with suspected Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease has normal plain radiographs on initial presentation but persistent hip pain and limp. Which of the following is most accurate regarding imaging and management of this condition?","choices":{"A":"Because plain radiographs are normal, Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease can be excluded and the child should be reassured and observed; the condition is never present when initial AP and frog-leg lateral hip radiographs are both interpreted as normal by a radiologist","B":"Ultrasound of the hip is the definitive imaging modality for Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease, demonstrating increased echogenicity of the femoral head cartilage before X-ray changes appear, and treatment consists of ultrasound-guided aspiration of the joint effusion","C":"Bone scan and MRI can detect Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease before radiographic changes appear; conservative management with NSAIDs, activity restriction, physical therapy, and bracing or casting for containment is first-line, with surgery reserved for cases failing conservative care","D":"CT scan of the pelvis with contrast is the most sensitive initial imaging study for Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease, demonstrating contrast pooling in the femoral head, and treatment always requires immediate surgical core decompression to restore femoral head perfusion","E":"Dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry scanning of the hip is indicated when plain films are normal, demonstrating reduced bone mineral density of the femoral head before structural changes appear, and treatment is calcium and vitamin D supplementation to improve remineralization"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Initial X-rays can be negative in LCP, and MRI or bone scan should be obtained when clinical suspicion remains high. Conservative management includes NSAIDs, rest and activity restriction, physical therapy to maintain range of motion, and bracing or casting to maintain femoral head containment in the acetabulum during the revascularization phase. Surgery is reserved for failure of conservative care.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LCP imaging: Initial X-rays may be negative — MRI and bone scan detect earlier. Treatment: NSAIDs, rest, PT, bracing/casting for containment, crutches. Surgery if conservative fails. COMLEX: Maintaining containment of femoral head in acetabulum is the therapeutic goal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — Avascular necrosis of the hip: pathophysiology of femoral head ischemia and management across age groups"},{"id":"sdl10_q13","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old obese male presents with 3 weeks of dull left hip pain and a limp. He denies trauma. On exam, the left leg is externally rotated. Hip radiographs are obtained and confirm the diagnosis. Which of the following best describes the underlying pathology?","choices":{"A":"The proximal femoral epiphysis undergoes avascular necrosis due to disruption of the lateral epiphyseal arterial supply from within the hip joint capsule, causing the femoral head to collapse into the acetabulum in the absence of any significant shear force","B":"Repetitive impact loading causes a stress fracture through the femoral neck at the subcapital region, producing gradual anterior and superior displacement of the femoral head relative to the femoral neck in overweight adolescent males during rapid growth","C":"Acetabular labral hypertrophy from compensatory overload causes progressive anterior femoral head coverage deficit, producing cam-type femoroacetabular impingement and secondary physeal stress in overweight adolescent males during peak growth","D":"Inflammatory synovitis from a prior viral illness causes the proximal femoral growth plate to weaken through cytokine-mediated degradation of physeal cartilage, allowing the femoral neck to rotate anteriorly relative to the fixed femoral head during the preslip phase","E":"Shearing forces through a physiologically weakened proximal femoral physis cause the femoral head epiphysis to slip posteriorly and inferiorly relative to the femoral neck, producing the characteristic external rotation deformity and antalgic gait seen in this presentation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"SCFE is a developmental condition in which the proximal femoral epiphysis slips posteriorly and inferiorly relative to the femoral neck through a weakened physis. The physis is weakened by physiologic hormonal changes during puberty, exacerbated by obesity, metabolic disorders, and other factors. The result is the classic presentation of an obese adolescent with dull atraumatic hip pain, antalgic gait, and external rotation deformity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SCFE = posterior and inferior epiphyseal slip through weakened proximal femoral physis. Classic = obese adolescent, dull atraumatic hip pain, external rotation deformity. Radiographs mandatory. COMLEX: Referred knee pain is a trap; always X-ray the hip.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 1 — Developmental dysplasia of the hip: pediatric hip developmental conditions and their management"},{"id":"sdl10_q14","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 14-year-old female with SCFE is classified as unstable. Which of the following best describes how SCFE is staged and what determines stability classification?","choices":{"A":"SCFE is staged entirely by patient age: preslip in children under age 10, acute SCFE in those aged 10 to 12, and chronic SCFE in those over age 12; stability is determined by whether bilateral hip radiographs show symmetric or asymmetric epiphyseal height","B":"SCFE stability is based solely on the degree of epiphyseal slip angle on radiograph: mild slip below 30 degrees is stable, moderate slip 30 to 50 degrees is borderline, and severe slip above 50 degrees is unstable regardless of functional weight-bearing status","C":"SCFE is classified by duration into preslip, acute under 6 weeks, and chronic over several weeks; stable SCFE allows weight bearing without pain and unstable SCFE causes a painful gait, with a much higher AVN risk when unstable","D":"SCFE is classified only by duration, with acute lasting under 3 weeks and chronic lasting over 3 months; stability is determined by MRI perfusion imaging of the femoral head showing whether lateral epiphyseal vessels remain patent on gadolinium sequences","E":"SCFE staging uses the modified Loder classification exclusively based on slip width: stage 1 is less than one-third physis displacement, stage 2 is one-third to two-thirds, and stage 3 is greater than two-thirds; all stage 3 cases are automatically classified as unstable"},"correct":"C","explanation":"SCFE is classified by duration: preslip, acute under 6 weeks, and chronic over several weeks. Stability is determined clinically: stable SCFE allows weight-bearing without pain, while unstable SCFE presents with a painful gait. Unstable SCFE carries a dramatically higher risk of AVN because ongoing physeal shear disrupts the remaining blood supply to the femoral head.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SCFE stages: preslip, acute under 6 weeks, chronic. Stable = bears weight without pain; unstable = painful gait. Unstable = high AVN risk. COMLEX: All SCFE = non-weight bearing + urgent/emergent operative fixation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 1 — Developmental dysplasia of the hip: pediatric hip developmental conditions and their management"},{"id":"sdl10_q15","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old male presents with stable SCFE confirmed on radiograph. He walks without crutches and bears weight without pain, with a mild limp. Which of the following best describes initial management?","choices":{"A":"Immediate open reduction with periacetabular osteotomy is indicated for all SCFE presentations to prevent progressive AVN, as conservative management consistently results in femoral head collapse in the majority of pediatric patients with any degree of physeal slip","B":"The patient should be made non-weight bearing and referred urgently for operative fixation, the standard of care for SCFE regardless of stability, because further slippage with weight bearing increases the risk of avascular necrosis","C":"A hip abduction brace applied in 30 degrees of abduction is first-line conservative treatment for stable SCFE, worn continuously for 8 to 12 weeks until radiographic evidence of physeal closure is confirmed on AP and frog-leg lateral projections","D":"NSAIDs and activity restriction for 6 to 8 weeks with repeat radiographs at 4-week intervals is standard conservative management for stable SCFE, reserving operative fixation for cases where the slip angle increases by more than 10 degrees on surveillance imaging","E":"Closed manipulation under anesthesia followed by hip spica casting for 12 weeks is indicated for stable SCFE in adolescents under age 13, since surgical fixation carries an unacceptably high AVN risk from physeal instrumentation at this developmental stage"},"correct":"B","explanation":"All SCFE, regardless of stability, is managed with immediate non-weight bearing and urgent operative fixation. The standard surgical treatment is in situ fixation with a cannulated screw across the physis. Even in stable SCFE, continued weight-bearing risks further slip progression and AVN. Conservative management alone is insufficient for any confirmed SCFE.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SCFE management = non-weight bearing immediately + operative fixation for all types. In situ screw fixation is standard. COMLEX: Do not attempt closed reduction, as this increases AVN risk. Bilateral fixation may be considered for high-risk patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 1 — Developmental dysplasia of the hip: pediatric hip developmental conditions and their management"},{"id":"sdl10_q16","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the Salter-Harris fracture classification system and describe greenstick and torus fractures in pediatric patients.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 9-year-old girl rolls her ankle into inversion playing soccer. She has swelling and point tenderness directly over the distal fibular growth plate, about 1 to 2 cm above the tip of the lateral malleolus, and no tenderness over the anterior talofibular ligament. Radiographs show no fracture line. Which interpretation best fits the course reading on pediatric ankle injuries?","choices":{"A":"A grade 2 ATFL sprain, because ligaments are the weakest link at this age and tear before bone or cartilage gives way","B":"A Salter-Harris type V crush of the distal tibial physis, because inversion axially compresses the medial tibial plate","C":"A torus fracture of the distal fibular metaphysis, because inversion buckles the lateral cortex without a visible line","D":"A Salter-Harris type I distal fibular injury, as the physis is the weakest link and slides apart before ligaments tear","E":"A normal examination with growth-plate tenderness, because physeal pain is expected after any sprain in a growing child"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Nelson sports medicine chapter for this SDL notes that physeal injuries at the ankle, most commonly the distal fibula, typically follow an inversion injury and are predominantly Salter-Harris I fractures. In children younger than 12, the physeal plates are generally the weakest link and tend to slide or pull apart before the surrounding ligaments tear. A type I injury runs through the cartilaginous physis, which is not seen on radiographs, so point tenderness over the physis with normal films should be treated as a fracture. Calling it an ATFL sprain reverses the weak-link principle and ignores the site of tenderness. A type V crush involves axial compression, not an inversion pull on the fibula. A torus fracture is a visible cortical buckle at the metaphysis. Physeal tenderness is not an expected normal finding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Salter-Harris (SALTR): I = straight across the physis (may look normal), II = above (metaphysis), III = lower (epiphysis), IV = through all, V = rammed/crushed. Type I has the lowest and type V the highest risk of growth disturbance. COMLEX: In a child, tenderness over the physis after a \"sprain\" = presumed SH I fracture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — lateral ankle sprain mechanism and grading in adults"},{"id":"sdl10_q17","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the Salter-Harris fracture classification system and describe greenstick and torus fractures in pediatric patients.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl falls onto an outstretched hand. X-ray shows localized bulging of the cortex at the distal radius metaphysis without a complete cortical break. Which of the following best explains this fracture pattern?","choices":{"A":"The greenstick pattern occurs in children because pediatric bone has higher mineral content than adult bone, causing the cortex to crack completely on the tension side while the compression side remains intact, producing an incomplete transverse fracture through the diaphysis","B":"The Salter-Harris Type I fracture produces physeal disruption without cortical injury when axial force is applied to an immature bone, causing the growth plate to fail in shear before the adjacent metaphyseal cortex accumulates enough strain to buckle","C":"The buckle fracture in adults occurs because cortical bone is more brittle than pediatric bone, causing complete fracture with axial load rather than the plastic deformation seen in younger patients with more porous and compliant metaphyseal cancellous bone","D":"The torus fracture represents an avulsion of the dorsal cortex at the distal radial metaphysis caused by tensile stress from dorsal radiocarpal ligaments during wrist hyperextension, producing a dorsal cortical flake fragment visible on lateral X-ray","E":"Pediatric bone is more porous and compliant than adult bone, so axial compressive force causes the cortex to buckle rather than fracture completely, producing the characteristic cortical bulge of the torus fracture at the metaphysis without complete cortical disruption"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Torus fractures occur because pediatric bone is more porous and mechanically compliant than adult bone. When axial compressive force is applied, the relatively porous metaphyseal cortex buckles without fracturing completely, producing the characteristic cortical bulge. This is distinct from greenstick fractures, which result from bending forces and produce complete cortical failure on the tension side.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Torus fracture = axial compression buckles cortex without complete fracture; pediatric bone is more porous/compliant. Greenstick = bending force, one cortex breaks, other bends. COMLEX: Torus fractures are stable injuries treated conservatively with casting or splinting.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — Salter-Harris classification: physeal fracture grading and growth plate injury in lower extremity fractures"},{"id":"sdl10_q18","sdlNumber":10,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the Salter-Harris fracture classification system and describe greenstick and torus fractures in pediatric patients.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 9-year-old sustains a mid-shaft forearm injury after a fall. X-ray shows one cortex of the radius fractured completely while the opposite cortex is intact but angulated without a fracture line. Which of the following best characterizes this fracture pattern?","choices":{"A":"A greenstick fracture is a complete transverse fracture of both cortices of a long bone in a child that is identical to an adult fracture pattern, occurring when bending forces exceed the plastic deformation limit of pediatric bone at the diaphyseal-metaphyseal junction","B":"A greenstick fracture results from axial loading along the long axis of the bone, causing the periosteum to separate from the cortex on the compressive side without cortical fracture, producing a painful periosteal hematoma beneath the periosteal sleeve","C":"A greenstick fracture is an incomplete fracture unique to pediatric bone in which bending stress fractures one cortex completely while the opposite cortex bends without fracturing, analogous to the behavior of a fresh green tree branch that breaks on one side when bent","D":"A greenstick fracture involves axial compression of the metaphyseal cortex without complete fracture, producing a localized cortical buckle at the point of maximum compressive stress, and is treated identically to torus fractures with a short arm cast for 3 weeks","E":"A greenstick fracture is a physeal fracture pattern in which the fracture line propagates along the zone of provisional calcification within the growth plate, producing a clean separation of the epiphysis from the metaphysis without cortical injury"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Greenstick fractures are incomplete fractures of pediatric long bones resulting from bending forces. One cortex fractures completely on the tension side while the opposite cortex bends without fracturing, because pediatric bone is more flexible and has a higher plastic deformation tolerance. The name derives from the analogy of bending a fresh green tree branch, which splits on one side before breaking completely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Greenstick fracture = bending force, one cortex fractures, other bends; pediatric only. Compare to torus = axial compression with cortical buckle, no complete fracture. COMLEX: Greenstick may require reduction to correct angulation before casting.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — Salter-Harris classification: physeal fracture grading and growth plate injury in lower extremity fractures"},{"id":"sdl10_q19","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of radial head subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 2-year-old female is brought to the ED after her older sibling pulled her arm to help her up from the floor. She holds the left arm adducted, slightly pronated, and refuses to lift it. Elbow X-ray is normal. Vitals are stable. Which of the following describes the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":{"A":"Perform closed reduction by supinating the forearm while applying gentle pressure over the radial head and flexing the elbow; a palpable click indicates successful reduction, and most children regain normal arm function within minutes without imaging follow-up","B":"Apply a posterior long arm splint with the elbow at 90 degrees and the forearm in neutral rotation, and arrange orthopedic follow-up within 1 to 2 weeks for formal reduction under procedural sedation in a controlled surgical outpatient setting","C":"Obtain CT arthrography of the elbow to confirm annular ligament interposition and plan the optimal reduction approach, as blind closed reduction attempts can further displace the ligament and convert a subluxation to a complete elbow dislocation","D":"Administer oral ibuprofen for pain control and arrange return visit in 5 days to reassess, since nursemaid's elbow typically resolves spontaneously within 48 to 72 hours without formal reduction in children under age 3","E":"Refer to orthopedic surgery for arthroscopic removal of the interposed annular ligament, as closed reduction is contraindicated in children under age 3 due to the risk of physeal injury to the proximal radial growth plate during the supination maneuver"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Nursemaid's elbow is treated with closed reduction using the supination-flexion or hyperpronation technique. In the supination-flexion method, the forearm is supinated while gentle pressure is applied over the radial head, followed by elbow flexion. A palpable or audible click indicates successful reduction. Most children recover full use of the arm within minutes. Imaging is not required after successful reduction in children under age 5 with a classic presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Reduction technique = supinate forearm with gentle pressure over radial head, then flex elbow. Click = success. Child should use arm within minutes. COMLEX: No imaging needed after successful reduction in classic presentation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — Elbow joint dislocations: differentiating subluxation from dislocation in pediatric elbow injuries"},{"id":"sdl10_q20","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of radial head subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 3-year-old male presents with left arm holding after a pulling injury. History and exam are classic for nursemaid's elbow. The child is 3 years old, the mechanism is consistent, and the child has no swelling or bruising. Which of the following best describes the appropriate use of imaging in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Bilateral elbow radiographs in AP and lateral projections are mandatory for all suspected cases of radial head subluxation in order to exclude concurrent fractures and to document the baseline radiocapitellar relationship before reduction is attempted","B":"MRI of the elbow without contrast is the preferred diagnostic modality for radial head subluxation, providing direct visualization of the displaced annular ligament and confirming the diagnosis when the clinical presentation is classic but imaging is required for documentation","C":"Fluoroscopy during the reduction maneuver is required in children under age 4 to confirm that the radiocapitellar line is restored, as physical examination alone cannot reliably confirm successful reduction in preverbal and minimally verbal children","D":"Radiographs are required before and after every reduction attempt as a standard medical-legal practice, since failure to obtain pre-reduction imaging and document a normal baseline radiograph constitutes a deviation from the standard of care in pediatric emergency medicine","E":"Imaging is not necessary in children under age 5 with a classic history and presentation consistent with radial head subluxation; radiographs should be obtained when age is over 5, the mechanism is atypical, or the child fails to regain normal function after reduction as expected"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Radial head subluxation is a clinical diagnosis. Imaging is not necessary when the history and presentation are classic, the patient is under age 5, and the mechanism is consistent with annular ligament injury. Radiographs are indicated when age is over 5, when the mechanism is atypical suggesting possible fracture, or when the child fails to recover normal function following an attempted reduction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: No imaging needed for classic nursemaid's elbow in child under 5 with typical mechanism. Image when: age over 5, atypical mechanism, or fails reduction. COMLEX: Clinical diagnosis — unnecessary imaging exposes young children to radiation without benefit.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — Elbow joint dislocations: differentiating subluxation from dislocation in pediatric elbow injuries"},{"id":"sdl10_q21","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of radial head subluxation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 2-year-old boy had a nursemaid's elbow reduced in clinic by supinating the forearm with pressure over the radial head; a click was felt and he is now reaching for toys with the arm. This is his second episode in 4 months. His parents ask about aftercare and the chance of it happening again. Which advice is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Place the arm in a long arm cast for 3 weeks, because the torn annular ligament must heal in a fixed position to prevent another episode","B":"Order elbow radiographs now, because any second episode means a radial head fracture is likely and must be excluded before discharge","C":"Refer for surgical repair of the annular ligament, because two episodes before age 3 show it is incompetent and will keep slipping","D":"No immobilization is needed; it can recur, so avoid pulling on the arms, parents can learn the reduction, and it is rare after age 5","E":"Keep the elbow in a sling in full extension for 2 weeks, because extension tightens the annular ligament around the radial head"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Nelson upper limb chapter for this SDL states that after the annular ligament is reduced (supination with pressure over the radial head, often with a palpable click), immobilization is not required, but recurrence can happen. Parents should avoid activities that apply traction to the elbows, they can learn the reduction maneuver for recurrent episodes, and recurrence beyond 5 years of age is rare. Casting is not needed after a successful reduction. Imaging is reserved for children over 5, an atypical mechanism, or failure to regain function; a recurrence with a classic history does not require films. Surgical repair is not part of management. A sling in extension is not recommended, and extension is not the position of reduction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Nursemaid's elbow = annular ligament subluxation from axial traction on a pronated, extended arm; ages 1–4, F > M, left > right. COMLEX: Reduce by supination (or hyperpronation) with pressure on the radial head; recheck that the child uses the arm.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — elbow dislocation presentation and reduction"},{"id":"sdl10_q22","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of little league shoulder and little league elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old male pitcher presents with progressive right proximal shoulder pain after his third pitching appearance this week. He has no pitch count monitoring and pitches 5 days per week. X-ray shows subtle widening of the proximal humeral physis compared to the contralateral shoulder. What is the diagnosis and the most important preventive measure?","choices":{"A":"Little league shoulder, a stress reaction of the proximal humeral growth plate from repetitive throwing; the most important preventive measure is implementing age-appropriate pitch count limits to restrict cumulative physeal stress across a week and across a season","B":"Rotator cuff impingement syndrome from subacromial narrowing in a skeletally immature athlete; the most important preventive measure is strengthening the rotator cuff muscles through internal and external rotation exercises with progressive elastic resistance band training","C":"Septic arthritis of the glenohumeral joint from bacteremic seeding of the shoulder synovium during intense training; the most important immediate step is joint aspiration under ultrasound guidance followed by IV antibiotics pending culture and sensitivity results","D":"Acromioclavicular joint sprain from repetitive overhead stress on the superior shoulder complex in a throwing athlete; the most important preventive measure is limiting horizontal adduction forces across the acromioclavicular joint during the follow-through phase of the pitch","E":"Bicipital tendinopathy from repetitive supination forces during the late cocking phase, producing inflammation at the biceps long head tendon origin at the superior labrum; the most important preventive measure is limiting curveball pitching in athletes under age 14"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Little league shoulder is a physeal stress reaction at the proximal humeral growth plate from repetitive throwing. The lack of pitch count monitoring in this case allowed cumulative physeal overload. Pitch counts are the most important preventive tool, as they limit cumulative stress on the open growth plate. Treatment is rest from throwing for up to 3 months, analgesics, and physical therapy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Little league shoulder = proximal humeral physeal stress from overuse in male adolescent pitchers. X-ray shows physeal widening. Pitch counts = primary prevention. Treatment: rest up to 3 months, analgesics, PT. COMLEX: Distinguish from rotator cuff pathology by age and physeal widening on X-ray.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — Supracondylar fractures: medial epicondyle and growth plate injuries of the pediatric elbow"},{"id":"sdl10_q23","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of little league shoulder and little league elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 14-year-old right-handed pitcher presents to the ED after feeling a pop in his elbow during pitch release. He has medial elbow pain, swelling, and limited range of motion. X-ray shows a displaced medial epicondyle fragment. Valgus stress testing reveals elbow instability. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Long arm cast application with the elbow at 90 degrees of flexion and the forearm in full pronation for 8 weeks is standard treatment; this position relaxes the flexor-pronator mass and restores attachment of the avulsed medial epicondyle through gradual fibrous union","B":"Immediate closed reduction of the medial epicondyle fragment under conscious sedation followed by a functional hinged elbow brace for 4 weeks with immediate initiation of range-of-motion exercises is appropriate for displaced fragments with valgus instability","C":"Conservative management with rest, ice, analgesics, and a sling for 4 to 6 weeks is appropriate because displaced medial epicondyle fragments in adolescents universally heal with fibrous union regardless of displacement magnitude or degree of instability","D":"Corticosteroid injection into the ulnar nerve groove to reduce traction neuropathy of the ulnar nerve, combined with a counterforce brace, is the first-line treatment when a pop is heard during pitching with associated valgus elbow instability","E":"Surgical fixation of the displaced medial epicondyle fragment is indicated given the significant displacement and valgus elbow instability; conservative management alone is insufficient when displacement and ligamentous instability are both present"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Significantly displaced medial epicondyle avulsion fractures with concurrent valgus elbow instability are indications for surgical fixation. The medial epicondyle is the attachment of the ulnar collateral ligament; displacement with instability means the UCL cannot be restored by conservative means alone. Rest alone for 4-6 weeks is appropriate for non-displaced or minimally displaced fractures without instability.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Displaced medial epicondyle avulsion with valgus instability = surgical fixation indicated. Non-displaced without instability = conservative (rest 4-6 wk). COMLEX: Little league elbow peak avulsion risk at age 12-14; surgery reserved for displacement with instability.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — Supracondylar fractures: medial epicondyle and growth plate injuries of the pediatric elbow"},{"id":"sdl10_q24","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of little league shoulder and little league elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physician evaluates two 13-year-old pitchers in the same clinic session. Patient A has proximal shoulder pain after throwing, with physeal widening at the proximal humerus on X-ray. Patient B has medial elbow pain worsened by valgus stress, with medial epicondyle apophyseal widening on X-ray. Which of the following correctly pairs each patient with their diagnosis and the growth plate involved?","choices":{"A":"Patient A has little league elbow affecting the medial epicondylar apophysis of the distal humerus, while patient B has little league shoulder affecting the greater tuberosity apophysis of the proximal humerus; both conditions are treated with surgical fixation regardless of severity","B":"Patient A has medial epicondyle apophysitis from repetitive valgus loading in the deceleration phase of pitching, while patient B has proximal humeral physeal stress reaction from excessive torque during the cocking phase; both require bilateral surgical referral","C":"Patient A has little league shoulder, a stress reaction at the proximal humeral growth plate from repetitive throwing, while patient B has little league elbow, which is apophysitis or avulsion at the medial epicondylar growth plate from repeated valgus and distracting forces during pitching","D":"Patient A has a proximal humeral stress fracture requiring immediate shoulder spica cast immobilization, while patient B has medial collateral ligament insufficiency requiring ulnar collateral ligament reconstruction due to valgus instability from medial apophyseal disruption","E":"Patient A has adhesive capsulitis triggered by repetitive overhead throwing in a skeletally immature shoulder, while patient B has ulnar collateral ligament sprain from the valgus torque of pitching; both are managed with a minimum 6-month rest period"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Little league shoulder and little league elbow are distinct overuse injuries in adolescent pitchers affecting different growth plates. Little league shoulder involves the proximal humeral physis from rotational stresses during throwing. Little league elbow involves the medial epicondylar apophysis from valgus and distracting forces during the cocking and acceleration phases. Both are prevented by pitch count programs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Little league shoulder = proximal humeral physis; age 11-16, rest up to 3 months. Little league elbow = medial epicondylar apophysis; age 9-14, rest 4-6 weeks. Both prevented by pitch counts. COMLEX: Know which growth plate each condition affects.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — Supracondylar fractures: medial epicondyle and growth plate injuries of the pediatric elbow"},{"id":"sdl10_q25","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Osgood-Schlatter disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 12-year-old male soccer player presents with bilateral anterior knee pain for 2 months, worse with kneeling, jumping, and stair climbing. He has palpable, tender prominences below both knees. He is in a rapid growth phase and has no history of injury. X-ray is not obtained. Which of the following best justifies appropriately deferring imaging?","choices":{"A":"Osgood-Schlatter disease is a clinical diagnosis based on tibial tuberosity pain and prominence in a growing athlete; X-ray does not change the initial conservative management, which centers on activity modification, physical therapy, and analgesics","B":"X-ray should be deferred because radiation exposure to the proximal tibial growth plate significantly increases the risk of physeal arrest and leg length discrepancy when cumulative X-ray dose reaches the 0.1 Gy threshold in skeletally immature adolescents","C":"X-ray is deferred because Osgood-Schlatter disease is caused by a metabolic disorder of calcium deposition at the tibial tuberosity, and radiographs cannot detect the early calcium dysregulation that precedes structural bony changes at the apophysis","D":"Radiographs are deferred in bilateral cases because bilateral tibial tuberosity pain eliminates the possibility of a fracture or neoplasm, which would present unilaterally, and bilateral symptoms confirm the diagnosis by exclusion from other pathology","E":"X-ray is deferred because the proximal tibial apophysis is always radiolucent in adolescents experiencing rapid skeletal growth, rendering radiographs unreliable for assessment of apophyseal injury during the active growth phase of the condition"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Osgood-Schlatter disease is a clinical diagnosis. The characteristic presentation — tibial tuberosity pain and prominence in an active adolescent during rapid growth — does not require X-ray confirmation. Imaging does not change initial management, which focuses on rebalancing stress and recovery through activity modification, PT, and analgesics. X-ray may be obtained to exclude other pathology when presentation is atypical.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osgood-Schlatter = clinical diagnosis; no imaging required. X-ray shows tibial tuberosity irregularity or avulsion only if obtained. COMLEX: Treat with activity modification, PT, analgesics, prolotherapy for refractory cases.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — Patellofemoral syndrome: distinguishing anterior knee pain etiologies in growing athletes"},{"id":"sdl10_q26","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Osgood-Schlatter disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old female sprinter has Osgood-Schlatter disease with persistent tibial tuberosity pain despite 6 weeks of activity modification, physical therapy, and NSAIDs. Her coach asks whether there are any advanced treatment options. Which of the following is an advanced treatment option for refractory Osgood-Schlatter disease?","choices":{"A":"Corticosteroid injection directly into the infrapatellar fat pad provides sustained relief for 6 to 12 months in refractory Osgood-Schlatter disease by reducing patellar tendon tension at its tibial insertion and decreasing local inflammatory cytokine concentrations","B":"Arthroscopic debridement of the tibial tuberosity apophysis provides definitive relief in refractory Osgood-Schlatter disease by removing the avulsed apophyseal ossicle and smoothing the tibial tuberosity prominence under direct arthroscopic visualization","C":"Prolotherapy, a form of regenerative injection therapy at the patellar tendon insertion on the tibial tuberosity, is an option for refractory cases of Osgood-Schlatter disease aimed at stimulating tissue repair at the tendon-bone junction","D":"Extracorporeal shock wave therapy is contraindicated in Osgood-Schlatter disease because ultrasonic energy directed at an open growth plate in skeletally immature athletes produces physeal microhemorrhage and accelerates apophyseal avulsion","E":"Quadriceps tendon transfer to the tibial metaphysis is the surgical treatment of choice for refractory Osgood-Schlatter disease in adolescents who have completed skeletal maturation and continue to experience pain with kneeling activities"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course specifically cites prolotherapy as a regenerative injection option for refractory Osgood-Schlatter disease. Prolotherapy involves injection of an irritant solution at the tendon-bone junction to stimulate a healing response. Corticosteroid injections are generally avoided in tendon insertion sites in growing athletes due to risk of tendon weakening and physeal effects.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osgood-Schlatter refractory treatment = regenerative injection (prolotherapy). Standard treatment = activity modification, PT, analgesics.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — Patellofemoral syndrome: distinguishing anterior knee pain etiologies in growing athletes"},{"id":"sdl10_q27","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Osgood-Schlatter disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old boy presents with right anterior knee pain. He is actively growing. Examination reveals diffuse peripatellar tenderness worsened by prolonged sitting and stair climbing, without a palpable prominent or tender tibial tuberosity. Which of the following conditions is most consistent with this presentation, and how does it differ from Osgood-Schlatter disease?","choices":{"A":"Patellar tendinopathy, which differs from Osgood-Schlatter disease by involving the tendon substance at the inferior pole of the patella rather than the tibial tuberosity, and presents with pain worsening during jumping activities rather than prolonged sitting or stair climbing","B":"Prepatellar bursitis, which differs from Osgood-Schlatter disease by producing fluctuant swelling directly anterior to the patella rather than at the tibial tuberosity, and is associated with repetitive kneeling occupations rather than sport-related rapid skeletal growth","C":"Patellofemoral syndrome, which differs from Osgood-Schlatter disease by producing diffuse peripatellar and retropatellar pain with prolonged sitting and stairs and no tibial tuberosity prominence, from patellar maltracking rather than apophyseal traction","D":"Medial collateral ligament sprain, which differs from Osgood-Schlatter disease by localizing pain to the medial knee joint line rather than the anterior tibial tuberosity, and is produced by a valgus mechanism of injury rather than repetitive patellar tendon traction","E":"Iliotibial band syndrome, which differs from Osgood-Schlatter disease by producing lateral knee pain at the lateral femoral condyle rather than anterior tibial tuberosity pain, and is exacerbated by running and cycling rather than jumping and kneeling activities"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Patellofemoral syndrome causes diffuse peripatellar or retropatellar pain worsened by activities that increase patellofemoral contact stress, such as prolonged sitting, stair climbing, and squatting. Unlike Osgood-Schlatter disease, there is no tibial tuberosity prominence or tenderness, and the mechanism is patellar maltracking rather than apophyseal traction stress. Both occur in adolescent athletes but are clinically distinguishable by location and associated findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Osgood-Schlatter = tender tibial tuberosity prominence, worse with kneeling and jumping. Patellofemoral syndrome = diffuse peripatellar pain, worse with prolonged sitting and stairs, no tuberosity prominence. COMLEX: Know both anterior knee pain differentials in growing athletes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — Patellofemoral syndrome: distinguishing anterior knee pain etiologies in growing athletes"},{"id":"sdl10_q28","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Legg-Calve-Perthes disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 7-year-old male presents with 3 weeks of left hip pain and limp. Exam shows decreased internal rotation and abduction of the left hip. Initial AP and frog-leg lateral hip radiographs are reported as normal. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in the diagnostic workup?","choices":{"A":"Reassure the family that normal X-rays exclude Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease and schedule a follow-up appointment in 6 months if symptoms persist, because this condition is never present when initial AP and frog-leg lateral radiographs of the hip are both read as normal","B":"Perform hip joint aspiration to rule out transient synovitis or septic arthritis, since Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease cannot present at age 7 without radiographic changes visible on plain film when both AP and frog-leg lateral projections are obtained and interpreted as normal","C":"Prescribe NSAIDs and activity restriction for 4 weeks and repeat X-rays at that time, as Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease is a self-limited condition that produces visible radiographic changes within 4 weeks if the diagnosis is correct","D":"Order MRI or bone scan of the hips, which can detect Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease earlier than plain radiographs by identifying abnormal perfusion or bone marrow signal changes in the femoral head before bony structural changes are visible on X-ray","E":"Order a complete blood count, erythrocyte sedimentation rate, and C-reactive protein panel, since Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease presents with elevated inflammatory markers that can confirm the diagnosis biochemically when radiographs are unrevealing"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Initial X-rays can be negative in early LCP. When clinical suspicion remains high — a child aged 4-9 with insidious hip pain, limp, decreased abduction and IR, and normal labs — MRI or bone scan should be obtained. MRI identifies abnormal marrow signal (ischemia) and bone scan shows reduced perfusion in the affected femoral head, both detectable before plain radiograph changes appear.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LCP can have normal initial X-rays. Order MRI or bone scan when X-rays are normal but clinical suspicion is high. MRI = gold standard for early detection. COMLEX: Normal inflammatory markers help distinguish LCP from septic hip.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — Avascular necrosis of the hip: ischemic femoral head pathology across adult and pediatric populations"},{"id":"sdl10_q29","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Legg-Calve-Perthes disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Three children are diagnosed with Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease. A physician reviewing prognosis with the families notes that some children do well without treatment while others do poorly despite treatment. According to the course reading on long-term prognosis, which factor is the most important predictor of outcome?","choices":{"A":"Age at diagnosis, with children younger than 5 years doing well and children older than 8 to 9 years doing poorly","B":"Sex of the child, with girls doing well because they are affected less often and have fewer bilateral cases overall","C":"Duration of limp before diagnosis, with a limp under 2 weeks predicting full recovery whatever the age of the child","D":"Whether crutches are used, because relief of weight bearing prevents femoral head collapse during the revascularization phase","E":"Side involved, with left-sided disease doing better because the dominant leg receives more load during the collapse"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The pediatric orthopedics chapter assigned in this block states that prognosis can often be predicted from certain factors, with age being the most important: age at diagnosis under 5 years is good and over 8 to 9 years is poor. Other factors are hip motion (abduction maintained above 30 degrees is good, under 15 degrees is poor), extent of involvement (under 50% of the femoral head is good), and the absence of subluxation or lateral calcification. Younger children have more cartilage and greater remodeling potential. Sex is not listed as the key predictor. Duration of limp is not a listed factor. The chapter notes there is no evidence that crutches or relief of weight bearing affect femoral head collapse. Side of involvement is not a prognostic factor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LCP = idiopathic AVN of the femoral head, ages 4–9, M:F 4:1, bilateral ~10%; MRI and bone scan detect it before X-ray. COMLEX: Containment (abduction bracing or casting) and maintaining hip abduction are the goals of treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — osteochondrosis as AVN in children"},{"id":"sdl10_q30","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of Legg-Calve-Perthes disease.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 5-year-old boy with Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease has radiographic containment of the femoral head within the acetabulum and no subluxation on initial imaging. His parents ask what the treatment plan involves. Which of the following most accurately describes the principles of conservative management?","choices":{"A":"Because the child is under age 6, no treatment is required beyond parental reassurance; Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease in children under age 6 is universally self-limiting and produces no residual femoral head deformity when left completely untreated","B":"High-dose systemic corticosteroids are the standard treatment to suppress the inflammatory component of Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease, reducing synovial inflammation and preventing secondary capsular contracture that contributes to femoral head subluxation","C":"Conservative management with NSAIDs for pain, activity restriction or crutches to protect weight bearing, physical therapy to maintain hip range of motion, and bracing or casting to contain the femoral head in the acetabulum during revascularization","D":"Immediate varus derotational osteotomy is indicated for all children with Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease because even brief delays in surgical containment produce spherical femoral head deformity that worsens long-term functional and radiographic outcomes","E":"Intraarticular bisphosphonate injection is first-line treatment for Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease, slowing osteoclastic resorption of avascular trabeculae and preventing femoral head collapse during the revascularization phase in children under age 8"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Conservative management of LCP includes NSAIDs, rest and activity restriction, physical therapy to maintain hip range of motion, crutches for weight-bearing protection, and bracing or casting to maintain femoral head containment in the acetabulum during revascularization. Surgery is reserved for cases failing conservative management. The goal is to prevent femoral head deformity while the avascular bone undergoes slow revascularization and remodeling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LCP conservative treatment = NSAIDs, rest, PT, bracing or casting for containment, crutches. Surgery if conservative fails. COMLEX: Femoral head containment in acetabulum is the central therapeutic principle throughout conservative and surgical management.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — Avascular necrosis of the hip: ischemic femoral head pathology across adult and pediatric populations"},{"id":"sdl10_q31","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 12-year-old obese female presents with 4 weeks of right knee pain and a mild limp. Hip examination is not initially performed. Hip radiograph obtained later shows posterior and inferior displacement of the right femoral epiphysis. Which of the following best explains the referred knee pain and what key diagnostic lesson does this case illustrate?","choices":{"A":"The referred knee pain is caused by direct compression of the femoral nerve between the displaced femoral head and the inguinal ligament, producing dermatomal sensory loss over the anterior thigh and medial knee in the distribution of the saphenous branch","B":"The referred knee pain arises from iliotibial band tightening secondary to the altered gait pattern in SCFE, as the external rotation deformity increases iliotibial band tension at Gerdy's tubercle on the lateral tibia and refers pain to the lateral knee","C":"The referred knee pain is produced by compression of the sciatic nerve between the posteriorly displaced femoral head and the posterior acetabular wall, causing posterior thigh and posterior knee pain exacerbated by hip extension and internal rotation","D":"The referred knee pain is caused by hip capsular distension from the effusion associated with SCFE, which stimulates obturator nerve articular branches supplying both the hip capsule and the medial knee, producing medial knee pain as the primary presenting symptom in all cases","E":"SCFE classically presents with anterior thigh or knee pain referred from the hip through the femoral and obturator nerve distributions, so knee pain in an obese adolescent should always prompt hip examination and radiographs to exclude SCFE"},"correct":"E","explanation":"SCFE can present with knee pain rather than hip pain due to referred pain via the femoral and obturator nerve distributions, which supply both the hip and the anterior thigh and knee. This is a classic diagnostic trap. The key lesson is that any obese adolescent presenting with knee pain and a limp should have the hip examined and hip radiographs obtained to exclude SCFE, even when knee pain is the only complaint.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SCFE trap = knee pain without obvious hip pain. Referred pain via femoral and obturator nerves. Classic vignette: obese adolescent with knee pain, limp, and a normal knee exam — get hip X-ray. COMLEX: Radiographs mandatory for all suspected SCFE.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — Developmental dysplasia of the hip: spectrum of pediatric hip developmental conditions"},{"id":"sdl10_q32","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old male is diagnosed with an unstable SCFE. He is currently unable to bear weight due to severe hip pain. Which of the following best describes the management urgency and the key risk associated with the unstable classification?","choices":{"A":"Unstable SCFE can be managed with bed rest and traction for 24 to 48 hours before surgical scheduling, as a brief delay in operative fixation does not significantly increase the risk of avascular necrosis compared to immediate operative intervention","B":"Unstable SCFE requires urgent operative fixation because the inability to bear weight reflects ongoing physeal shear forces that sharply raise the risk of avascular necrosis compared with stable SCFE, where residual blood supply is more often preserved","C":"Unstable SCFE should be treated with emergent closed reduction under general anesthesia followed by hip spica casting for 8 weeks, as surgical instrumentation of the unstable physis universally worsens AVN risk by disrupting residual periosteal blood supply","D":"Unstable SCFE has the same operative urgency as stable SCFE and can be scheduled electively within 2 weeks; the stability classification is used only to predict long-term functional outcomes and does not influence the timing of initial surgical management","E":"Unstable SCFE is initially managed with intravenous bisphosphonate therapy for 48 hours before surgical fixation, as the antiresorptive effect stabilizes the weakened physis and reduces the degree of further slip during transfer to the operating room"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Unstable SCFE carries a dramatically higher risk of AVN than stable SCFE because the ongoing inability to bear weight indicates continued physeal shear that progressively compromises the remaining blood supply to the femoral head. Urgent operative fixation in situ is required. Closed reduction is generally avoided as it can further disrupt residual blood supply. Stable SCFE also requires operative fixation but with less urgent timing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Unstable SCFE = painful gait, unable to bear weight, high AVN risk — requires urgent in situ fixation. Stable = weight-bearing without pain, lower AVN risk — still needs fixation but less emergent. COMLEX: Do not attempt closed reduction of SCFE — increases AVN risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — Developmental dysplasia of the hip: spectrum of pediatric hip developmental conditions"},{"id":"sdl10_q33","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 9-year-old female with hypothyroidism and obesity presents with bilateral hip pain and is found to have bilateral SCFE on radiographs. She is younger than the typical SCFE patient, and both hips have slipped at presentation. Which of the following best explains why this patient developed SCFE in this atypical pattern?","choices":{"A":"Obesity alone is sufficient to cause bilateral SCFE in females, as excess body weight increases shear forces at both femoral physes simultaneously; hypothyroidism has no independent effect on physeal integrity and is a coincidental finding in this patient","B":"Hypothyroidism produces premature physeal closure through TSH-mediated effects on growth plate chondrocytes, which paradoxically weakens the physis during the preslip phase by accelerating the transition from proliferative to hypertrophic chondrocytes","C":"Bilateral SCFE in a young female indicates an underlying genetic connective tissue disorder such as Marfan syndrome, where fibrillin deficiency weakens the extracellular matrix of the physis; thyroid disorders are not associated with bilateral SCFE presentation","D":"Metabolic disorders such as hypothyroidism weaken the proximal femoral physis by altering the hormonal environment that maintains physeal integrity; combined with obesity-related shear forces, they predispose to SCFE at a younger age and in both hips","E":"Bilateral SCFE is always caused by exogenous corticosteroid therapy, which suppresses IGF-1 at the growth plate and prevents normal physeal mineralization; the presence of hypothyroidism suggests this patient was treated with corticosteroids for an autoimmune thyroiditis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bilateral SCFE at younger than typical age in a female should prompt evaluation for an underlying metabolic disorder. Hypothyroidism is one of the metabolic causes of SCFE listed in the course slides, along with obesity, inflammation, and familial predisposition. Hormonal alterations from hypothyroidism weaken the physis, while obesity increases shear stress, creating a synergistic risk for bilateral and early-onset SCFE.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SCFE causes: physiologic hormonal changes, obesity, trauma, inflammation, metabolic disorders, familial. Atypical age or bilateral SCFE = evaluate for metabolic disorder. Hypothyroidism is the classic metabolic association. COMLEX: Always screen for metabolic disorders in atypical SCFE presentations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — Developmental dysplasia of the hip: spectrum of pediatric hip developmental conditions"},{"id":"sdl10_q34","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the Salter-Harris fracture classification system and describe greenstick and torus fractures in pediatric patients.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old competitive female gymnast has months of dorsal radial-sided wrist pain that worsens with handstands and vaulting and settles with rest. Radiographs compared with the other wrist show widening of the distal radial physis with cystic metaphyseal changes. Which mechanism best explains this condition?","choices":{"A":"Repetitive axial loading causes temporary metaphyseal ischemia that blocks cartilage calcification, so the physis widens","B":"A single fall on an outstretched hand crushes the germinal layer, producing a Salter-Harris type V injury with early arrest","C":"Repetitive gripping causes traction on the extensor tendons at the radial styloid, producing an avulsion of the apophysis","D":"Axial loading buckles the dorsal metaphyseal cortex, producing a torus fracture that progresses to physeal widening","E":"Repetitive wrist flexion compresses the median nerve, which denervates the physis and leads to cystic change and widening"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Nelson growth plate injury section for this SDL describes gymnast's wrist: chronic wrist pain in up to 79% of young gymnasts, particularly girls aged 12 to 14. Repetitive axial loading and dorsiflexion through the distal radius can induce temporary metaphyseal ischemia, preventing cartilage calcification and causing the physis to widen, with microfractures possible. Radiographs compared with the other side show physeal widening with cystic metaphyseal change and, later, positive ulnar variance; MRI helps when films are inconclusive. A type V crush is a single acute compression injury that is often radiographically occult. The radial styloid is not a traction apophysis for the wrist extensors. A torus fracture is an acute buckle from one fall and does not cause chronic physeal widening. Median nerve compression does not affect the physis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Physis = pressure growth plate (longitudinal growth); apophysis = traction growth plate at tendon attachments. Physeal fractures peak at peak height velocity and are about twice as common in males. COMLEX: Gymnast's wrist: PRICE, rest from loading; SH I and II fractures are treated with closed reduction and immobilization.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — distal radius and other hand and wrist fractures"},{"id":"sdl10_q35","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the Salter-Harris fracture classification system and describe greenstick and torus fractures in pediatric patients.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Two 8-year-old children present to the ED after falls. Child 1 has an X-ray showing a cortical bulge at the distal radius metaphysis without complete cortical disruption. Child 2 has an X-ray showing complete fracture of one cortex with the opposite cortex intact and angulated but not broken. Which of the following correctly identifies both fracture types and their distinct mechanisms?","choices":{"A":"Child 1 has a greenstick fracture caused by bending force that fractures one cortex while the other bends without breaking; child 2 has a torus fracture caused by axial compressive force that buckles the cortex without producing a complete fracture on either side","B":"Both children have torus fractures, as the distal radius is the most common site for both fracture types in pediatric patients; the difference in X-ray appearance reflects only the projection angle rather than a true difference in fracture mechanism or pattern","C":"Child 1 has a Salter-Harris Type I fracture of the distal radial physis caused by tensile stress at the growth plate; child 2 has a Salter-Harris Type II fracture with a metaphyseal fragment on the compressive side of the angulated fracture","D":"Child 1 has a torus fracture from axial compression, where the porous pediatric cortex buckles without fracturing through; child 2 has a greenstick fracture from bending force, where one cortex fractures while the other bends but stays intact","E":"Child 1 has a complete distal radius fracture with periosteal hinging on the compressive side preventing complete displacement; child 2 has plastic deformity of the radius where no cortical fracture occurs but the bone curves under sustained bending force without breaking"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Torus fractures result from axial compressive forces — the porous pediatric metaphyseal cortex buckles without completely fracturing, producing a cortical bulge. Greenstick fractures result from bending forces — one cortex fractures completely on the tension side while the opposite cortex bends without fracturing on the compression side. Both are pediatric-specific fracture patterns resulting from the unique mechanical properties of immature bone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Torus = axial compression, cortical buckle without complete fracture. Greenstick = bending force, one cortex breaks completely, other bends. Both unique to pediatric bone. COMLEX: Both are stable fracture patterns managed conservatively with casting or splinting.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — Salter-Harris classification: physeal fracture grading in lower extremity fractures"},{"id":"sdl10_q36","sdlNumber":10,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the Salter-Harris fracture classification system and describe greenstick and torus fractures in pediatric patients.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 9-year-old sustains a high-energy distal femur injury. CT confirms a fracture that passes through the metaphysis, growth plate, and epiphysis as a single continuous fracture line. The orthopedic surgeon counsels the family about growth arrest risk. Which Salter-Harris type is this, and why does it carry higher risk than Types I and II?","choices":{"A":"Salter-Harris Type III; it carries higher risk than Types I and II because intraarticular extension of the fracture through the epiphysis exposes the germinal layer to joint fluid and synovial metalloproteinases that degrade growth plate cartilage and prevent normal longitudinal growth","B":"Salter-Harris Type IV; it carries higher risk than Types I and II because the fracture crosses all three bone zones at once, so precise physeal alignment is hard to restore and a bony bridge can form, causing angular deformity or leg length discrepancy","C":"Salter-Harris Type V; it carries higher risk than Types I and II because axial compressive crushing of the physis destroys the germinal cell layer and the extent of damage is frequently underestimated on initial imaging because the physis appears only narrowed rather than displaced","D":"Salter-Harris Type II; it carries higher risk than Type I because the metaphyseal fragment creates a stress riser at the physis that concentrates shear stress during weight-bearing, propagating the fracture line into the germinal layer of the growth plate during the healing period","E":"Salter-Harris Type I; it carries higher risk than expected because high-energy mechanisms produce concurrent vascular disruption to the periosteal blood supply, which is the dominant source of nutrition to the germinal layer of the physis in children under age 10"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Salter-Harris Type IV fractures traverse the metaphysis, physis, and epiphysis as a single continuous fracture line. This creates a uniquely difficult reduction challenge because the fracture must be anatomically reduced across all three bone zones simultaneously. Failure to restore perfect alignment risks the formation of a bony bridge across the growth plate, which can produce angular deformity or leg length discrepancy as the physis continues to grow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Salter-Harris Type IV = fracture through metaphysis, physis, and epiphysis; crosses all three zones; requires anatomic reduction and often internal fixation. COMLEX: Growth arrest risk: Type I least, Type V worst. Types III and IV require anatomic reduction due to intraarticular extension or cross-zone disruption.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — Salter-Harris classification: physeal fracture grading in lower extremity fractures"}],"11":[{"id":"sdl11_q01","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the structural features of synovial, fibrous, and cartilaginous joints, including their anatomical characteristics and functional classifications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which layer of the synovial joint capsule is responsible for secreting synovial fluid into the joint cavity?","choices":{"A":"Outer fibrous membrane, which blends with the periosteum of adjacent bones and provides the primary structural stability to the joint capsule","B":"Articular hyaline cartilage, which covers the opposing bone surfaces and distributes compressive forces but does not produce synovial fluid","C":"Periosteum, which forms the outermost connective tissue covering of bone and provides attachment points for tendons and ligaments near the joint","D":"Inner serous synovial membrane, which lines the capsule and secretes synovial fluid that lubricates and nourishes avascular articular cartilage","E":"Periarticular fibrous bursa, which is a separate fluid-filled sac adjacent to the joint that reduces friction between tendons and bony prominences"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Synovial joints have a two-layered capsule: the outer fibrous membrane blends with periosteum for structural support, and the inner serous synovial membrane secretes synovial fluid. The cavity is a potential space; articular cartilage covers bone surfaces but is avascular and relies on synovial fluid for nutrition. Bursae are separate from the joint cavity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the two capsule layers and that synovial membrane (not cartilage) secretes fluid. COMLEX: Synovial joint anatomy underpins OMM concepts of joint play and somatic dysfunction — fluid dynamics are affected by fascial restriction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — OA as the clinical consequence of synovial joint degeneration"},{"id":"sdl11_q02","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the structural features of synovial, fibrous, and cartilaginous joints, including their anatomical characteristics and functional classifications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A fibrous joint in which two bones are connected by an interosseous membrane and remain partially mobile is best classified as which subtype of fibrous joint?","choices":{"A":"Suture, a fibrous joint in which bones either overlap or interlock along irregular edges, allowing no meaningful movement and found exclusively between skull bones","B":"Gomphosis, a fibrous joint in which a peg-like process inserts into a bony socket via periodontal ligament fibers, as seen between tooth roots and alveolar bone","C":"Syndesmosis, a fibrous joint in which bones are united by a fibrous ligament or interosseous membrane, permitting partial mobility that depends on the length and compliance of the connecting fibers","D":"Synchondrosis, a primary cartilaginous joint in which bones are united by hyaline cartilage, typically a temporary union that permits growth and is later replaced by bone","E":"Amphiarthrosis, a functional classification describing joints with slight mobility that encompasses both fibrous and cartilaginous structural types rather than a specific structural subtype"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fibrous joints are subdivided by the nature of the connecting tissue: sutures (interlocking bone edges, skull), syndesmoses (interosseous ligament/membrane, partially mobile), and gomphosis (tooth-in-socket). The distal tibiofibular joint and the interosseous membrane of the forearm are classic syndesmosis examples. Synchondrosis is cartilaginous, not fibrous.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distinguish suture (immobile, skull), syndesmosis (partially mobile, interosseous membrane/SI joint), and gomphosis (tooth socket). COMLEX: Syndesmosis disruption in high ankle sprains creates instability relevant to OMM evaluation of the ankle and tibiofibular relationship.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — OA as the clinical consequence of synovial joint degeneration"},{"id":"sdl11_q03","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the structural features of synovial, fibrous, and cartilaginous joints, including their anatomical characteristics and functional classifications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The epiphyseal growth plate in a skeletally immature child is best classified as which type of joint?","choices":{"A":"Secondary cartilaginous joint (symphysis), in which bones are united by fibrocartilage providing permanent slight mobility such as that found at the pubic symphysis and intervertebral discs","B":"Primary cartilaginous joint (synchondrosis), in which bones are united by hyaline cartilage forming a temporary union that permits growth and is eventually ossified and replaced by bone at skeletal maturity","C":"Fibrous syndesmosis, in which bones are connected by an interosseous membrane or ligament permitting partial mobility that depends on the length of the fibrous connecting fibers","D":"Synovial hinge joint, in which articulating surfaces are separated by a fluid-filled cavity lined by a secretory synovial membrane and permitting only flexion and extension","E":"Specialized elastic fibrocartilaginous joint, in which elastin fibers embedded in a fibrocartilage matrix allow bending and recoil during repetitive loading cycles in development"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Primary cartilaginous joints (synchondroses) are united by hyaline cartilage and are typically temporary unions present during development. The epiphyseal plate is the classic example; it permits longitudinal bone growth and is replaced by bone (synostosis) at skeletal maturity. Secondary cartilaginous joints (symphyses) use fibrocartilage and are permanent, as seen at the pubic symphysis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Epiphyseal plate = synchondrosis (hyaline cartilage, temporary). Pubic symphysis = symphysis (fibrocartilage, permanent). COMLEX: Growth plate vulnerability underlies Salter-Harris fracture classification and the rationale for cautious OMM in skeletally immature patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — OA as the clinical consequence of synovial joint degeneration"},{"id":"sdl11_q04","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the composition and roles of connective tissues (ligaments, tendons, cartilage) in joint stability, movement, and their interaction with surrounding tissues.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The connective tissue found in tendons and ligaments is classified as which specific type, and what histological feature confers its high tensile strength?","choices":{"A":"Dense irregular connective tissue, characterized by collagen bundles arranged in multiple directions simultaneously to resist forces applied from various angles, as found in the deep dermis","B":"Loose areolar connective tissue, characterized by sparse collagen fibers in abundant ground substance with numerous resident and transient cell types facilitating rapid nutrient diffusion","C":"Specialized cartilaginous connective tissue, characterized by chondrocytes embedded in a matrix of collagen and proteoglycans without direct blood supply at the articular surface","D":"Dense regular connective tissue, with parallel collagen bundles aligned along the axis of stress, few fibroblasts, and minimal ground substance, giving unidirectional tensile strength","E":"Reticular connective tissue, characterized by a delicate meshwork of type III collagen fibers supporting lymphoid organs such as lymph nodes, spleen, and adrenal cortex"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ligaments and tendons are composed of dense regular connective tissue: parallel dense bundles of collagen, few fibroblasts, few blood vessels, high tensile strength, and very little ground substance. The outer surface has dense irregular CT continuous with adjacent muscle or bone. Dense irregular CT resists multidirectional forces, while dense regular CT excels along one axis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dense regular CT = tendons/ligaments (parallel collagen bundles). Dense irregular CT = dermis, joint capsule (multidirectional). COMLEX: Tendon and ligament composition explains why chronic overuse injuries heal slowly due to poor vascularity and why fascial OMM targets the surrounding dense irregular layer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — ACL/MCL/PCL ligament injuries as failures of dense regular connective tissue"},{"id":"sdl11_q05","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the composition and roles of connective tissues (ligaments, tendons, cartilage) in joint stability, movement, and their interaction with surrounding tissues.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Collagen type IV is uniquely distinguished from collagen types I, II, and III by which structural property?","choices":{"A":"Collagen type IV forms large fibrils arranged in parallel bundles providing the high tensile strength characteristic of tendons, organ capsules, and dermal connective tissue","B":"Collagen type IV forms sheets or network arrangements rather than fibrils, making it the primary structural protein of epithelial and endothelial basal laminae throughout the body","C":"Collagen type IV forms cross-linked elastin scaffolds that confer recoil properties to structures subject to repeated stretching such as arterial walls and lung parenchyma","D":"Collagen type IV forms short fibrillar linking structures that anchor extracellular matrix components to one another and maintain the overall integrity of the connective tissue scaffold","E":"Collagen type IV forms the reticular network of thin delicate fibers that support lymphoid organs such as lymph nodes, adrenal cortex, and bone marrow stroma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Collagen types I, II, and III form large fibrils (tendons, cartilage, skin). Type IV is unique in forming sheets or networks rather than fibrils, serving as the structural backbone of epithelial basal laminae. Elastic fibers contain elastin (not collagen) plus fibrillin. Reticular fibers are predominantly type III collagen in a thin meshwork.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HY collagen types: I = bone/tendon/skin, II = cartilage, III = reticular/healing/fetal skin, IV = basement membrane. Type IV defect = Alport syndrome (renal basement membrane). COMLEX: Collagen type distribution explains tissue properties relevant to OMM — type I dominates fascial planes targeted by myofascial release.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — ACL/MCL/PCL ligament injuries as failures of dense regular connective tissue"},{"id":"sdl11_q06","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the composition and roles of connective tissues (ligaments, tendons, cartilage) in joint stability, movement, and their interaction with surrounding tissues.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Fibroblasts are the predominant cell type of connective tissue. Which of the following correctly identifies their embryologic origin and primary function?","choices":{"A":"Fibroblasts originate from bone marrow precursors similar to macrophages and plasma cells, circulating in the bloodstream before homing to connective tissue sites of injury","B":"Fibroblasts derive exclusively from cranial neural crest cells in all body regions, explaining their capacity for both structural ECM synthesis and local immune modulation","C":"Fibroblasts are ectodermally derived cells that differentiate locally within each tissue during organogenesis and rarely contribute to ECM synthesis under normal physiologic conditions","D":"Fibroblasts originate from adipose precursor cells in the deep dermis that retain pluripotency and can regenerate the full range of connective tissue components after injury","E":"Fibroblasts arise from mesenchyme of mesodermal origin and produce the majority of extracellular matrix components including collagen fibers, reticular fibers, and ground substance macromolecules"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Fibroblasts arise from mesenchyme (mesodermal origin) and are the primary ECM-producing cells, secreting collagen subunits, proteoglycans, and other matrix proteins. Other CT cells include macrophages and plasma cells (bone marrow origin) and adipocytes (mesenchymal origin). Tendon and ligament fibroblast origin depends on body position: head = neural crest; limbs = lateral plate mesoderm; trunk = somites.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fibroblast = mesenchymal origin, makes ECM. Distinguish from macrophages (bone marrow), mast cells (bone marrow), and adipocytes (mesenchymal). COMLEX: Fibroblast activity in fascial connective tissue is the target of myofascial OMM — mechanical stimulation upregulates collagen synthesis and remodeling.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — ACL/MCL/PCL ligament injuries as failures of dense regular connective tissue"},{"id":"sdl11_q07","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of joint movement, including the types of movements (e.g., flexion, extension, rotation).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The atlantoaxial joint allows the head to rotate from side to side. Which category of synovial joint movement does this represent?","choices":{"A":"Angular movement, defined as motion that changes the angle between two articulating bones, including flexion, extension, abduction, and adduction at joints such as the elbow and knee","B":"Gliding movement, in which two flat articular surfaces slide past each other without significant angular change, as seen at the carpal bones of the wrist and facet joints of the spine","C":"Rotational movement, in which one bone rotates around its longitudinal axis, exemplified by the dens of C2 rotating within the ligamentous ring of C1 during head turning","D":"Special movement, such as inversion or eversion, which occurs only at anatomically specific sites such as the subtalar and transverse tarsal joints","E":"Circumduction, a composite movement that combines flexion, extension, abduction, and adduction in sequence to trace a cone-shaped arc, seen at multiaxial joints such as the hip and shoulder"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Synovial joint movements are classified as gliding (flat surfaces translate past each other), angular (change of angle, e.g., elbow flexion), rotational (one bone rotates around its longitudinal axis, e.g., atlantoaxial joint, radio-ulnar pivot), or special movements (inversion, eversion, pronation, etc.). The atlantoaxial joint is a pivot joint permitting uniaxial rotation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Atlantoaxial = pivot = rotational movement. Elbow = hinge = angular. Wrist carpals = plane = gliding. COMLEX: Atlantoaxial motion restriction is a common finding in cervical somatic dysfunction; understanding rotation vs. side-bending mechanics is essential for HVLA and muscle energy technique.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — Glenohumeral dislocation mechanics explained by multiaxial joint motion"},{"id":"sdl11_q08","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of joint movement, including the types of movements (e.g., flexion, extension, rotation).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following correctly defines flexion as a type of angular joint movement?","choices":{"A":"A straightening motion that results in an increase in the angle between two articulating bones, returning a flexed joint toward anatomic neutral position or beyond it into hyperextension","B":"A bending motion that results in a decrease in the angle between two articulating bones, moving the distal segment toward the proximal segment and reducing the inter-segmental angle","C":"Movement of an appendage away from the body midline in the frontal plane, increasing the lateral distance between the limb and the central axis of the body","D":"Rotation of a limb segment toward the body midline along a vertical axis, reducing the anterior-facing angle of the limb relative to the sagittal plane","E":"A special movement unique to the ankle in which the dorsal surface of the foot moves toward the anterior surface of the tibia, reducing the dorsal foot-to-shin angle"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Angular movements change the angle between bones. Flexion decreases this angle (bending); extension increases it (straightening). Abduction moves away from midline; adduction moves toward midline. Internal rotation moves toward midline along a vertical axis; external rotation moves away. Dorsiflexion and plantarflexion are special movements at the ankle.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic traps — extension is increasing angle (not straightening past neutral); abduction vs. adduction confusion on anatomy questions. COMLEX: Flexion and extension terminology directly maps to OMM somatic dysfunction naming conventions (e.g., FRSL vs. ERSL in spinal mechanics).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — Glenohumeral dislocation mechanics explained by multiaxial joint motion"},{"id":"sdl11_q09","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of joint movement, including the types of movements (e.g., flexion, extension, rotation).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Inversion of the foot and eversion of the foot are best categorized under which classification of synovial joint movement?","choices":{"A":"Angular movements, which are defined by changes in the inter-segmental angle between articulating bones, including flexion, extension, abduction, and adduction at synovial joints","B":"Rotational movements, which involve one bone rotating around its longitudinal axis as seen at the atlantoaxial pivot joint during head rotation from side to side","C":"Gliding movements, in which two flat articular surfaces translate past one another without meaningful angular change, as seen between carpal bones of the wrist","D":"Special movements, a category that includes inversion, eversion, protraction, retraction, elevation, depression, dorsiflexion, plantarflexion, and opposition at specific sites","E":"Circumduction movements, which trace a cone-shaped arc by combining flexion, extension, abduction, and adduction in sequence at multiaxial ball-and-socket joints"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Special movements do not fit neatly into gliding, angular, or rotational categories and are named for the specific anatomical site where they occur. Inversion (plantar surface medially) and eversion (plantar surface laterally) occur at the subtalar and transverse tarsal joints. Other special movements include dorsiflexion/plantarflexion, pronation/supination, protraction/retraction, and elevation/depression.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know all special movements and their locations; inversion/eversion at subtalar joint, pronation/supination at radio-ulnar joint, dorsiflexion/plantarflexion at talocrural joint. COMLEX: Inversion/eversion restriction at the subtalar joint is a common somatic dysfunction after ankle sprain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — Glenohumeral dislocation mechanics explained by multiaxial joint motion"},{"id":"sdl11_q10","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze how exercise, physical conditioning, and aging impact joint health.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Which of the following is a recognized direct structural consequence of normal joint aging?","choices":{"A":"Increased production of hyaluronic acid within the synovial fluid, leading to paradoxically improved joint lubrication despite reduced overall synovial fluid volume","B":"Increased joint stiffness from capsular thickening and shortening of periarticular ligaments, which together reduce range of motion and functional mobility with age","C":"Hypertrophy of periarticular muscles due to chronic loading stimulus, partially compensating for cartilage loss by improving active stabilization of the aging joint","D":"Progressive replacement of hyaline articular cartilage by fibrocartilage, which provides improved shock absorption at the cost of reduced articular surface congruity","E":"Expansion of the synovial membrane lining and increased synovial fluid volume, improving nutrient delivery to avascular cartilage as its own intrinsic nutrition declines"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Aging produces a constellation of joint changes: reduced flexibility, capsular thickening and ligament shortening causing stiffness, hyaline cartilage degeneration and thinning, reduction in synovial fluid volume and quality, and decreased periarticular muscle mass. These changes cumulatively impair joint function and predispose to OA and fall risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know aging effects on joints — cartilage thins, synovial fluid decreases, ligaments shorten, muscle mass decreases. COMLEX: Aging-related joint stiffness creates palpable somatic dysfunction patterns; OMM aims to restore joint mobility and soft tissue pliability that aging reduces.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — Knee OA as the clinical endpoint of aging and deconditioning on joints"},{"id":"sdl11_q11","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze how exercise, physical conditioning, and aging impact joint health.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Regular physical exercise improves joint health primarily through which of the following mechanisms?","choices":{"A":"Stimulating chondrocyte proliferation to directly regenerate lost articular cartilage, histologically reversing the degenerative changes of osteoarthritis at the joint surface","B":"Inducing hypertrophy of the synovial membrane and increasing its surface area, thereby raising the total protein and hyaluronic acid content of synovial fluid","C":"Improving joint lubrication through synovial fluid production and distribution, reducing soft tissue stiffness, and strengthening periarticular stabilizing muscles","D":"Promoting calcification of periarticular tendons and ligaments, increasing passive joint stability and reducing the risk of traumatic ligamentous injury","E":"Decreasing bone density through controlled loading cycles, redistributing mechanical stress away from the articular surface and toward the cortical diaphysis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Exercise benefits for joint health include improved bone density, improved balance, reduction in pain, improved soft tissue pliability and reduced stiffness, improved joint lubrication, and strengthening of supportive muscles. Exercise does not directly regenerate cartilage or cause beneficial calcification of soft tissues. Synovial membrane hypertrophy can be pathological.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Exercise improves lubrication, reduces stiffness, strengthens periarticular muscles. Does NOT reverse cartilage loss histologically. COMLEX: Exercise prescription is central to osteopathic management of musculoskeletal conditions; combining OMM with graded exercise maximizes functional restoration.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — Knee OA as the clinical endpoint of aging and deconditioning on joints"},{"id":"sdl11_q12","sdlNumber":11,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze how exercise, physical conditioning, and aging impact joint health.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Which age-related change to articular cartilage most directly explains the onset of joint pain and loss of function in elderly patients with osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Degeneration and progressive thinning of hyaline articular cartilage, which reduces shock absorption and exposes subchondral bone to increased mechanical stress and erosion","B":"Overgrowth of the synovial membrane filling the joint cavity, mechanically obstructing joint movement and generating a reactive inflammatory response that destroys articular cartilage","C":"Increased collagen cross-linking within the cartilage matrix, paradoxically stiffening it to the point where it can no longer distribute compressive forces across the articular surface","D":"Proliferation of chondrocytes at the articular margin, forming osteophytes that impinge on adjacent soft tissues and restrict range of motion without affecting articular cartilage itself","E":"Accumulation of calcium pyrophosphate crystals within the cartilage matrix, triggering recurrent pseudogout attacks that progressively destroy cartilage through crystal-induced inflammation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aging causes progressive thinning and degeneration of hyaline articular cartilage, reducing its shock absorption and load distribution capacity. Once the cartilage is lost, subchondral bone is exposed, causing pain and reactive bone changes (sclerosis, osteophyte formation). These are consequences of cartilage loss rather than primary causes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA = cartilage degeneration and thinning → subchondral bone exposure → pain and loss of function. Pseudogout involves CPPD crystals but is episodic, not the primary mechanism of OA. COMLEX: OMM reduces the joint loading that accelerates cartilage degradation; muscle strengthening offloads the articular surface.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — Knee OA as the clinical endpoint of aging and deconditioning on joints"},{"id":"sdl11_q13","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the structural features of synovial, fibrous, and cartilaginous joints, including their anatomical characteristics and functional classifications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old man presents with bilateral knee pain worsening over 6 months, aggravated by stairs and prolonged standing. BMI is 31. Vitals are normal. Exam reveals bilateral crepitus, mild bony enlargement, and no warmth or effusion. X-ray shows symmetric joint space narrowing and subchondral sclerosis. Which structural component of the synovial joint is most directly compromised in this patient, explaining his loss of smooth pain-free movement?","choices":{"A":"The outer fibrous membrane of the joint capsule, which blends with periosteum and maintains structural alignment of articulating bones but does not directly contribute to the smoothness of articular movement","B":"The inner serous synovial membrane lining the joint capsule, which produces synovial fluid for lubrication and nutrition but whose primary dysfunction causes effusion rather than crepitus or joint space narrowing","C":"The periarticular fibrous bursae adjacent to the joint, which reduce friction between tendons and bony prominences outside the joint cavity rather than at the articular surfaces within it","D":"The fibrous outer layer of the joint capsule at its attachment to the periosteum, which stiffens with capsular fibrosis but does not explain the symmetric joint space narrowing seen on radiograph","E":"Articular hyaline cartilage covering the bone surfaces within the synovial joint cavity, which normally distributes compressive forces evenly across the joint and provides a nearly frictionless surface for movement"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Joint space narrowing on X-ray reflects cartilage loss. Articular hyaline cartilage distributes compressive forces, absorbs mechanical pressure, and provides a smooth surface (with synovial fluid) for low-friction movement. Its degeneration produces crepitus and pain. Synovial membrane dysfunction causes effusion, not narrowing. Bursae are extracapsular. Capsular fibrosis causes stiffness.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OA pathology = cartilage loss → subchondral sclerosis + osteophytes + joint space narrowing on X-ray. Know each component of the synovial joint and what its specific failure causes clinically. COMLEX: OMM for knee OA targets the periarticular soft tissue and capsular restriction, indirectly protecting remaining cartilage.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, and management"},{"id":"sdl11_q14","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the structural features of synovial, fibrous, and cartilaginous joints, including their anatomical characteristics and functional classifications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A medical student is reviewing a histology slide from a 50-year-old cadaver specimen. The joint shown connects two skull bones via interlocking bony projections with no intervening cartilage, fibrous tissue, or joint space. Movement across this structure is zero. The student correctly classifies this as which type of joint?","choices":{"A":"Synchondrosis, a primary cartilaginous joint in which bones are united by hyaline cartilage that permits slight bending early in life, typically a temporary union replaced by bone at skeletal maturity","B":"Syndesmosis, a fibrous joint in which bones are united by a fibrous ligament or interosseous membrane, permitting partial mobility that depends on the length and tension of the connecting fibers","C":"Symphysis, a secondary cartilaginous joint united by thick fibrocartilage providing strength and slight permanent mobility, as seen at the pubic symphysis and intervertebral disc junctions","D":"Synostosis, a synarthrosis in which bone is fused directly to bone with no intervening material, permitting no movement and representing the normal adult fate of previously separate skull bones","E":"Suture, a fibrous joint in which cranial bone edges overlap or interlock with fibrous tissue interposed between them, found in young skulls before complete bony fusion has occurred"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In adult skulls, sutures progressively ossify into synostoses where bone is directly fused to bone with zero movement. Sutures in children still have fibrous tissue interposed. Synchondroses use hyaline cartilage. Syndesmoses have ligamentous tissue. Symphyses use fibrocartilage. The absence of any intervening tissue and complete immobility specifies synostosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distinguish suture (fibrous, skull, children) from synostosis (fused bone, adults, skull after ossification). Know all three structural categories and their subtypes. COMLEX: Cranial OMM techniques are predicated on the concept of slight motion at cranial sutures — understanding that sutures exist as fibrous joints before synostosis informs this OMM approach.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, and management"},{"id":"sdl11_q15","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the structural features of synovial, fibrous, and cartilaginous joints, including their anatomical characteristics and functional classifications.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old soccer player presents after landing awkwardly on an outstretched arm. MRI confirms anterior glenohumeral dislocation. The orthopedic consultant explains that the shoulder's extreme mobility — the greatest of any joint in the body — is a direct consequence of its synovial joint subtype. Which subtype correctly describes the glenohumeral joint and accounts for its multiplanar motion capability?","choices":{"A":"Ball-and-socket joint, a multiaxial design in which a rounded head fits into a concavity, permitting flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, rotation, and circumduction","B":"Condyloid joint, a biaxial design in which an oval condyle articulates with an elliptical fossa permitting flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, and circumduction but restricting pure axial rotation","C":"Saddle joint, a biaxial design in which reciprocal saddle-shaped articular surfaces allow movement in two orthogonal planes, best exemplified by the first carpometacarpal joint of the thumb","D":"Hinge joint, a uniaxial design in which a spool-shaped surface articulates with a concave surface permitting only flexion and extension along a single fixed transverse axis","E":"Pivot joint, a uniaxial design in which a rounded bony process fits within a ligamentous ring permitting only rotation around the longitudinal axis of the bone involved"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ball-and-socket joints are multiaxial, allowing movement in all three planes. The glenohumeral joint trades stability for mobility due to the shallow glenoid fossa. Condyloid joints allow two axes (no pure rotation); saddle joints allow two axes at the CMC thumb joint; hinge joints (elbow) allow only one axis (flex/ext); pivot joints (atlantoaxial, proximal radio-ulnar) allow only rotation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the 6 synovial subtypes: plane, hinge, pivot, condyloid, saddle, ball-and-socket — and their uniaxial/biaxial/multiaxial classification. Hip and shoulder = ball-and-socket. COMLEX: Glenohumeral instability predisposes to dislocation; OMM for shoulder conditions addresses capsular restriction and rotator cuff muscle imbalance affecting all planes of motion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, and management"},{"id":"sdl11_q16","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the composition and roles of connective tissues (ligaments, tendons, cartilage) in joint stability, movement, and their interaction with surrounding tissues.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A developmental biology lecture traces where tendons and ligaments come from. The lecturer notes that the embryologic source differs along the rostrocaudal axis of the body. From which embryologic source do the tendons and ligaments of the limbs arise?","choices":{"A":"Cranial neural crest cells, which migrate into the limb buds and form tendons and ligaments throughout the upper and lower limbs","B":"The somites, whose sclerotome cells migrate into the limb buds with the myotome and form tendons alongside the limb muscles","C":"The lateral plate mesoderm, which also forms the limb skeleton, while somites form trunk tendons and neural crest forms head tendons","D":"The surface ectoderm of the apical ectodermal ridge, which folds inward to lay down the collagen of limb tendons and ligaments","E":"The intermediate mesoderm, which extends into the limb buds next to the developing kidneys and gives rise to the dense regular tissue"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course slide on tendon and ligament origin states that the source depends on position along the rostrocaudal axis: in the head they arise from cranial neural crest cells; in the neck and shoulder girdle from lateral plate mesoderm, cranial neural crest, and paraxial mesoderm; in the limbs from lateral plate mesoderm; and in the trunk from the somites. Cranial neural crest supplies head tendons, not limb tendons. Somites form trunk tendons, and the somite cells that enter the limb become muscle (myotome), not limb tendons. The AER is a signaling ectoderm that drives limb outgrowth and does not form connective tissue. Intermediate mesoderm forms urogenital structures and does not contribute to the musculoskeletal system.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Connective tissue derives from mesenchyme (mesoderm); limb skeleton and limb tendons/ligaments come from lateral plate mesoderm, while limb muscle comes from migrating somite (myotome) cells. COMLEX: Tendons join muscle to periosteum; ligaments join the periosteum of adjacent bones.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — mesodermal origins of musculoskeletal tissues"},{"id":"sdl11_q17","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the composition and roles of connective tissues (ligaments, tendons, cartilage) in joint stability, movement, and their interaction with surrounding tissues.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A first-year medical student is studying for a histology practical. The review sheet describes a protein that constitutes approximately 30% of human dry weight, can form fibers, sheets, and networks, and is highly resistant to tearing forces. Type I forms large fibrils found in tendons and dermis. Type II forms fibrils in cartilage. Type IV forms sheets in basal laminae. What protein is being described?","choices":{"A":"Elastin, a structural protein embedded within fibrillin microfibrils forming cross-linked elastic networks that confer recoil properties to arterial walls, skin, and lung parenchyma","B":"Fibrillin, a glycoprotein that forms the microfibrillar scaffold into which elastin is deposited, creating elastic fibers throughout connective tissue in skin, vasculature, and ligaments","C":"Proteoglycan, a ground substance macromolecule composed of a protein core with attached glycosaminoglycan chains that retains water within the extracellular matrix of cartilage and connective tissue","D":"Laminin, a glycoprotein found exclusively in basement membranes that anchors epithelial cells to type IV collagen networks via integrin receptor binding at the cell surface","E":"Collagen, a family of structural proteins produced by fibroblasts that polymerize into elongated structures and represent the most abundant protein in the human body by dry weight"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Collagen is the most abundant protein in the human body (30% of dry weight). Types I/II/III form large fibrils; type IV forms sheets for basal laminae. Elastin (with fibrillin) forms elastic fibers with recoil properties. Proteoglycans are ground substance components. Laminins are basement membrane glycoproteins that anchor epithelium to collagen via integrins.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Collagen = 30% dry weight, most abundant protein. Key pathology: type I defect in OI, type V (classic) or type III (vascular subtype) in Ehlers-Danlos, type IV in Alport syndrome. COMLEX: The elastic and collagen fiber composition of fascia determines its mechanical properties and explains why sustained OMM pressure induces tissue creep and eventual release.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — ACL/PCL/MCL/LCL injuries and the unhappy triad"},{"id":"sdl11_q18","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the composition and roles of connective tissues (ligaments, tendons, cartilage) in joint stability, movement, and their interaction with surrounding tissues.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman with progressive knee pain is told her articular cartilage is thinning. Her orthopedist explains that hyaline cartilage covering the articular surface uniquely lacks a perichondrium at that location. Which of the following best explains the functional significance of this anatomical feature for normal joint physiology?","choices":{"A":"Absence of perichondrium allows water and nutrients to exchange between the cartilage matrix and synovial fluid during loading cycles, nourishing avascular articular cartilage","B":"Absence of perichondrium reduces friction at the articular surface by preventing a fibrous layer from being interposed between opposing cartilage surfaces during joint loading","C":"Absence of perichondrium allows chondrocytes direct vascular access from synovial membrane capillaries, supporting aerobic metabolism within the cartilage during loading","D":"Absence of perichondrium permits collagen fibers to orient themselves parallel to the articular surface throughout the full depth of the cartilage matrix to resist shear forces","E":"Absence of perichondrium exposes the cartilage surface to direct innervation from capsular nerve branches, explaining the severe pain experienced when cartilage itself is damaged"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Articular hyaline cartilage lacks perichondrium at the exposed surface, allowing pressure-dependent water exchange with synovial fluid — the mechanism by which avascular chondrocytes receive nutrients. Loading compresses the matrix and expels water; decompression draws in synovial fluid. Cartilage itself lacks nerves and blood vessels; pain in OA comes from subchondral bone and capsular innervation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Articular cartilage is avascular, alymphatic, and aneural — nutrition comes from synovial fluid via pressure-dependent exchange. This explains why immobilization harms cartilage and loading protocols help. COMLEX: Joint mobilization in OMM promotes synovial fluid circulation, providing trophic support to avascular articular cartilage.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — ACL/PCL/MCL/LCL injuries and the unhappy triad"},{"id":"sdl11_q19","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of joint movement, including the types of movements (e.g., flexion, extension, rotation).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physical therapist documents shoulder range of motion. When the patient raises their arm forward in the sagittal plane from anatomic position and then returns it to anatomic position, which pair of terms correctly labels these two movements?","choices":{"A":"The forward arm raise is shoulder abduction (moving away from midline in the frontal plane) and returning to neutral is shoulder adduction (moving toward the body midline)","B":"The forward arm raise is shoulder internal rotation (moving the limb toward midline along a vertical axis) and returning to neutral is shoulder external rotation (moving away from midline)","C":"The forward arm raise is shoulder flexion (movement forward in the sagittal plane) and returning to neutral is shoulder extension (movement back toward anatomic position)","D":"The forward arm raise is shoulder circumduction and returning to neutral is shoulder elevation, with both being composite or special movements at the glenohumeral joint","E":"The forward arm raise is shoulder protraction (moving the shoulder anteriorly) and returning to neutral is shoulder retraction (moving the shoulder posteriorly) at the scapulothoracic joint"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Angular movements are defined by inter-segmental angle changes. Flexion decreases the angle (arm forward); extension increases it (arm back to neutral or beyond). Abduction/adduction occur in the frontal plane. Internal/external rotation occur around a vertical axis. Circumduction is a composite movement tracing a cone. Protraction/retraction are special movements of the scapula, not the glenohumeral joint.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Flexion = decreasing angle. Extension = increasing angle. These definitions must be applied consistently across all joints. COMLEX: Correct motion terminology is mandatory for documenting SOAP notes in OMM — imprecise terminology (e.g., calling flexion \"elevation\") causes diagnostic errors in somatic dysfunction assessment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — Glenohumeral joint dislocations: presentation and management"},{"id":"sdl11_q20","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of joint movement, including the types of movements (e.g., flexion, extension, rotation).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old basketball player is evaluated after sustaining a lateral ankle sprain. Physical examination reveals that the plantar surface of his foot is turned to face medially and he is unable to actively return it to neutral. Which term correctly names this foot position, and to which category of joint movement does it belong?","choices":{"A":"Inversion, a special movement in which the plantar surface of the foot faces medially, occurring at the subtalar and transverse tarsal joints rather than as angular or rotational motion","B":"Adduction, an angular movement in which the foot moves toward the body midline in the frontal plane, reducing the lateral distance between the foot and the central axis of the lower limb","C":"Internal rotation, a movement in which the entire lower extremity rotates medially along a vertical axis at the hip joint, rotating the foot passively but not representing a foot-specific movement","D":"Plantarflexion, a special movement in which the dorsal surface of the foot moves away from the anterior tibia, pointing the toes downward as occurs when standing on the toes","E":"Pronation, a forearm-specific special movement rotating the palm posteriorly that when incorrectly applied to the foot refers to a combined eversion, dorsiflexion, and abduction pattern"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Inversion (plantar surface faces medially) and eversion (plantar surface faces laterally) are special movements at the subtalar/transverse tarsal joints. They do not fit angular or rotational categories. Lateral ankle sprains most commonly injure the anterior talofibular ligament through inversion mechanism. Plantarflexion and dorsiflexion are distinct special movements at the talocrural joint.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Inversion = subtalar joint, plantar surface medial, lateral ankle sprain mechanism. Eversion = opposite. Know which ligaments are stressed in inversion (ATFL, CFL) vs. eversion (deltoid) sprains. COMLEX: The subtalar joint is a common site of somatic dysfunction after ankle sprains; OMM addresses the restricted motion at the talocalcaneal joint to restore inversion-eversion balance.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — Glenohumeral joint dislocations: presentation and management"},{"id":"sdl11_q21","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of joint movement, including the types of movements (e.g., flexion, extension, rotation).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A physical medicine resident is comparing range of motion between the elbow and hip joint during a clinical encounter. The elbow allows only flexion and extension along a single fixed axis, while the hip allows movement in all three planes of space. Which pair of synovial joint subtypes correctly corresponds to the elbow and hip, respectively?","choices":{"A":"The elbow is a pivot joint, a uniaxial design permitting only rotation around the longitudinal axis, while the hip is a condyloid joint, a biaxial design permitting flexion, extension, and abduction but restricting pure axial rotation","B":"The elbow is a hinge joint, a uniaxial design permitting only flexion and extension, while the hip is a ball-and-socket joint, a multiaxial design permitting movement in all three planes including flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, and rotation","C":"The elbow is a condyloid joint, a biaxial design permitting flexion, extension, and circumduction, while the hip is a saddle joint, a biaxial design permitting movement in two orthogonal planes only","D":"The elbow is a plane joint, a uniaxial gliding design where flat surfaces translate past each other without angular change, while the hip is a ball-and-socket joint, a multiaxial design permitting three-plane movement","E":"The elbow is a saddle joint, a biaxial design with reciprocal concave and convex articular surfaces, while the hip is a pivot joint, a uniaxial design allowing only rotation around its longitudinal axis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hinge joints (elbow, knee) are uniaxial, permitting only flexion/extension. Ball-and-socket joints (hip, shoulder) are multiaxial, permitting movement in all three planes. Pivot joints (atlantoaxial, proximal radio-ulnar) allow only rotation. Condyloid joints (MCP, wrist) allow two axes. Plane joints (carpals, facets) allow gliding. Saddle joints (CMC thumb) allow two axes with reciprocal surfaces.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Memorize the 6 synovial joint types and their representative locations. Elbow = hinge (humeroulnar); the proximal radio-ulnar is the pivot within the elbow that allows forearm rotation. COMLEX: Classifying joints by their axes of motion underpins OMM diagnosis — restriction of a normally multiaxial joint in a specific plane localizes the dysfunction being treated.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — Glenohumeral joint dislocations: presentation and management"},{"id":"sdl11_q22","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze how exercise, physical conditioning, and aging impact joint health.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with mild knee osteoarthritis and a recent fall asks whether a regular exercise program will do anything beyond easing her knee pain. Which additional benefit of exercise on the musculoskeletal system is listed in the course?","choices":{"A":"Improved bone density and improved balance, which together lower her risk of falls and fractures","B":"Regrowth of the thinned articular cartilage to its original thickness within a few months of training","C":"Reversal of osteophytes already present at the joint margins once body weight has been reduced","D":"Conversion of the fibrocartilage in her menisci into hyaline cartilage that absorbs load better","E":"Lengthening of the joint capsule beyond its youthful size, so that range of motion exceeds normal"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course lists the role of exercise in joint health as improved bone density, improved balance, reduced pain, more pliable joint soft tissue with less stiffness, better joint lubrication, and stronger supportive muscles. For an older woman who has fallen, better balance and bone density lower the chance of a fall and of a fracture if she does fall. Articular cartilage has no capacity to heal or regenerate, so exercise does not regrow it. Osteophytes are not reversed by exercise or weight loss. Meniscal fibrocartilage does not convert to hyaline cartilage. Exercise reduces stiffness but does not lengthen the capsule beyond normal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Aging joints: stiffer capsule, shorter ligaments, thinner hyaline cartilage, less synovial fluid, less muscle mass. Exercise counters these and also improves bone density and balance. COMLEX: Weight-bearing exercise is part of osteoporosis and fall prevention in older adults.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — mechanical loading and bone remodeling"},{"id":"sdl11_q23","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze how exercise, physical conditioning, and aging impact joint health.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 77-year-old man presents with bilateral hip stiffness and aching worse in the morning and after prolonged sitting. He denies trauma. Radiographs show symmetric joint space narrowing, marginal osteophytes, and subchondral sclerosis. Physical examination reveals markedly reduced internal rotation bilaterally. Which combination of age-related joint changes most directly explains both the radiographic findings and the restricted internal rotation on examination?","choices":{"A":"Increased synovial fluid volume with elevated protein content causing joint distension, which mechanically restricts capsular compliance and produces both the radiographic narrowing and the motion restriction","B":"Progressive replacement of dense regular connective tissue in the hip ligaments by loose areolar tissue, paradoxically increasing joint laxity and producing instability rather than the restricted motion seen here","C":"Hypertrophy of the periarticular muscles from compensatory overloading, generating passive stiffness that mimics capsular restriction but that would resolve completely with targeted strengthening exercises","D":"Focal accumulation of calcium pyrophosphate crystals within the articular cartilage causing pseudogout attacks that progressively destroy cartilage and produce episodic rather than chronic symmetric restriction","E":"Degeneration and progressive thinning of hyaline articular cartilage causing joint space narrowing, combined with capsular thickening and ligament shortening that restrict motion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The combination of joint space narrowing on X-ray and restricted internal rotation on exam reflects the two dominant mechanisms of joint aging: cartilage degeneration (structural, causes narrowing and crepitus) and capsular thickening with ligament shortening (functional, causes stiffness and range of motion restriction). Synovial hypertrophy causes effusion, not narrowing. Pseudogout is episodic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Two mechanisms = structural (cartilage loss) + functional (capsular/ligament stiffness). Know how to distinguish OA from inflammatory arthritis and crystal arthropathies clinically. COMLEX: Restricted internal rotation is a hallmark of hip somatic dysfunction and OA; OMM targeting the posterior hip capsule can restore some motion in early-to-moderate disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 8 — Knee osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, and management"},{"id":"sdl11_q24","sdlNumber":11,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze how exercise, physical conditioning, and aging impact joint health.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A physical therapist is counseling a healthy 48-year-old recreational athlete about maintaining joint health as she ages. The therapist explains that aging and regular exercise have opposing effects on the same joint tissues. Which of the following best contrasts the effects of aging versus exercise on the joint and its surrounding structures?","choices":{"A":"Aging uniformly increases bone density through age-related osteoblast stimulation, while excessive aerobic exercise paradoxically reduces density through mechanical unloading of the axial skeleton and limbs","B":"Aging promotes chondrocyte proliferation as a compensatory response to cartilage wear, while high-impact exercise suppresses this regenerative capacity and accelerates irreversible articular degeneration","C":"Aging selectively shortens all periarticular ligaments through gradual scarring and contracture, while targeted stretching exercise selectively elongates ligaments beyond their normal anatomic resting length","D":"Aging reduces synovial fluid, thickens the capsule, shortens ligaments, and reduces muscle mass, whereas exercise improves lubrication, keeps soft tissue pliable, and strengthens stabilizing muscles","E":"Aging converts all diarthrodial synovial joints into synarthroses through progressive ossification, while regular aerobic exercise completely prevents this conversion by maintaining synovial fluid flow"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Aging effects on joints: reduced flexibility, capsular thickening and ligament shortening (stiffness), hyaline cartilage thinning, reduced synovial fluid volume and quality, decreased muscle mass. Exercise counteracts these: improves lubrication, soft tissue pliability, joint lubrication, bone density, balance, pain reduction, and muscle strengthening. Aging does not ossify all diarthroses or universally increase bone density.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the complete list of aging joint changes and how exercise addresses each one. COMLEX: This bidirectional contrast underpins the osteopathic philosophy of maintaining health through activity — OMM facilitates motion restoration while exercise prescription prevents recurrence of dysfunction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 8 — Knee osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, and management"}]};
